\id JHN Original Douay-Rheims (1582/1610)
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\h John
\toc1 The Holy Gospel of Jesus Christ according to John.
\toc2 John
\toc3 JHN
\mt1 The Holy Gospel of Jesus Christ according to John.
\is THE ARGUMENT OF S. JOHN'S GOSPEL.
\ip S. John's Gospel may be divided into four parts.
\ip The first part is, of the acts of Christ before his solemn manifestation of himself, while John Baptist was yet baptizing: Chap. 1. 2. 3. 4.
\ip The second, of his Acts in Jury (having now begun his solemn manifestation in Galilee, Nat, 4, 12 (the second Easter of Pasch of his preaching: Chap. 5. For of the first Pasch, we had in the first part, chap. 2, 13: \it And the Pasch of the Jews was at hand.\it* And that feast whereof we have in this \f - \ft * This speech very common in this Gospel, as appeareth by the places here marked, declareth that he writeth to the Gentiles.\f* second part, chap. 5, 1: \it After this there was a festival day of the Jews\it*, is thought of good \f - \ft \f* Authors, to be the feast of Pasch.
\ip Iren. li. 2. c. 39.
\ip The third part is, of this Acts in Galilee, and in Jury, about the third Pasch, and after it: cap. 6, to the 12. For so we have chap. 6, 4: \it and Pasch the festival day of the Jews was at hand.\it*
\ip The fourth part is of the fourth Pasch (which we have in the end of the chap. 11, 55: \it And the Pasch of the Jews was at hand\it* ) that is to say, of the Holy Week of his Passion in Hierusalem: chap. 12.
\ip unto the end of the book.
\ip By which division it is manifest, that the intent of this Evangelist writing after the other three, was, to omit the Acts of Christ in Galilee, because the other three had written them at large: and to report his Acts done in Jury, which they had omitted.
\ip And this he doth, because Iury with Hierusalem and the Temple, being the principal part of the Country, there abode the principal of the Jews, both for authority, and also for learning in the law or knowledge of the Scriptures, and therefore that was the place, where our Lord JESUS finding in the Head itself and in the leaders of the rest, such willful obstinacy and desperate resistance, as the Prophets had foretold, did by this occasion, much more plainly than in Galilee, both say and prove, at sundry times, even every year of his preaching, himself to be the CHRIST that had been so long promised unto them, & expected of them: & the same CHRIST to be not only a man, as they imagined, but also the natural, consubstantial, & coeternal Son of God the Father, who now had sent him. Therefore these were the words and deeds that served best the purpose of this Evangelist, being, to shew the glory & excellency of this person JESUS: that thereby the Gentiles might see, how worthily Hierusalem & the Jews were reprobated, who had refused yea & crucified such an one: and how well & to their own salvation themselves might do, to receive him and to believe in him. For this to have been his purpose, himself declareth in the end, saying: \it These are\it* \f - \ft \+it Io. 20, 32.\+it*\f* \it written, that you may believe that JESUS is CHRIST the Son of God: and that believing, you may have life in his name.\it*
\ip And hereupon it is, that S. Hierome writeth thus in his life: \it John the Apostle, whom Jesus loved\it* \f - \ft Hier. in Catal. a Io. 21, 20. b Mat. 4. 21. c Act. 12, 2.\f* \it very much, the son of Zebedee, the brother of James the Apostle whom Herod after our Lord's Passion beheaded, last of all wrote the Gospel, at the request of the Bishops of Asia, against Cerinthus, and other Heretics, and specially against the assertion of the Ebionites then rising, who say that Christ was not before MARY. Whereupon also he was compelled to utter his Divine Nativity.\it* Of his three Epistles, and of his Apocalypse, shall be said in their own places.
\ip It followeth in S. Hierome, that \it In the Second persecution under Domitian, for fourteen years after the persecution of Nero he was exiled into the isle Patmos. But after that Domitian was slain, and his acts for his passing cruelty repealed by the Senate: under Nerva the Emperor he returned to Ephesus, and there continuing unto the time of Trajane the Emperor, he founded and governed all the Churches of Asia: and worn with old age, he died the threescore and eight year after the Passion of our Lord, and was buried besides the same city.\it*
\ip \it Whose excellency the same holy Doctor thus briefly describeth. li. 1. Advers. Iovinianum.\it*
\ip IOHN the Apostle, one of our Lord's Disciples, who was the youngest among the Apostles, and whom the faith of Christ found a virgin, remained a virgin, and therefore is more loved of our \f - \ft a Io. 13, 23. 24. &c. 21, 20.\f* Lord, and lieth upon the breast of JESUS: and that which Peter durst not ask, he desireth him to ask: and after the resurrection, when Mary Magdalen had reported that our Lord was risen again, both of them ran to the Sepulchre, but he came thither first: and when they were in the ship \f - \ft b Io. 20, 4.\f* and fished in the lake of Genesareth, JESUS stood on the shore, neither did the Apostles know whom they saw: only the virgin knoweth the virgin, and saith to Peter, \it It is our Lord:\it* This John \f - \ft c Io. 21, 7.\f* was both an Apostle, and Evangelist, and Prophet: and Apostle, because he wrote to the Churches as a Master: an Evangelist because he compiled a book of the Gospel, which (except Matthew) none other of the twelve Apostles did: a Prophet, for he saw in the isle Patmos, where he was banished by Domitian the Emperor for the testimony of our Lord, the Apocalypse, containing infinite mysteries of things to come. Tertullian also reporteth, that at Rome being cast into a barrel of hot boiling oil, he came forth more pure and fresher or livelier, than he went in. Yea and his Gospel itself much differeth from the rest. Matthew beginneth to write as of a man: Mark of the prophecy of Malachie and Esay: Luke of the Priesthood of Zachary: the first hath the face of a man, because of the genealogy: the second the face of a lion, for the voice of one crying in the desert: the third the face of a calf, because of the Priesthood. But John as an Eagle flieth to the things on high, & mounteth to the Father himself, saying: \it In the beginning was the WORD, and the WORD was with God, and God was the WORD.\it* Thus far S. Hierome.
\ip \it Upon this Gospel there are the famous commentaries of S. Augustine called\it* Tractatus in Evang. Ioan. to. 9. \it and twelve books of S. Cyril's commentaries.\it*
\c 1
\cd The preface of the Evangelist, commending Christ (as being God the Son incarnate) to the Gentiles, and setting out the blindness of the Jews in not receiving him. 19. Then, the testimonies of John Baptist, first to the solemn \it legacy of the Jews: 29. secondly, when he saw \+sc Jesus\+sc* come to him: 35. thirdly, to his own Disciples also, putting them over from himself to \+sc Jesus\+sc*. Who made it plainer to them that he is Christ, 40. and so began he also to have Disciples.\it* \f - \ft The 1. part: THE ACTS of Christ before his manifestation, whilst John Baptist was yet baptizing.\f*
\p
\v 1 In \f 1 \fr 1.1 \ft The Gospel at the third Mass upon Christmas day. And every day at the end of Mass.\f* the beginning was the \sc Word\sc*, and the \sc Word\sc* was with God, and God was the \sc Word\sc*. \ef - \fr 1.1 \fq Was the word. \ft The second Person in Trinity which is the natural, only, and eternal Son of God the Father, is called the \+sc Word\+sc*: ¹ not as the holy Scriptures or speeches of the Prophets and Apostles (written and spoken by God's commandment for the uttering of his divine will towards man) be called his word, but in a more divine, eminent, and ineffable sort, to express unto us in a sort, by a term agreeable to our capacity, that the Son of God so is, and so from everlasting is born of God the Father, as our prime concept (which is our internal and mental word) is and issueth out of our intelligence & mind. This \+sc Word\+sc* then, Son, or second Person in the holy Trinity, was and had his being then already, when other creatures (of what sort so ever) had but their beginning, and therefore cannot be a creature, as many Heretics before the writing of this Gospel thought, and as the Arrians after taught. And this first sentence of the Gospel not only the faithful, ² but the Platonics did so admire (as S. Augustine writeth) ³ that they wished it to be written in gold. \fq ¹ \ft How God the Son is called the WORD. \fq ² \ft The Platonics. \fq ³ \ft \+it August. de Civ. Dei li. 10. c. 29.\+it*\ef* \ef - \fr 1.1 \fq With God. \ft Because a man might say, If the \+sc Word\+sc* were before any thing was created, where or how could he be? The Evangelist preventing that carnal concept, saith first, that the was with God, ¹ whose bing dependeth not upon time, place, space, or any other creatures, all which were made by him. Secondly, he giveth us to understand, that the \+sc Word\+sc* had his proper substance or personality distinct from God the Father, whereby Sabellius the old Heretic is refuted. Thirdly, here is insinuated the order of these two persons, one towards the other, to wit, that the Son is with and of the Father, and not the Father of the Son. Fourthly, you may confute here the blasphemy of Calvin, ² holding the second Person to be God, not as of God the Father, but as of himself. And yet such are the books that our youth now read commonly in England, and that by commandment. \fq ¹ \ft The WORD coeternal with the Father, distinct in person, and of the Father. \fq ² \ft \+it Calv. inst. li. 1. c. 13. sect. 23 & 25.\+it*\ef* \ef - \fr 1.1 \fq God was the word. \ft Lest any man upon the premises, which set forth the relation and distinction of the second Person from the first, might think that the Father only were God, the Evangelist expressly teacheth, the \+sc Word\+sc* to be God. ¹ For though the words seem to lie otherwise (because we have of purpose so owed the elegancy which the Evangelist himself observed in placing them so, and therefore they stand so both in Greek and Latin) yet indeed the construction is thus, \+it The \+sc Word\+sc* was God,\+it* ² and (as in his first Epistle the same Apostle writeth) \+it true God:\+it* lest any might say (as the Arians did) that he was God indeed, but not truly and naturally, but by common adoption or calling, as good men in the Church be called the sons of God. What wonderful wrangling and tergiversation the Arians used to avoid the evidence of this place, we see in S. Augustine li. 3. de Doct. Christ. c. 2. even such as the Protestants do, ³ to avoid the like words, \+it This is my body\+it*, concerning the B. Sacrament. \fq ¹ \ft The WORD true God by nature. \fq ² \ft \+it 1. Io. c. 5, 20.\+it* \fq ³ \ft The Protestants are like the wrangling Arians.\ef*
\v 2 This was in the beginning with God.
\v 3 All things were made by him: and without him was made \f 1 \fr 1.3 \fq nothing. That which was made \ft \+it nothing that was made.\+it*\f* nothing. That which was made, \ef - \fr 1.3 \fq By him. \ft Again, by this he signifieth the eternity, divinity, omnipotency, and equality of the \+sc Word\+sc* or Son, ¹ with God the Father, because by him all things were created. All thins he saith, both visible of this world: and invisible, as Angels and all spiritual creatures. Whereupon it is evident also, that himself is no creature, being the creator of all: neither is sin of his creation, being a defect of a thing, rather than a thing itself, and therefore neither of nor by him. \fq ¹ \ft The WORD not a creature but the creator.\ef*
\v 4 in him was life, and the life was the light of men:
\v 5 and the light shineth in darkness, and the darkness did not comprehend it.
\v 6 There was a man sent from God, whose name was John.
\v 7 This man came for testimony; to give testimony of the light, that all might believe through him.
\v 8 He was not the light, but to give testimony of the light.
\v 9 It was the true light, which lighteneth every man that cometh into this world.
\v 10 He was in the world, and the world was made by him, and the world knew him not.
\v 11 He came into his own, and his own received him not.
\v 12 But as many as received him, he gave them power to be made the sons of God, to those that believe in his name. \ef - \fr 1.12 \fq He gave them power. \ft Free will to receive or acknowledge Christ, & power given to men, if they will, ¹ to be made by Christ the sons of God: but not forced or drawn thereunto by any necessity. \fq ¹ \ft Free-will.\ef*
\v 13 Who, not of blood, nor of the will of flesh, nor of the will of man, but of God are born.
\v 14 \f 1 \fr 1.14 \ft ET VERBUM CARO FACTUM EST.\f* \sc And The Word Was Made Flesh\sc*, and dwelt in us (and we saw the glory of him, glory as it were of the Only-begotten of the Father) full of grace and verity. \ef - \fr 1.14 \fq The Word made flesh. \ft This is the high and divine testimony of Christ's incarnation and that he vouchsaved to become man, For the acknowledging of which inexplicable benefit and giving humble thanks for the same, all Christian people in the world by tradition of the Fathers prostrate themselves or kneel down, ¹ when they hear it sung or said at the holy Mass, either in this Gospel: or in the Creed by these words, \+sc Et Homo Factus Est\+sc*. \fq ¹ \ft Humble kneeling at the solemn words of Christ's incarnation.\ef*
\v 15 John giveth testimony of him, and cryeth saying: This was he of whom I spake, He that shall come after me, is made before me: because he was before me.
\v 16 \f 1 \fr 1.16 \ft He is preferred & made of more dignity and excellency than I, because he was before me & all things, eternal God.\f* And of his fullness we all have received, and grace for grace.
\v 17 For the law was given by Moyses, grace and verity was made by \sc Jesus\sc* Christ.
\v 18 God no man hath seen at any time: the only-begotten Son which is in the bosom of the Father, he hath declared. \ef - \fr 1.18 \fq No man hath seen. \ft Never man in this mortality saw God in the very shape and natural form of the divine essence, but men see him only in the shape of visible creatures, ¹ in or by which it pleaseth him to shew himself unto many diversly in this world: but never in such sort as when he shewed himself in the person of the son of God, being made truly man and conversing with men. \fq ¹ \ft How mortal men see God.\ef*
\v 19 \f 1 \fr 1.19 \ft The Gospel upon the 3. Sunday in Advent.\f* And this is John's testimony, when the Jews sent from Jerusalem Priests and Levites to him, that they should ask him, who art thou?
\v 20 And he confessed, and did not deny: and he confessed, That I am not \sc Christ\sc*.
\v 21 And they asked him: What then? Art thou \x - \xt Mal. 4, 5.\x* Elias? And he said: I am not. Art thou \x - \xt Deu. 18, 15.\x*\f 3 \fr 1.21 \ft By like the Jews ignorantly understood not the place in Deuteronomy, of Christ, and therefore they ask also whether he be the Prophet there spoken of. See also c. 7, 40.\f* the Prophet? And he answered: No.
\v 22 They said therefore unto him: Who art thou, that we may give an answer to them that sent us? what sayest thou of thyself?
\v 23 He said: \it I am the voice of one crying in the desert, make straight the way of our Lord,\it* \x - \xt Esa. 40, 3.\x* as Esaie the Prophet said.
\v 24 And they that were sent were of the Pharisees.
\v 25 And they asked him, and said to him: Why then doest thou baptize, if thou be not Christ, nor Elias, nor the Prophet?
\v 26 John answered them, saying: \x - \xt Mt. 3, 11. Mr. 1, 8. Lu. 3, 16.\x*\f 2 \fr 1.26 \ft He doth often here signify the great difference of his Baptism & of Christ's, as of his person & Christ's. See Annot. Mat. 3.\f* I baptize in water; but there hath stood in the midst of you whom you know not.
\v 27 The same is he that shall come after me, that is made before me; whose latchet of his shoe I am not worthy to loose.
\v 28 These things were done in Bethania beyond Jordan, where John was baptizing.
\v 29 \f 1 \fr 1.29 \ft The Gospel on the octave of the Epiphany.\f* The next day John saw \sc Jesus\sc* coming to him, and he saith: \it Behold the lamb of God, behold him that taketh away the \it* \f 2 \fr 1.29 \ft \+it sins\+it*\f* \it sin of the world.\it* \f 3 \fr 1.29 \ft AGNUS DEI at Mass.\f*
\v 30 This is he of whom I said: After me there cometh a man, which is made before me: because he was before me.
\v 31 And I knew him not, but that he may be manifested in Israel, therefore came I baptizing in water.
\v 32 And John gave testimony, saying: That I saw the Spirit descending as a dove from Heaven, and he remained upon him. \ef - \fr 1.32 \fq The Spirit. \ft Here is an evident testimony of the third Person in Trinity, ¹ which is the Holy Ghost: so that in this one Chapter we find expressly against all Heretics, Jews, & Pagans, set forth the truth of the Church's doctrine concerning the whole Trinity. \fq ¹ \ft The B. Trinity.\ef*
\v 33 And I knew him not: but he that sent me to baptize in water, he said to me: He upon whom thou shalt see the Spirit descending and remaining upon him, he it is that baptizeth in the Holy Ghost.
\v 34 And I saw; and I gave testimony that this is the Son of God.
\v 35 \f 1 \fr 1.35 \ft The Gospel upon S. Andrew's eve.\f* The next day again John stood, and two of his Disciples.
\v 36 And beholding \sc Jesus\sc* walking, he saith: Behold the Lamb of God.
\v 37 And the two Disciples heard him speaking, and they followed \sc Jesus\sc*.
\v 38 And \sc Jesus\sc* turning, and seeing them following him, saith to them, What seek you? Who said to him: Rabbi (which is called by interpretation, Master) where dwellest thou?
\v 39 He saith to them: Come and see. They came, and saw where he abode, and they tarried with him that day: and it was about the tenth hour.
\v 40 And Andrew the brother of Simon Peter was one of the two that had heard of John, and followed him.
\v 41 He findeth first his brother Simon, and saith to him: We have found \f 1 \fr 1.41 \ft Messias in Hebrew, in Greek Christ, in English Anointed, to wit, with the spiritual oil of grace above his brethren. \+it Ps. 44.\+it*\f* \sc Messias\sc*, which is being interpreted, \sc Christ\sc*.
\v 42 And he brought him to \sc Jesus\sc*. And \sc Jesus\sc* looking upon him, said: Thou art Simon the son of Jona: thou shalt be called \f 1 \fr 1.42 \ft Cephas in Syriac, & Peter in Greek, in English, Rock. See Mat. 16, 18.\f* \it Cephas,\it* which is interpreted, Peter. \ef - \fr 1.42 \fq Looking upon him. \ft This beholding of Simon, insinuateth Christ's designment and preferring of him to be the chief Apostle, the Rock of the Church, ¹ and his Vicar, and therefore upon that Divine providence and intention he accordingly changeth his name, calling him for Simon, \+it Cephas\+it*, ² which is a Syriake word, as much to say as, Rock or Stone. And S. Paul commonly calleth him by this name Cephas, whereas other both Greeks and Latins call him altogether by the Greek word, \+it Peter\+it*, which signifieth the self same thing. whereof S. Cyril saith, ³ that our Saviour by fortelling that his name should no more now be Simon, but \+it Peter\+it*, did by the word itself aptly signify, that on him, as on a rock and stone most firm, he would build his Church. \fq ¹ \ft Peter by his new name designed to be the Rock of the Church. \fq ² \ft \+it Cephas. Petrus.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it Li. 2. c. 12. in Ioan.\+it*\ef*
\v 43 On the morrow he would go forth into Galilee, and he findeth Philippe. And \sc Jesus\sc* saith to him: Follow me.
\v 44 And Philippe was of Bethsaida, the city of Andrew and Peter.
\v 45 Philippe findeth Nathanael, and saith to him: Him whom Moyses in the law, and the Prophets wrote of, we have found, \sc Jesus\sc* the son of Joseph, of Nazareth.
\v 46 And Nathanael said to him: From Nazareth can there be any good? Philippe saith to him: Come and see.
\v 47 \f 1 \fr 1.47 \ft The Gospel in a votive Mass of the holy Angels.\f* \sc Jesus\sc* saw Nathanael coming to him, and he saith of him: Behold an Israelite in very deed, in whom there is no guile.
\v 48 Nathanael saith to him: How knowest thou me? \sc Jesus\sc* answered and said to him: Before that Philippe did call thee, when thou wast under the figtree, I saw thee.
\v 49 Nathanael answered him and saith: Rabbi, thou art the Son of God, thou art the King of Israel.
\v 50 \sc Jesus\sc* answered, and said to him: Because I said unto thee, I saw thee under the figtree, thou believest; greater than these things shalt thou see.
\v 51 And he saith to him: Amen Amen I say to you, You shall see the Heaven opened, and the \x - \xt Gen. 28, 12.\x* Angels of God ascending and descending, upon the Son of man.
\c 2
\cd At the request of his mother he worketh his first miracle, turning water into wine at a marriage in Galilee, although the time of his manifestation be not yet come. 12. Then in Hierusalem at Pasch, being but one, and yet obscure, he throweth out of the Temple most miraculously all the merchants. 28. And being yet of the blind Jews asked a sign, he signifieth so long before, that they should kill him, but he will rise again the third day. 23. Which also presently they would do, but that he knowing their false hearts (though many believe in him) will not tarry among them.
\p
\v 1 \sc And\sc* \f 1 \fr 2.1 \ft The Gospel upon the 2. Sunday after the Epiphany.\f* the third day there was a marriage made in Cana of Galilee: and the mother of \sc Jesus\sc* was there.
\v 2 And \sc Jesus\sc* also was called, and his Disciples to the marriage. \ef - \fr 2.2 \fq JESUS also was called. \ft By his vouchsaving to come with his to the Marriage, ¹ he approveth the custom of the Faithful in meeting at honest feats and recreations for maintenance of love, peace, and amity among themselves: he reproveth the heresy of Tatian, Marcion, and such like condemning wedlock: lastly (as S. Cyril saith) ² he sanctifieth and blesseth the Marriage of the Faithful in the new Testament, making it a new creature in him, and discharging it of the manifold maledictions and disorders wherein it was before. By which benediction the often divorces, remarriages, and pluralities of wives, and the women's servile subjection and imparity in that case, be redressed and reduced to the primitive institution, and so Christian marriage made a Sacrament. See \+it S. Aug. de nupt. & concup. li. 1. c. 10 & 21. li. 1 de adult. coniug. c. 8.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Christ with his presence honoureth and approveth Marriage. \fq ² \ft \+it Cyril. in 2. Io. c. 22.\+it*\ef*
\v 3 And the wine failing, the mother of \sc Jesus\sc* saith to him: They have no wine. \ef - \fr 2.3 \fq They have no wine. \ft Our Lady many ways understood that now the time approached of manifesting himself to the world by miracles and preaching, and nothing doubted but that he would now begin at her request. ¹ Whereby we learn that Christ ordinarily giveth not his graces, but humbly asked and requested thereunto, and that his mother's intercession is more than vulgarly effectual, and that he denieth her nothing. \fq ¹ \ft Our Lady's intercession.\ef*
\v 4 And \sc Jesus\sc* saith to her: What is to me and thee woman? My hour cometh not yet. \ef - \fr 2.4 \fq What is to me and thee? \ft Because this speech is subject to diverse senses, ¹ we keep the words of our text, lest by turning it into any English phrase, we might straiten the Holy Ghost's intention to some certain sense either not intended, or not only intended, and so take away the choice and indifferency from the reader, whereof (in holy Scripture specially all Translators must beware. Christ then may mean here, what is that, Woman, to me & thee being but strangers, that they want wine? as some interpret it. Or (which is the more proper use of that kind of speech in holy Writ) what have I to do with thee? that is, why should I have respect to thy desire in this case? In matters touching my charge & the commission of my father for preaching, working miracles, and other graces, I must not be tied to flesh and blood. Which was not a reprehension of our Lady, or signification that he would not hear her in this or other things pertaining to God's glory or the good of men, for the event sheweth the contrary. But it was a lesson to the company that heard it, and namely to his Disciples, that respect of kindred should not draw them to do any thing against reason, or be the principal motion why they do their duties, but God's glory. \fq ¹ \ft Translators of holy Scriptures.\ef*
\v 5 His mother saith to the ministers: Whatsoever he shall say to you, do ye. \ef - \fr 2.5 \fq What soever he shall say. \ft By this you see, our Lady by her divine prudence and entire familiarity and acquaintance with all his manner of speeches, ¹ knew it was no check to her, but a doctrine to others: and that she had no repulse, though he seemed to say his time was not yet come to work miracles: not doubting but he would begin a little before his ordinary time for her sake, as S. Cyril thinketh he did: ² and therefore she admonisheth the waiters to mark well, & to execute whatsoever Christ should bid them do. \fq ¹ \ft Our Lady doubteth not but Christ will grant her petition. \fq ² \ft \+it li. 2. in Io. c. 23.\+it*\ef*
\v 6 And there were set there six water-pots of stone, according to the purification of the Jews, holding every one two or three measures.
\v 7 \sc Jesus\sc* saith to them: Fill the water-pots with water. And they filled them up to the top.
\v 8 And \sc Jesus\sc* saith to them: Draw now, and carry to the chief steward. And they carried it.
\v 9 And after the chief steward tasted the \f 1 \fr 2.9 \ft He that seeth water turned in to wine, needeth not dispute or doubt how Christ changed bread into his body.\f* water made wine, and knew not whence it was, but the ministers knew that had drawn the water; the chief steward calleth the bridegroom,
\v 10 and saith to him: Every man first setteth the good wine, and when they have well drunk, then that which is worse. But thou hast kept the good wine until now.
\v 11 This beginning of miracles did \sc Jesus\sc* in Cana of Galilee: and he manifested his glory, and his Disciples believed in him.
\v 12 After this he went down to Capharnaum himself and his mother, and his brethren, and his Disciples; and there they remained not many days.
\v 13 \f 1 \fr 2.13 \ft The Gospel upon Monday in the fourth week of Lent.\f* And the Pasch of the Jews was at hand, and \sc Jesus\sc* went up to Jerusalem:
\v 14 and he found in the Temple them that sold oxen and sheep and doves, and the bankers sitting.
\v 15 And when he had made as it were a whip of little cords, he cast them all out of the temple, the sheep also and the oxen, and the money of the bankers he poured out, and the tables he overthrew. \ef - \fr 2.15 \fq Cast them out. \ft By this chastising corporally the defilers and abusers of the Temple, he doth not only shew his power, but being but one poor man he could by force execute his pleasure upon so many sturdy fellows: but also his sovereign authority over all offenders, and that not upon their souls only, ¹ as by excommunication and spiritual penalties, but so far as is requisite for the execution of spiritual jurisdiction, upon their bodies and goods also. That the Spiritualty may learn, how far and in what cases, for just zeal of Christ's Church, they may use and exercise both spiritually and temporally their forces and faculties against offenders, specially against the profaners of God's Church, according to the Apostle's allusion 1 Cor. 3. \+it If any defile the Temple of God, him will God destroy.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Profaners of God's Church are to be punished in soul & body by the Spiritual power.\ef*
\v 16 And to them that sold doves, he said: Take away these things hence, and make not the house of my Father, a house of merchandise.
\v 17 And his Disciples remembered that it is written: \it The zeal of thy house hath eaten me.\it* \x - \xt Ps. 68, 10.\x*
\v 18 The Jews therefore answered and said to him: What sign doest thou shew us, that thou doest these things?
\v 19 \sc Jesus\sc* answered and said to them: \x - \xt Mt. 26, 61. 27, 40.\x* Dissolve this temple, and in three days I will raise it.
\v 20 The Jews therefore said: In forty and six years was this temple built, & wilt thou raise it in three days?
\v 21 But he spake of the temple of his body.
\v 22 Therefore when he was risen again from the dead, his Disciples remembered, that he said this, and they believed the scripture and the word that \sc Jesus\sc* did say.
\v 23 And when he was at Jerusalem in the Pasch, upon the festival day, many believed in his name, seeing his signs which he did.
\v 24 But \sc Jesus\sc* did not commit himself unto them, for that he knew all, \ef - \fr 2.24 \fq JESUS committed not himself. \ft S. Augustine applieth this their first faith and belief in Christ, ¹ suddenly raised upon the admiration of his wonders, ² but yet not fully formed or established in them, unto the faith of Novices or Catechumens in the Church: and Christ's not committing his Person to them as yet, to the Church's like wariness and wisdom, in not opening nor giving to them our Lord in the B. Sacrament, because all were not to be trusted with that high point without full trial of their faith. \fq ¹ \ft \+it Tract. in Io. 11.\+it* \fq ² \ft The B. Sacrament is not to be given to novices or younglings in faith.\ef*
\v 25 and because it was not needful for him that any should give testimony of man; for he knew what was in man.
\c 3
\cd He teacheth Nicodemus, that to come to the kingdom of God, Baptism is necessary, as being our Regeneration, 10. which point Nicodemus as then not understanding, 11. he sheweth that they must believe him, and what good cause there is for them so to do. 23. After this he also baptizeth, and John likewise at the same time. 25. Whereupon a question being moved, Whether Baptism is better, 25. John answereth it by saying, that he is so far inferior to Christ, as a mere man to God most high.
\p
\v 1 \sc And\sc* \f 1 \fr 3.1 \ft The Gospel upon Holy Rood day, or Invention of the holy Cross. Maij 3.\f* there was a man of the Pharisees, named Nicodemus, a Prince of the Jews.
\v 2 This man came to \sc Jesus\sc* by night, and said to him: Rabbi, we know that thou art come from God a Master; for no man can do these signs which thou doest, unless God be with him.
\v 3 \sc Jesus\sc* answered, and said to him: Amen, Amen I say to thee, unless a man be born again, he cannot see the Kingdom of God.
\v 4 Nicodemus said to him: How can a man be born, when he is old? can he enter into his mother's womb again & be born?
\v 5 \sc Jesus\sc* answered: Amen, Amen I say to thee, unless a man be born again of water and the Spirit, he cannot enter into the Kingdom of God. \ef - \fr 3.5 \fq Born again of water. \ft As no man can enter into this world not have his life and being in the same, except he be born of his carnal parents: ¹ no more can a man enter into the life & state of grace which is in Christ, or attain to life everlasting, unless he be born and baptized of water and the Holy Ghost. Whereby we see first, this Sacrament to be called our regeneration or second birth, in respect of our natural and carnal, which was before. Secondly, that this Sacrament consisteth of an external element of water, and internal virtue of the Holy Spirit: wherein it excelleth John's Baptism, which had the external element, but not the spiritual grace. thirdly, that no man can enter into the kingdom of God, nor into the fellowship of Holy Church, without it. Whereby the ² Pelagians, and Calvinists be condemned, that promise life everlasting to young children that die without Baptism, and all other that think only faith to serve, or the external element of water superfluous or not necessary: our Saviour's words being plain and general. Though in this case, God which hath not bound his grace, in respect of his own freedom, to any Sacrament, may and doth accept them as baptized, which either are martyred before they could be baptized, ³ or else depart this life with vow and desire to have that Sacrament, but by some remediless necessity could not obtain it. Lastly, it is proved that this Sacrament giveth grace \+it ex opere operato\+it*, that is of the work itself (which all Protestants deny) because it so breedeth our spiritual life in God. as our carnal birth giveth the life of the world. \fq ¹ \ft Baptism in water necessary to salvation. \fq ² \ft \+it * August. hæres. 88.\+it* \fq ³ \ft Baptism in two cases not necessary, but otherwise supplied.\ef*
\v 6 That which is born of the flesh, is flesh: & that which is born of the Spirit, is spirit.
\v 7 Marvel not, that I said to thee, You must be born again.
\v 8 The Spirit breatheth where \f 1 \fr 3.8 \ft We follow rather S. August. & those ancient Fathers, which most commonly understand this place of the Holy Ghost, & not of the wind: although both senses be good.\f* he will; and thou hearest his voice, but thou knowest not whence he cometh and whither he goeth: so is every one that is born of the Spirit.
\v 9 Nicodemus answered, & said to him: How can these things be done?
\v 10 \sc Jesus\sc* answered, and said to him: Thou art a Master in Israel, and art thou ignorant of these things?
\v 11 Amen, Amen I say to thee, that we speak that which we know, and that which we have seen we testify, and our testimony you receive not.
\v 12 If I have spoken to you earthly things, and you believe not: how if I shall speak to you heavenly things, will you believe?
\v 13 And no man hath ascended into Heaven, but he that descended from Heaven, the Son of man which is in Heaven.
\v 14 And as \x - \xt Nu. 21, 9.\x* Moyses exalted the serpent in the desert, so must the Son of man be exalted:
\v 15 that every one which believeth in him, perish not, but may have life everlasting.
\v 16 \f 1 \fr 3.16 \ft The Gospel upon Monday in the Whitsunweek.\f* For so God loved the world, that he gave his only-begotten Son; that every one that believeth in him, perish not, but may have life everlasting.
\v 17 For God sent not his Son into the world, to judge the world, but that the world may be saved by him.
\v 18 He that believeth in him, is not judged. But he that doth not believe, is already judged: because he hath not believed in the name of the only-begotten Son of God. \ef - \fr 3.18 \fq Is judged already. \ft He that believeth in Christ with faith which worketh by charity (as the Apostle speaketh) ¹ shall not be condemned at the later day nor at the hour of his death. but the Infidel, ² be he Jew, Pagan, or Heretic, is already (if he die in his incredulity) by his own profession and sentence condemned, and shall not come to judgement either particular or general, to be discussed according to his works of mercy done or omitted. In which sense S. Paul saith that the obstinate Heretic is condemned by his own judgement, preventing in himself, ³ of his own free will, the sentence both of Christ and of the Church. \fq ¹ \ft \+it Gal. 5, 6.\+it* \fq ² \ft Every infidel, and namely heretics, are judged already. \fq ³ \ft \+it Tit. 3, 11.\+it*\ef*
\v 19 And this is the judgement: because the light is come into the world, and men have loved the darkness rather than the light: for their works were evil.
\v 20 For every one that doeth ill, hateth the light, and cometh not to the light, that his works may not be controlled.
\v 21 But he that doeth verity, cometh to the light, that his works may be made manifest, because they were done in God.
\v 22 After these things \sc Jesus\sc* came and his Disciples into the country of Jewry; & there he abode with them, and baptized.
\v 23 And John also was baptizing in Ænon beside Salim; because there was much water there, and they came, and were baptized.
\v 24 For John was not yet cast into prison.
\v 25 And there rose a question of John's Disciples with the Jews concerning purification.
\v 26 And they came to John, and said to him: Rabbi, he that was with thee beyond Jordan, to whom \x - \xt Io. 1, 19.\x* thou didst give testimony, behold he baptizeth, and all come to him.
\v 27 John answered and said: A man cannot receive any thing, unless it be given him from Heaven,
\v 28 Yourselves do bear me witness, \x - \xt Io. 1, 20.\x* that I said, I am not \sc Christ\sc*; but that I am sent before him.
\v 29 He that hath the bride, is the bridegroom: but the friend of the bridegroom that standeth and heareth him, rejoiceth with joy for the voice of the bridegroom. This my joy therefore is filled.
\v 30 He must increase, and I diminish.
\v 31 He that cometh from above, is above all. He that is of the earth, of the earth he is, and of the earth he speaketh. He that cometh from Heaven, is above all. \ef - \fr 3.31 \fq He that cometh from above. \ft As though he should say, No marvel that men resort to Christ so fast and make less account of men. ¹ for, his baptism and his preaching and his person are all from heaven immediately, He bringeth all from the very bosom, mouth, and substance of God his Father. Whatsoever is in me, is but a little drop of his brace. His spirit and graces are above all measures of men's gifts, even according to his Manhood: and all power temporal and spiritual, the kingdom and the Priesthood, and all sovereignty in heaven and earth are bestowed upon him as he is man also. \fq ¹ \ft The excellency of Christ's power and graces.\ef*
\v 32 And what he hath seen and heard, that he testifieth: and his testimony no man receiveth.
\v 33 He that hath received his testimony, hath signed that God is true.
\v 34 For he whom God hath sent, speaketh the words of God. For God doth not give the Spirit by measure.
\v 35 The Father loveth the Son: & he hath given all things in his hand.
\v 36 He that believeth in the Son: hath life everlasting: but he that is incredulous to the Son, shall not see life, but the wrath of God remaineth upon him.
\c 4
\cd Leaving Jewry because of the Pharisees, in the way to Galilee he talketh with a Samaritane woman, telling her that he will give water of everlasting life, 16. shewing himself to know men's secrets, 19. preferring the Jews' religion before the Samaritanes', but ours (the Christian Catholic religion) before them both, 25. and uttering unto her that he is Christ. 28. which by her testimony and his preaching very many Samaritanes do believe: he in the mean time foretelling his Disciples, of the harvest he will send them into. 45 The Galilæans also receive him, where again he worketh his second miracle.
\p
\v 1 \sc When Jesus\sc* therefore understood that the Pharisees heard that \sc Jesus\sc* maketh more Disciples, and baptizeth, than John,
\v 2 (howbeit \f 1 \fr 4.2 \ft He did not baptize ordinarily: yet that he baptized his Apostles, S. Aug. thinketh it very probable, \+it ep. 108.\+it*\f* \sc Jesus\sc* did not baptize, but his Disciples)
\v 3 he left Jewry, and went again into Galilee.
\v 4 And he had of necessity to pass through Samaria.
\v 5 \x - \xt Gen. 48, 22.\x* He cometh therefore into a city of Samaria which is called Sichar; \f 2 \fr 4.5 \ft The Gospel upon Friday in the 3. week in Lent.\f* beside the manor that Jacob gave to Joseph his son.
\v 6 And there was there the fountain of Jacob. \sc Jesus\sc* therefore wearied of his journey, sat so upon the fountain. It was about the sixth hour.
\v 7 There cometh \f 1 \fr 4.7 \ft This woman is a figure of the Church, not yet justified, but now to be justified. \+it Aug. tract. 15 in Ioan.\+it*\f* a woman of Samaria to draw water. \sc Jesus\sc* said to her: Give me to drink.
\v 8 For his Disciples were gone into the city, to buy meats.
\v 9 Therefore that Samaritane woman saith to him: How doest thou being a Jew, ask of me to drink, which am a Samaritane woman? For the Jews \f 1 \fr 4.9 \ft There were many other causes why the faithful Jews could not abide the Samaritans, but their precise abstaining from their company & conversation, was their Schismatical Temple and service in mount Garizim.\f* do not communicate with the Samaritans.
\v 10 \sc Jesus\sc* answered, and said to her: If thou didst know the gift of God, and who he is that saith unto thee, Give me to drink; thou perhaps wouldest have asked of him, and he would have given thee living water.
\v 11 The woman saith to him: Sir, neither hast thou wherein to draw, and the well is deep; whence hast thou the living water?
\v 12 art thou greater than our Father Jacob, who gave us the well, & himself drank of it, and his children, and his cattle?
\v 13 \sc Jesus\sc* answered, and said to her: Every one that drinketh of this water, shall thirst again; \f 1 \fr 4.13 \ft c He speaketh of his baptizing in the Holy Ghost. See Io. c. 7, 39.\f* but he that shall drink of the water that I will give him, shall not thirst for ever,
\v 14 but the water that I will give him, shall become in him a fountain of water springing up unto life everlasting.
\v 15 The woman saith to him: Lord give me this water, that I may not thirst, nor come hither to draw.
\v 16 \sc Jesus\sc* saith to her: Go, call thy husband, and come hither.
\v 17 The woman answered and said: I have no husband. \sc Jesus\sc* said to her: Thou hast said well, that I have no husband.
\v 18 For thou hast had five husbands; and he whom thou now hast, is not thy husband. This thou hast said truly.
\v 19 The woman saith to him: Lord, I perceive that thou art a Prophet.
\v 20 Our Fathers adored in this mountain, and you say, \x - \xt Deu. 12, 6. Ps. 121. 13.\x* that at Jerusalem is the place where men must adore. \ef - \fr 4.20 \fq Our Fathers adored. \ft By adoration is meant doing of sacrifice. ¹ for other offices of Religion might be done in any place. The Samaritanes to defend their adoring in Garizim, pretended their worshipping there to be more ancient than the Jews in Hierusalem, referring it to Jacob: Whereas indeed that Patriarch adoring there before the Temple was appointed, or the Law given, made nothing for their Schism: Which was begun by Manasses a fugitive Priest, only to hold his unlawful wife thereby, and to obtain Superiority, in Schism: ² which he could not do in the unity of his brethren: long after the Temple of Hierusalem, from which the revolt was made. Therefore Christ giveth sentence for the Jews and the Temple of Hierusalem, affirming that they had a good ground thereof, but the Samaritanes none at all. \fq ¹ \ft The Schismatical temple contendeth against the true Temple. \fq ² \ft \+it Ioseph. li. antiq. 11. c. 8.\+it*\ef* \ef - \fr 4.20 \ft Josephus also recordeth how the Samaritanes demanded of Alexander the Great, the like privileges and immunities as he had granted to the high Priest and Temple of Hierusalem, pretending their Temple to be as great and as worthy, ¹ and themselves to be Jews as the other, and to worship the same God. but their Schismatical hypocrisy was easily spied and dimissed with nothing. Another time the Jews and Samaritanes (as the same writer testifieth) ² made a great stir in Alexandria about the truth and antiquity of the Schismatical temple and service in Garizim and the other true Temple of Salomon: in so much that the matter was put to arbitrement by Ptolomæus the king's commandment, only to try whether of the two was first. And the Schismatics (as their custom is) \+it per saltum\+it* can make their Church or service as old as they list, referring it to the Patriarchs, as our Schismatics do now to Christ and the Apostles. But when the trial was made, only they of Hierusalem did invincibly prove by continual succession of their Priests, ³ and by the just note of the time when the Schismatics went out from them, that theirs was the lawful, and the other the false temple and false adoration. and to it was judged, and the Samaritanes put to silence. Afterward the said Schismatics (which is lightly the end of all Schisms) revolted quite from the Jews' religion, and dedicated their temple in Garizim to Jupiter Olympius, as Calvin's supper and his bread and wine is like at length to come to the sacrifice of Ceres and Bacchus. \fq ¹ \ft The true Temple prevaileth. \fq ² \ft \+it Ioseph. li. 13. antiq. c. 6.\+it* \fq ³ \ft The true Temple is proved by continual succession.\ef*
\v 21 \sc Jesus\sc* saith to her: Woman believe me, that the hour shall come, when you shall neither in this mountain, nor in Jerusalem adore the Father.
\v 22 \x - \xt 4. Reg. 17, 28. 36.\x* You adore that you know not: we adore that we know; for salvation is of the Jews.
\v 23 But the hour cometh, and now it is, when the true adorers shall adore the Father in spirit and verity. For the Father also seeketh such to adore him. \ef - \fr 4.23 \fq In Spirit and verity. \ft Our Saviour foretelleth her that the end & ceasing of their sacrifice & adoration in both the Temples should shortly be, ¹ and even then was begun to be fulfilled: instructing her in three things concerning that point. First, that the true Sacrifice should be tied no more to that one place or nation, but that true adoration should be throughout all Nations according to the Prophecy of Malachie. ² Secondly, that the gross and carnal adoration by the flesh and blood of beasts and other external terrene creatures, not having in them grace, spirit, and life, should be taken away, & another sacrifice succeed, which should be in itself invisible, celestial, divine, full of life, Spirit, and grace. and thirdly, that this adoration and sacrifice should be the verity itself, whereof all the former sacrifices and hosts were but shadows and figures. and he calleth that here spirit and truth, which in the first Chapter is called grace and truth. ³ All which is no more but a prophecy and description of the Sacrifice of the faithful Gentiles in the body and blood of Christ: not that it is not by external means given to us (for otherwise we being men consisting of flesh and blood could not be capable thereof) but that it is spirit and life in itself, being the flesh of the \+sc Word\+sc* of God. And if a man enlarge the word of Adoration, (which here as is said, signifieth properly the worship of God by sacrifice) to all the Sacraments of the new Law, they all likewise be spirit and grace, the Holy Ghost working invisibly and internally upon our souls by every one of them. Whereupon our Baptism, is water & the Holy Ghost: our Penance, the word of absolution and the Holy Ghost: our Confirmation, oil & the Holy Ghost by imposition of hands: finally, all the adoration of the Catholic Church, is properly spiritual, though certain external creatures for our natures, state and necessity, be joined thereunto. Take heed therefore thou gather not of Christ's words, that Christian men should have no use of external office towards God: for that would take away all sacrifice, Sacraments, prayers, Churches, and society of men in his Service. \fq ¹ \ft Christian adoration throughout all nations in every place, in spirit & verity: that is, in the Sacraments and service of the new law, full of spirit & grace: in the verity of things before prefigured, specially the true sacrifice of Christ's body and blood. \fq ² \ft \+it Mal. 1, 11.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it Io. 1, 17.\+it*\ef*
\v 24 God is a Spirit, and they that adore him, must adore in spirit and verity.
\v 25 The woman saith to him: I know that \sc Messias\sc* cometh, (which is called \sc Christ\sc*) therefore when he cometh, he will shew us all things.
\v 26 \sc Jesus\sc* saith to her: I am he, that speak with thee.
\v 27 And incontinent his Disciples came: and they marveled that he talked with a woman. No man for all that said: What seekest thou, or why talkest thou with her?
\v 28 The woman therefore left her water-pot: and she went into the city, and saith to those men:
\v 29 Come, and see a man that hath told me all things whatsoever I have done. Is not he \sc Christ\sc*?
\v 30 They went forth therefore out of the city, and came to him.
\v 31 In the mean time the Disciples desired him, saying: Rabbi eat.
\v 32 But he said to them: I have meat to eat which you know not.
\v 33 The Disciples therefore said one to another: Hath any man brought him for to eat?
\v 34 \sc Jesus\sc* saith to them: My meat is to do the will of him that sent me, to perfit his work.
\v 35 Do not you say that yet there are four months, and harvest cometh? Behold I say to you, lift up your eyes, and see the Countries, that they are white already to harvest.
\v 36 And he that reapeth, receiveth hire, and gathereth fruit unto life everlasting: that both he that soweth, and he that reapeth, may rejoice together.
\v 37 For in this is the saying true: that it is one man that soweth, and it is another that reapeth.
\v 38 I have sent you to reap that which you laboured not: others have laboured, & you have entered into their labours.
\v 39 And of that city many believed in him of the Samaritans, for the word of the \f 1 \fr 4.39 \ft This woman mystically being the Church, it is here signified, that they which at the first believe because the Church teacheth so, afterward be much confirmed, finding it in the Scripture also, and by other instructions.\f* woman giving testimony, that he told me all things whatsoever I have done.
\v 40 Therefore when the Samaritans were come to him, they desired him that he would tarry there. And he tarried there two days.
\v 41 And many more believed for his own word.
\v 42 And they said to the woman, That now not for thy saying do we believe; for ourselves have heard, and do know that this is the Saviour of the world indeed.
\v 43 And after two days he departed thence; and went into Galilee.
\v 44 For \sc Jesus\sc* himself gave testimony that a Prophet hath not honour in his own country:
\v 45 Therefore \x - \xt Mt. 4, 12. Mr. 1, 14. Luc. 4, 14.\x* when he was come into Galilee, the Galilæans received him, whereas they had seen all things that he had done at Jerusalem in the festival day: for themselves also came to the festival day.
\v 46 \x - \xt Io. 2, 9.\x* He came again therefore into Cana of Galilee, \f 2 \fr 4.46 \ft The Gospel upon the 20. Sunday after Pentecost.\f* where he made water wine. And there was a certain lord whose son was sick at Capharnaum.
\v 47 He having heard that \sc Jesus\sc* came from Jewry into Galilee, went to him, and desired him that he would come down and heal his son, For he began to die.
\v 48 \sc Jesus\sc* therefore said to him: Unless you see signs and wonders, you believe not.
\v 49 The lord saith to him: Lord, come down before that my son die.
\v 50 \sc Jesus\sc* saith to him: Go, thy son liveth. The man believed the word that \sc Jesus\sc* said to him, and went.
\v 51 And as he was now going down, his servants met him: and they brought word, saying, That his son lived.
\v 52 He asked therefore of them the hour, wherein he was amended. And they said to him, That yesterday at the seventh hour the fever left him.
\v 53 The father therefore knew that it was in the same hour wherein \sc Jesus\sc* said to him: Thy son liveth, and himself believed and his whole house.
\v 54 This again the \x - \xt Io. 2, 11.\x* second sign did \sc Jesus\sc*, when he was come from Jewry into Galilee.
\c 5
\cd Curing a bedred man at the pond of miracle, because he doth it on the Sabboth, the blind Jews do persecute him, 7. and again because he saith that God is his natural father. 19. He thereupon continueth saying, the Father's operation and his to be in every thing all one, and that he shall do greater things than these miraculous cures, to wit, 21. quicken the dead in soul by sin, as being appointed Judge of all, 28. yea and quicken the dead in bodies also. incontinent judging all uprightly. 31. And that these are not brags of his own, but his witnesses to be 33. John Baptist, 26. his own miraculous works, 37. his Father's voice at his Baptism, 39. the Scriptures also, namely of Moyses. \f - \ft The 2. part: THE ACTS of Christ in Jewry (having already begun his solemn Manifestation in Galilee Mt. 4, 12) the second Pasch of his preaching.\f*
\p
\v 1 \sc After\sc* \f 1 \fr 5.1 \ft The Gospel upon Imber Friday in the first week of Lent.\f* these things there was a festival day of the Jews, and \sc Jesus\sc* went up to Jerusalem.
\v 2 And there is at Jerusalem upon \f 1 \fr 5.2 \fq upon \ft By our Latin text and the Greek, this miraculous pond was in or upon \+it Probatica,\+it* that is, a place where the sheep to be sacrificed, were kept. But by other Latin copies, S. Hierom, and some Greek Fathers, \+it Probatica\+it* is the very pond itself: so called, because the sheep of sacrifice were there washed.\f* Probatica a pond which in Hebrew is surnamed \f 2 \fr 5.2 \fq Bethsaida \ft \+it Bethesda,\+it*\f* Bethsaida, having five porches. \ef - \fr 5.2 \fq A pond. \ft This is as great a wonder and work as was in the old Law, yet never recorded in the Scripture before: the conditions and circumstances of the same much to be distinctly weighed against the Miscreants of this time for many causes. First, ¹ that God without derogation of his honour, yea to the great commendation of it, doth give virtue of miracles and cure to water or other creature. Secondly, ² that he giveth such virtues to these creatures specially which be by use and occupying in sacred functions or otherwise, as it were sanctified: for this pond was it wherein the carcasses of sheep (therefore called Probatica & other beasts to be sanctified, were first washed, to which being always red (as S. Hierom saith) ³ with the blood of hosts, this force was given, for the commendation of the sacrifices of the Law there offered. How much more may we acknowledge such works of God miraculously done in or about the Sacrifice or Sacraments of the new Testament, which faithless men wholly reject and condemn for fables, because they know not the Scriptures nor the power of God. Thirdly, ⁴ that this operation was given at one time more than another, & rather on great festival days than other vulgar times (for this was the feast of Pasch or Pentecost) as days more sanctified, and when the people made greater concourse: which sheweth that we should not wonder to see great miracles done at the Memories and feasts of Martyrs or other great Festivities, ⁵ more than at other places and times. Fourthly, that the Angels or some special Saints are Presidents or Patrons of such places of miracle, and workers also under God of the effects that there extraordinarily be done. which ought to make Christians less doubt, that the force of diverse waters in the world is justly attributed by our forefathers and good stories to the prayers and presence of Saints, which profane incredulous men refer only to nature, untruly pretending that God is more glorified by the works of nature, which be of his ordinary providence, than by the grace of Miracle given to his Saints or Angels by his extraordinary providence. Fifthly, ⁶ that miracles be not wrought on men by their faith only and as well by their presence in spirit as in body, or upon the parties desire or devotion only, according to the Heretics pretext that God is a like present by his power and grace to every man and place: and therefore that men need not to go from their own houses or countries to seek holiness or health at the places of Christ's or his Saint's birth, death, memories: for none could have benefit of this water but he that could touch it, and be in it corporally, and at that just time when the water was in motion by the Angel. Yea sixthly, ⁷ we may consider that in such cases to make the matter more marvelous, rare, and more earnestly to be sought for, and to signify to us that God hath all such extraordinary operations in his own will and commandment, without all rules of our reasons and questioning thereon, none could be healed but that person who first could get into the pond after the Angel came and stirred the same. Seventhly, ⁸ that these graces of corporal cures given to this water, ⁹ prefigure the like force and the Sacrament of Baptism for the cure of souls, though we need not seek the correspondence thereof to the figure in every point. Lastly, ¹⁰ Christ by his power of excellency and prerogative could and did heal this poor man that could get nobody to help him into the water, because he earnestly and long desired the remedy by God appointed, but was excluded by necessity: as our Lord saveth all such as die without Baptism, if they in their own persons earnestly intended, desired, and sought for the same. \fq ¹ \ft 1. Virtue of miracles given to creatures. \fq ² \ft 2. The same given specially to sanctified creatures. \fq ³ \ft \+it Hiero. de locis Heb. post med.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft 3. Miracles done at one time more than at another, specially in greater solemnities. \fq ⁵ \ft 4. Angels and Saints patrons & workers in places of miracles. \fq ⁶ \ft 5. Miracles in certain places, & wrought upon them that, corporally visit the same. See. S. Augustine ep. 137. \fq ⁷ \ft 6. All reasoning in these matters must yield to God's pleasure. \fq ⁸ \ft 7. This water is a figure of Baptism. \fq ⁹ \ft \+it Hiero. con. Lucifer. c. 3. to. 2.\+it* \fq ¹⁰ \ft Neither Jews nor Heretics find the truth, because they search not the Scriptures deeply, but read superficially.\ef*
\v 3 In these lay a great multitude of sick persons, of blind, lame, withered, expecting the stirring of the water.
\v 4 And an Angel of our Lord descended at a certain time into the pond; and the water was stirred. And he that had gone down first into the pond after the stirring of the water, was made whole of whatsoever infirmity he was holden.
\v 5 And there was a certain man there that had been eight and thirty years in his infirmity.
\v 6 Him when \sc Jesus\sc* had seen lying, & knew that \f 1 \fr 5.6 \ft \+it c multum tempus haberet,\+it*\f* he had now a long time, he saith to him: Wilt thou be made whole?
\v 7 The sick man answered him: Lord, I have no man, when the water is troubled, to put me into the pond. For whiles I come, another goeth down before me.
\v 8 \sc Jesus\sc* saith to him: Arise, take up thy bed, and walk.
\v 9 And forthwith he was made whole: and he took up his bed, and walked. And it was the Sabboth that day.
\v 10 The Jews therefore said to him that was healed: It is the Sabboth, thou mayst not take up thy bed.
\v 11 He answered them: He that made me whole, he said to me, take up thy bed, and walk.
\v 12 They asked him therefore, what is that man that said to thee, take up thy bed, and walk?
\v 13 But he that was made whole, knew not who it was. For \sc Jesus\sc* shrunk aside from the multitude standing in the place.
\v 14 Afterward \sc Jesus\sc* findeth him in the temple, & said to him: Behold thou art made whole; sin no more, lest some worse thing chance to thee. \ef - \fr 5.14 \fq Sin no more. \ft We may gather hereby that this man's long infirmity was for punishment of his sins, and that men often attribute their sickness to other natural defects, and seek for remedies of the world in vain, when the sin for which it was sent ¹, remaineth, or is not repented of: and therefore that in all infirmities men should first turn to God & go to their Ghostly father, and then call for the worldly Physicians afterward. \fq ¹ \ft 8. Christ extraordinarily healeth and saveth without creatures.\ef*
\v 15 That man went his way, and told the Jews that it was \sc Jesus\sc* that made him whole.
\v 16 Thereupon the Jews persecuted \sc Jesus\sc*, because he did these things on the Sabboth.
\v 17 But \sc Jesus\sc* answered them: My Father worketh until now; and I do work.
\v 18 Thereupon therefore the Jews sought the more to kill him: because he did not only break the Sabboth, but also he said God was his Father, making himself equal to God.
\v 19 \sc Jesus\sc* therefore answered, and said to them: Amen, amen, I say to you, the Son cannot do any thing of himself, but that which he seeth the Father doing. For what things soever he doeth, these the Son also doeth in like manner.
\v 20 For the Father loveth the Son, and sheweth him all things that himself doeth, and greater works than these will he shew him, that you may marvel.
\v 21 For as the Father doth raise the dead and quickeneth: so the Son also quickeneth whom he will.
\v 22 For neither doth the Father judge any man: but all judgement he hath given to the Son,
\v 23 that all may honour the Son, as they do honour the Father. He that honoureth not the Son, doth not honour the Father, who sent him.
\v 24 Amen, amen I say to you, that he which heareth my word, and believeth him that sent me, hath life everlasting: and he cometh not into judgement, but \f 1 \fr 5.24 \fq shall pass \ft \+it is passed.\+it*\f* shall pass from death into life.
\v 25 \f 1 \fr 5.25 \ft The Gospel upon All souls day.\f* Amen, amen I say to you, that the hour cometh, and now it is when the dead shall hear the voice of the Son of God, and they that have heard, shall live.
\v 26 For as the Father hath life in himself; so he hath given to the Son also to have life in himself:
\v 27 and he hath given him power to do judgement also because he is the Son of man.
\v 28 Marvel not at this, because the hour cometh wherein all that are in the graves, shall hear his voice,
\v 29 and they that have \f 1 \fr 5.29 \ft Not faith only, but good and ill deeds shall be counted, and accordingly rewarded at the day of judgement.\f* done good things, shall come forth into the resurrection of life: but they that have done evil into the resurrection of judgement.
\v 30 I cannot of myself do any thing. As I hear, so I judge: and my judgement is just, because I seek not my will, but the will of him that sent me.
\v 31 If I give testimony of myself, my testimony is not true.
\v 32 There is another that giveth testimony of me: and I know that the testimony is true which he giveth of me.
\v 33 \x - \xt Io. 1, 19.\x* You sent to John; and he gave testimony to the truth,
\v 34 But I receive not testimony of man: but I say these things that you may be saved. \ef - \fr 5.34 \fq I receive not. \ft Our Master meaneth that man's testimony is not necessary to him, nor that the truth of his Divinity dependeth on worldly witnesses or men's commendations: though to us such testimonies be agreeable and necessary, and so for our instruction he vouchsaved to take the testimonies of John the Baptist and Moyses and the Prophets: and departing out of this world, to send forth all his Apostles, and in them all Bishops and Lawful Pastors, to be his witnesses from Hierusalem to the ends of the world.\ef*
\v 35 He was the lamp burning and shining. And you would for a time rejoice in his light.
\v 36 But I have a greater testimony than John. For the works which the Father hath given me to perfit them: the very works themselves which I do, give testimony of me, that the Father hath sent me.
\v 37 And the Father that sent me, himself hath \x - \xt Mt. 3, 17.\x* given testimony of me: neither have you heard his voice at any time, nor seen his shape,
\v 38 and his word you have not remaining in you: because whom he hath sent, him you believe not.
\v 39 Search the \f 1 \fr 5.39 \ft Catholics search the scriptures, and find there, Peter's & his successors Primacy, the real presence, the Priest's power to forgive sins, justification by faith & good works, Virginity preferred before matrimony, breach of the vow of continency damnable, voluntary poverty, Penance, alms,\f* scriptures, for you think in them to have life everlasting: & the same are they that give testimony of me: \ef - \fr 5.39 \fq Search the Scriptures. \ft He reprehendeth the Jews, that reading daily the Scriptures and acknowledging that in them they should find life and salvation, they yet looked over them so superficially that they could not find therein him to be \+sc Christ\+sc* their King, ¹ Lord, life, and Saviour. For the special masters & Scribes of the Jews then, were like unto our Heretics now, who be ever talking and turning and shuffling the Scriptures, but are of all men most ignorant in the deep knowledge thereof. And therefore our Master referreth them not to the reading only or learning them without book, or having the sentences thereof gloriously painted or written in their Temple, houses, or coats: but to the deep search of the meaning and mysteries of the Scriptures, which are not so easily to be seen in the letter. \fq ¹ \ft Sin the cause of sickness and infirmities.\ef*
\v 40 and you will not come to me that you may have life.
\v 41 Glory of men I receive not.
\v 42 But I have known you, that the love of God you have not in you.
\v 43 I am come in the name of my Father, and you receive me not: if \f 1 \fr 5.43 \ft He meaneth specially Antichrist. How then can the\f* another shall come in his own name, him you will receive.
\v 44 How can you believe, that receive glory one of another: and the glory which is of God only, you seek not?
\v 45 Think not that I will accuse you to the Father. There is that accuseth you, Moyses, in whom you trust.
\v 46 For if you did believe Moyses: you would perhaps believe me also. For of me he hath written.
\v 47 And if you do not believe his writings: how will you believe my words.
\c 6
\cd Having with five loaves fed five thousand 16. (walking also the night after upon the sea) 22. on the morrow the people there upon resorting unto him, 27. he preacheth unto them of the Bread which he will give: telling them that he is come from heaven, and therefore able to give such bread as can quicken the world, even his own flesh: and that all his Elect shall believe as much. 60. Many notwithstanding do murmur at this doctrine, yea and become apostatæs, though he tell them that they shall see by his Ascension into heaven, that he is descended from heaven, but the Twelve stick unto him, believing that he is God omnipotent, as he said. Among whom also (that no man be scandalized) he signifieth that he foreknoweth which will become a traitor: as among the foresaid, which would become apostatæs. \f - \ft The 3. part. His ACTS in Galilee, & in Jewry, about the third Pasch and after.\f*
\p
\v 1 \sc After\sc* \x - \xt Mt. 14, 13. Mar. 6, 32. Lu. 9, 10.\x*\f 2 \fr 6.1 \ft The Gospel upon Mid-lent Sunday.\f* these things \sc Jesus\sc* went beyond the sea of Galilee, which is of Tiberias.
\v 2 and a great multitude followed him, because they saw the signs which he did upon those that were sick.
\v 3 \sc Jesus\sc* therefore went up into the mountain, and there he sat with his Disciples.
\v 4 And the Pasch was at hand, the festival day of the Jews.
\v 5 When \sc Jesus\sc* therefore had lifted up his eyes, and saw that a very great multitude cometh to him, he saith to Philippe: Whence shall we buy bread, that these may eat?
\v 6 And this he said, tempting him. For himself knew what he would do?
\v 7 Philippe answered him: Two hundred penny worth of bread is not sufficient for them, that every man may take a little piece.
\v 8 One of his Disciples, Andrew the brother of Simon Peter, saith to him:
\v 9 There is a boy here that hath five barley loaves, & two fishes; but what are these among so many?
\v 10 \sc Jesus\sc* therefore saith: Make the men to sit down. And there was much grass in the place. The men therefore sat down, in number about five thousand.
\v 11 \sc Jesus\sc* therefore took the loaves; and when he had given thanks, he distributed to them that sat. In like manner also of the fishes as much as they would.
\v 12 And after they were filled, he said to his Disciples: Gather the fragments that are remaining, lest they be lost.
\v 13 They gathered therefore, and filled twelve baskets with fragments of the five barley loaves, which remained to them that had eaten.
\v 14 Those men therefore when they had seen what a sign \sc Jesus\sc* had done, said, That this is the Prophet indeed that is to come into the world.
\v 15 \sc Jesus\sc* therefore when he knew that they would come to take him, and make him king, \x - \xt Mt. 14, 23. Mr. 6, 46.\x* he fled again into the mountain himself alone.
\v 16 And when even was come, his Disciples went down to the sea.
\v 17 And when they were gone up into the ship, they came beyond the sea into Capharnaum: And now it was dark and \sc Jesus\sc* was not come unto them.
\v 18 And the sea arose, by reason of a great wind that blew.
\v 19 When they had rowed therefore about five and twenty or thirty furlongs, they see \sc Jesus\sc* walking upon the sea, and to draw nigh to the ship, and they feared.
\v 20 But he said to them: It is I, fear not.
\v 21 They would therefore have taken him into the ship: and forthwith the ship was at the land to which they went.
\v 22 The next day, the multitude that stood beyond the sea, saw that there was no other boat there but one, and that \sc Jesus\sc* had not entered into the boat with his Disciples, but that his Disciples only were departed.
\v 23 But other boats came in from Tiberias beside the place where they had eaten the bread, our Lord \f 1 \fr 6.23 \ft These words do plainly import, that the giving thanks was an effectual blessing of the bread and working the multiplication thereof.\f* giving thanks.
\v 24 When therefore the multitude saw that \sc Jesus\sc* was not there, nor his Disciples, they went up into the boats, & came to Capharnaum seeking \sc Jesus\sc*.
\v 25 And when they had found him beyond the sea, they said to him: Rabbi, when camest thou hither?
\v 26 \sc Jesus\sc* answered them, and said: Amen, amen I say to you, you seek me not because you have seen signs, but because you did eat of the loaves, and were filled.
\v 27 Work not the meat that perisheth, but that endureth unto life everlasting, which the Son of man will give you. For him the Father, God, hath signed. \ef - \fr 6.27 \fq Work not the meat. \ft By their greedy seeking after him for meat of the body, he taketh occasion to draw them to the desire of a more excellent food which he had to give them, and so by little to open unto them the great mean and mystery of the B. Sacrament: which (as he proveth) doth not only far pass their ordinary bread or his marvelous multiplied loaves, but Manna itself, which they thought came from heaven, and so much wondered at it.\ef*
\v 28 They said therefore unto him: What shall we do that we may work the works of God?
\v 29 \sc Jesus\sc* answered, and said to them: This is the work of God, that you believe in him whom he hath sent.
\v 30 They said therefore to him: What sign therefore doest thou, that we may see, and may believe thee? what workest thou?
\v 31 Our \x - \xt Exo. 16, 4. 14. Ps. 77, 24.\x* Fathers did eat Manna in the desert as it is written, \it Bread from Heaven he gave them to eat.\it*
\v 32 \sc Jesus\sc* therefore said to them: \f 1 \fr 6.32 \ft Why we keep the Hebrew word, \+it Amen,\+it* & translate it not, See the Annot. \+it c. 8. vers. 14.\+it*\f* Amen, amen I say to you, Moyses gave you not the bread from Heaven, but my Father giveth you the true bread from Heaven. \ef - \fr 6.32 \fq The true bread. \ft Though the person of Christ incarnate, even out of the Sacrament also, be meant under the Metaphores of bread and drink from heaven: and our belief in him, be signified by eating and feeding: yet the causes why they should be recommended unto us in such terms, were, that he was to be eaten and drunken indeed in the forms of bread and wine: for the which cause his body on the cross is called ¹ his bread: & his blood shed on the cross, ² the blood of the grape: no doubt because the same body and blood were in Holy Sacrament to be eaten and drunken. In which speeches, either of Christ's person generally, or peculiarly of the same as in the B. Sacrament, \+it the true bread\+it* is not taken properly and specially for that substance which is of corn, and called with us bread: ³ but generally for food or meat, and therefore it hath joined with it lightly a term signifying a more excellent sort of sustenance: as, the true bread, the bread of heaven, the bread of life, Supersubstantial bread. in which sort the holy Sacrament which is Christ's body, is both here, and in S. Luke and S. Paul also, ⁴ often called bread, ⁵ even after consecration: not only for that it was made of bread, but because it is bread more truly, ⁶ and by more excellent property and calling, than that which ordinarily is named bread. \fq ¹ \ft Why Christ is called bread: & believing, eating. \fq ² \ft \+it Ierem. 11, 19. Gen. 49, 11.\+it* \fq ³ \ft What signifieth, The true bread. \fq ⁴ \ft \+it Lu. 24, 35. Act. 2, 42. 20, 7.\+it* \fq ⁵ \ft The B. Sacrament called bread. \fq ⁶ \ft \+it 1. Cor. 10.\+it*\ef*
\v 33 For the bread of God it is that descended from Heaven, and giveth life to the world.
\v 34 They said therefore unto him: Lord, give us always this bread.
\v 35 And \sc Jesus\sc* said to them: I am the bread of life, he that cometh to me, shall not hunger; and he that believeth in me, shall never thirst.
\v 36 But I said to you that both you have seen me and you believe not.
\v 37 \f 1 \fr 6.37 \ft The Gospel in the Anniversary of the dead.\f* All that the Father giveth me, shall come to me; and him that cometh to me I will not cast forth.
\v 38 Because I descended from Heaven, not to do mine own will, but the will of him that sent me.
\v 39 For this is the will of him that sent me, the Father: that all that he hath given me I leese not thereof, but raise it in the last day.
\v 40 And this is the will of my Father that sent me; that every one that seeth the Son, and believeth in him, have life everlasting, & I will raise him in the last day.
\v 41 The Jews therefore murmured at him, because he had said, I am the bread which descended from Heaven;
\v 42 and they said: Is not this \sc Jesus\sc* the son of Joseph, whose father and mother we know? How then saith he, That I descended from Heaven?
\v 43 \sc Jesus\sc* therefore answered and said to them: Murmur not one to another:
\v 44 \f 1 \fr 6.44 \ft The Gospel upon Imber Wednesday in Whitsunweek.\f* no man can come to me, unless the Father that sent me, draw him, and I will raise him up in the last day. \ef - \fr 6.44 \fq Draw him \ft The Father draweth us and teacheth us to come to his Son, and to believe these high and hard mysteries of his incarnation and of feeding us with his own substance in the Sacrament: ¹ ² not compelling or violently forcing any against their will or without any respect of their consent, as Heretics pretend: but by the sweet internal motions and persuasions of his grace and spirit he wholly maketh us of our own will and liking to consent to the same. \fq ¹ \ft \+it Aug. cont. duas Ep. Pelag. li. 1. c. 19. & Ser. 2. de verb. Ap. c. 2.\+it* \fq ² \ft God draweth us with our free will.\ef*
\v 45 It is written in the Prophets: \it And all shall be docible of God.\it* \x - \xt Esa. 54, 13.\x* Every one that hath heard of the Father, & hath learned, cometh to me.
\v 46 Not that any man hath seen the Father, but he which is of God; this hath seen the Father.
\v 47 Amen, amen I say to you: He that believeth in me, hath life everlasting.
\v 48 I am the bread of life.
\v 49 Your fathers did eat Manna in the desert; and they died. \ef - \fr 6.49 \fq Manna, and died. \ft The Heretics holding the fathers of the old Testament to have eaten of the same meat, and to have had as good Sacraments as we, be here refuted: Christ putting a plain difference in the very substance thereof, and in the graces and effects much more at large. ¹ Manna was only a figure of the B. Sacrament, though a very excellent figure thereof for many causes. It came in a sort from heaven, our Sacrament more: it was made by God miraculously, our Sacrament more: it was to be eaten for the time of their peregrination, our Sacrament more: it was to every man what he liked best, our Sacrament more: a little thereof served and sufficed as well as much, our Sacrament more: it was reserved for such days as it could not be gathered, and our Sacrament much more: it was kept for a memorial in the ark of the Testament, our Sacrament much more: the discontented and incredulous murmured and gainsaid it, at our Sacrament much more: it sustained their bodies in the desert, our Sacrament both body and soul much more. \fq ¹ \ft The manifold preeminences of the B. sacrament above Manna.\ef*
\v 50 This is the bread that descended from Heaven: that if any man eat of it, he die not.
\v 51 \f 1 \fr 6.51 \ft The Gospel in a daily Mass for the dead.\f* I am the living bread, that came down from Heaven. If any man eat of this bread, he shall live for ever: and \x - \xt Mt. 26, 26. Mr. 14, 22. Lu. 22, 19. 1. Cor. 11, 24.\x* the bread which I will give, is my flesh for the life of the world.
\v 52 The Jews therefore strove among themselves, saying: How can this man give us his flesh to eat? \ef - \fr 6.52 \fq How can this man? \ft \+it It came not to their mind that nothing was impossible to God, that wickedly said, How can this man give us his flesh? But we may make great profit of this sin, believing the Mysteries, and taking a lesson, never to say or once think, How? For it is a Jewish word and worthy all punishment.\+it* ¹ So saith \+it S. Cyril. li. 4. c. 13 in Io.\+it* Nevertheless if one asked only for desire to learn in humility, as our Lady did touching her having a child in her virginity, then he must take the Angel's answer to her, That it is of the Holy Ghost. so saith S. Damascene \+it li. 4. c. 14.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft In the B. Sacrament, \+it How\+it*, is a Jewish word.\ef*
\v 53 \sc Jesus\sc* therefore said to them: Amen, amen I say to you, unless you eat the flesh of the Son of man, and drink his blood, you shall not have life in you. \ef - \fr 6.53 \fq Unless you eat. \ft \+it Christ commending the Sacrament of the faithful unto us, said, Except you eat &c. you cannot have life in you. So the life saith of life, and to him that thinketh the life to be a liar, this meat shall be death & not life to him.\+it* ¹ August. \+it Ser. 2 de verb. Ap. c. 1.\+it* And S. Leo thus, ² \+it Because our Lord saith, Except you eat &c. let us so communicate that we nothing doubt of the truth of Christ's body and blood: for that is received with mouth, which is believed in heart: and they answer Amen in vain, that dispute against that which they receive\+it*. \fq ¹ \ft The real presence. \fq ² \ft \+it Ser. 6. de ieiun. 7. mens.\+it*\ef* \ef - \fr 6.53 \fq And drink. \ft This the Protestants allege for the necessity of receiving in both kinds: but in respect of themselves (who lightly hold all this chapter to pertain nothing to the sacramental receiving, ¹ but to spiritual feeding on Christ by faith only) it can make nothing for one kind or other. And in respect of us Catholics, who believe Christ's whole person both humanity and Divinity, both flesh and blood to be in either form, and to be wholly received no less in the first, than in the second or in both, this place commandeth nothing for both the kinds. \fq ¹ \ft Receiving in both kinds not necessary.\ef* \ef - \fr 6.53 \fq You shall not have life. \ft Though the Catholics teach these words to be spoken of the Sacrament, ¹ yet they mean not (no more than our Saviour here doth) to exclude all from salvation, that receive not actually and Sacramentally under one or both kinds. For then children that die after they be baptized and never received Sacramentally, should perish: which to hold, were heretical. Neither did S. Augustine mean, ² applying these words to infants also, that they could not be saved without receiving sacramentally, as not only the Heretics, but Erasmus did unlearnedly mistake him: ³ but his sense is that they were by the right of their Baptism joined to Christ's body Mystical, and thereby spiritually partakers of the other Sacrament also of Christ's body and blood. As all Catholic men that be in prison, joining with the Church of God in heart and desire to receive and be partakers with the Church of this Sacrament, and those specially that devoutly hear Mass and adore in presence the body and blood of Christ, joining in heart with the Priest, all these receive life and fruit of the Sacrament, though at every time they receive not sacramentally in one or both kinds. ⁴ And although in the Primitive Church the holy Sacrament in the second kind were often given even to infants to sanctify them, yet (as the holy Councel hath declared) it was never ministered unto them with opinion that they could not be saved without it. and therefore the Heretics do untruly charge the Church and the Fathers with that error. \fq ¹ \ft The Sacramental receiving of Christ's body, not always necessary to salvation. \fq ² \ft \+it Li. de pec. merit. c. 20.\+it* \fq ³ \ft The true meaning of S. Augustin's words touching infants receiving of the B. Sacrament. \fq ⁴ \ft \+it Conc. Trid. Ses. 21. c. 4.\+it*\ef*
\v 54 He that eateth my flesh, and drinketh my blood, hath life everlasting; and I will raise him up in the last day. \ef - \fr 6.54 \fq I will raise him. \ft \+it As the Son liveth by the Father, even so do we live by his flesh,\+it* saith S. Hilary. \+it li. 8. de Trin.\+it* And S. Cyril again thus. ¹ \+it Though by nature of our flesh we be corruptible, yet by participation of life we are reformed to the property of life.\+it* ² \+it For not only our souls were to be lifted up by the Holy Ghost to life everlasting, but this rude gross terrestrial body of ours is to be reduced to immortality, by touching, tasting, and eating this agreeable food of Christ's body. And when Christ saith, I will raise him up, he meaneth that this body which he eateth, shall raise him. Our flesh\+it* (saith Tertullian) ³ \+it eateth the body and blood of Christ, that the soul may also be fatted. therefore they shall both have one reward at the Resurrection.\+it* And S. Irenæus, ⁴ \+it How do they affirm that our bodies be not capable of life everlasting, which is nourished by the body and blood of our Lord? Either let them change their opinion, or else cease to offer the Eucharist.\+it* S. Gregory Nyssene also saith, ⁵ \+it That lively body entering into our body, changeth it and maketh it like and immortal.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft \+it Cyril. li. 4. c. 14, 15.\+it* \fq ² \ft The effects of the B. Sacrament both in our body and soul. \fq ³ \ft \+it Tertul. de resur. car. nu. 7.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft \+it Li. 4, c. 34.\+it* \fq ⁵ \ft \+it Nyss. in orat. catech. magna.\+it*\ef*
\v 55 \f 1 \fr 6.55 \ft The Gospel upon CORPUS CHRISTI day.\f* For my flesh, is meat indeed: and my blood is drink indeed. \ef - \fr 6.55 \fq Meat indeed. \ft Manna, was not the true meat: nor the water of the rock, ¹ the drink indeed: for they did but drive away death or famine for a time and for this life. \+it But the holy Body of Christ is the true food nourishing to life everlasting, and his blood the true drink that driveth death away utterly, for they be not the body and blood of a mere man, but of him that being joined to life is made life: and therefore are we the body and members of Christ, because by this benediction of the mystery we receive the son of God himself.\+it* So saith S. Cyril li. 4 c. 16 in Io. \fq ¹ \ft The B. Sacrament is the true Manna & water of the rock.\ef*
\v 56 He that eateth my flesh, and drinketh my blood, abideth in me, and I in him.
\v 57 As the living Father hath sent me, and I live by the Father: and he that eateth me, the same also shall live by me.
\v 58 This is the bread that came down from Heaven. Not as your fathers did eat Manna, and died. He that eateth this bread, shall live for ever. \ef - \fr 6.58 \fq He that eateth this bread. \ft By this place the holy Councel ¹ proveth that for the grace and effect of the Sacrament, ² which is the life of the soul, there is no difference whether a man receive both kinds or one, because our Saviour who before attributed life to the eating and drinking of his body and blood, doth here also affirm the same effect, which is life everlasting, to come of eating only under one form. Therefore the Heretics be seditious calumniators that would make the people believe, the Catholic Church and Priests to have defrauded them of the grace and benefit of one of the kinds in the Sacrament. Nay, it is they that have defrauded the world, by taking away both the real substance of Christ, and the grace from one kind and both kinds, ³ and from all other Sacraments. The Church doth only (by the wisdom of God's Spirit and by instruction of Christ and his Apostles, according to time and place, for God's most honour, the reverence of the Sacrament, and the people's most profit thereby) dispose of the manner and order, how the Priest, how the people shall receive, and all other particular points, \+it Which himself\+it* (saith S. Augustine) ⁴ \+it did not take order for, that he might commit that to the Apostles, by whom he was to dispose his Church's affairs.\+it* though both he and the Apostles and the Fathers of the primitive Church left us example of receiving under one kind, Christ ⁵ \+it at Emmaus\+it*, The Apostles \+it Act. 2. 42.\+it* ⁶ The primitive Church in giving the blood only to children, \+it Cypr. li. de lapsis, nu. 10.\+it* in reserving most commonly the body only, \+it Tertul. li. 2 ad uxo. nu. 4. Cypr. li. de lapsis, nu. 10.\+it* in houseling the sick therewith, \+it Euseb. ec. hist. li. 6 c. 16.\+it* in the holy Eremites also that received and reserved it commonly and not the blood, in the wilderness, \+it Basil. ep. ad Cæsariam Patritiam.\+it* and in diverse other cases which were too long to rehearse. \fq ¹ \ft \+it Conc. Trid. Ses. 21. c. 1.\+it* \fq ² \ft The whole grace and effect thereof in one kind, and therefore the people not defrauded. \fq ³ \ft Receiving in one or both kinds, indifferent, according to the holy Church's appointment. \fq ⁴ \ft \+it Ep. 118. c. 6. ad Ianuarium.\+it* \fq ⁵ \ft \+it Lu. 24, 15.\+it* \fq ⁶ \ft Authority of Scriptures and the Primitive Church for receiving in one kind.\ef* \ef - \fr 6.58 \ft ¹ Whereby the Church being warranted and in the ruling of such things fully taught by God's spirit, as well for the reproving of certain heresies, that Christ God and man was not whole and all in every part of the Sacrament, as specially for that the Christian people being now enlarged, and the communicants often so many at once, that neither so much wine could be conveniently consecrated, nor without manifold accidents of shedding or abusing be received (whereof the Protestants have no regard, because it is but common wine which they occupy, but the Church knowing it to be Christ's own blood, must have all dreadful regard) therefore I say she hath decreed and for some hundreth years put in use, that the Priest saying Mass, should always both consecrate and also receive both kinds, ² because he must express lively the Passion of Christ, and the separation of his blood from his body in the same, and for to imitate the whole action and institution as well in sacrificing as receiving as to whom properly it was said, \+it Do this\+it*, ³ for that was spoken only to such as have power thereby to offer and consecrate. But the Lay men, and the Clergy also when they do not execute or say Mass themselves, should receive in one kind, being thereby no less partakers of Christ's whole person and grace, than if they received both. For (as S. Paul saith) \+it He that eateth the hosts, is partaker of the altar\+it*. ⁴ He that eateth, saith he: for though there were drink-offerings or libaments joined lightly to every sacrifice, yet it was enough to eat only of one kind, for to be partaker of the whole. \fq ¹ \ft The causes of the Church's practice & ordinance concerning one kind. \fq ² \ft The Priests that say Mass, must receive both kinds. \fq ³ \ft \+it Lu. 22, 19. 2. Cor. 11.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft \+it 1. Cor. 10, 18.\+it*\ef*
\v 59 These things he said teaching in the Synagogue, in Capharnaum.
\v 60 Many therefore of his Disciples hearing it, said: This saying is hard, and who can hear it?
\v 61 But \sc Jesus\sc* knowing with himself that his Disciples murmured at this, he said to them: Doth this scandalize you?
\v 62 If then you shall see \x - \xt Io. 3, 13.\x* the Son of man ascend where he was before? \ef - \fr 6.62 \fq If you shall see. \ft Our Savior seemeth to insinuate, that such as believe not his words touching the holy Sacrament, ¹ and think it impossible for him to give his Body to be eaten in so many places at once, being yet in earth, should be much more scandalized and tempted after they saw or knew him to be ascended into heaven. Which is proved true in the Capharnaites of this time. whose principal reason against Christ's presence in the Sacrament is, that he is ascended into heaven: yea, who are so bold as to expound this same sentence for themselves thus, It is not this body or flesh which I will give you, for that I will carry with me to heaven. Whereby if they meant only that the condition and qualities of his body in heaven should be other than in the Sacrament, it were tolerable: for S. Augustine speaketh sometime in that sense. but to deny the substance of the body to be the same, that is wicked. \fq ¹ \ft Christ insinuateth that faithless men shall not believe his presence in the B. Sacrament, because he is ascended.\ef*
\v 63 It is the spirit that quickeneth, the flesh profiteth nothing. The words that I have spoken to you, be spirit and life. \ef - \fr 6.63 \fq The flesh profiteth nothing. \ft If this speech were spoken in the sense of the Sacramentaries, it would take away Christ's Incarnation, manhood, and death, no less than his corporal presence in the Sacrament. for if his flesh were not profitable, all these things were vain. Therefore \+sc Christ\+sc* denieth not his own flesh to be profitable, but that their gross and carnal conceiving of his words, of his flesh, and of the manner of eating the same, was unprofitable. ¹ Which is plain by the sentence following, where he warneth them, that his words be spirit and life, of high Mystical meaning, and not vulgarly and grossly to be taken, as they took them. And it is the use of the Scripture to call man's natural sense, reason, and carnal resisting or not reaching supernatural truths, flesh or blood. as, \+it Flesh and blood revealed not this to thee &c. Mat. 16.\+it* This carnality then of theirs, stood in two points specially: first, that they imagined that he would kill himself, and cut & mangle his flesh into parts, & so give it them raw or roast to be eaten among them. Which could not be meant, saith S. Augustine: ² for that had contained an heinous and barbarous fact, and therefore they might and should have been assured, that he would command no such thing: but some other sweet sense to be of his hard, mystical, or figurative words, and to be fulfilled in a Sacrament, mystery, and a marvelous divine sort, otherwise than they could comprehend. Secondly, they did err touching his flesh, in that they took it to be the flesh of a mere man, and of a dead man also, when it should come to be eaten: of which kind of flesh Christ here pronounceth, that is profiteth nothing. Whereupon S. Cyril saith, ³ \+it This body is not of Peter of Paul or any other like, but of Christ \+sc Jesus\+sc* who is the life itself: and therefore this Body giveth life, the very fullness of the Divinity dwelling in it.\+it* ⁴ And the holy Councel of Ephesus in the 11. Anathematisme expounded also by the said S. Cyril: \+it The Eucharist is not the body of any common person (for the flesh of a common man could not quicken) but of the \+sc Word\+sc* itself. But the Heretic Nestorius dissolveth the virtue of this Mystery, holding man's flesh only to be in the Eucharist\+it*. Thus there. And ⁵ S. Ignatius cited of Theodorete, and many other Fathers have the like. Whereby we may see that it cometh of the Divinity and Spirit (without which Christ's flesh cannot be) that this Sacrament giveth life. \fq ¹ \ft The Capharnaites' gross understanding of Christ's flesh to be given or eaten. And, how his flesh doth profit, and not profit. \fq ² \ft \+it August. de Doct. Chr. li. 3. c. 13.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it Li. 4. c. 23. in Io.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft Christ's flesh giveth life because it is the flesh of God & man. \fq ⁵ \ft \+it Ignatius apud Theodor. dial. 3.\+it*\ef*
\v 64 But there be certain of you that believe not. For \sc Jesus\sc* knew from the beginning who they were that did not believe, and who he was that would betray him. \ef - \fr 6.64 \fq That believe not. \ft It is lack of faith, ¹ you see here, that causeth men to spurn against this high truth of the Sacrament: as also it may be learned here, that it is the great and merciful gift of God that Catholic men do against their senses and carnal reasons, believe and submit themselves to the humble acknowledging of this Mystery: lastly, that it may well ² by Christ's insinuation of Judas, be gathered, that he specially spurned against our Master's speeches of the holy Sacrament. \fq ¹ \ft Judas the chief of them that believe not the real presence. \fq ² \ft \+it vers. 64.\+it*\ef*
\v 65 And he said: Therefore did I say to you, that no man can come to me, unless it be given him of my Father.
\v 66 After this many of his Disciples went back: and now they walked not with him. \ef - \fr 6.66 \fq Went back. \ft It can be no marvel to us now that so many revolt from the Church, by offence or scandal unjustly taken at Christ's body and blood in the Sacrament: seeing many of his Disciples that saw his wonderful life, doctrine, and miracles, forsook Christ himself, upon the speech & promise of the same Sacrament. ¹ for the mystery of it is so supernatural and divine in itself, and withal so low & base for our sake, by the shew of the forms of these terrene elements under which it is, and we eat it: that the unfaithful and infirm do so stumble at Christ in the Sacrament, as the Jews and Gentiles did at Christ in his humanity. For, the causes of contradictions of the Incarnation and Transubstantiation be like. And it may be verily deemed, that who soever now cannot believe the Sacrament to be Christ, because it is under the forms of bread and wine, and is eaten and drunken, would not then have believed that Christ had been God, because he was in shape of man, and crucified. ² To conclude, it was not a figure nor a mystery of bare bread and wine, nor any Metaphorical or Allegorical speech, that could make such a troop of his Disciples revolt at once. When he said he was a door, a vine, a way, a Pastor, and such like (unto which kind of speeches the Protestants ridiculously resemble the words of the holy Sacrament) who was so mad to mistake him, or to forsake him for the same? For the apostles at the least would have plucked them by the sleeves, and said, Go not away my masters, he speaketh parables. The cause therefore was their incredulity, and the height of the Mystery, for that they neither knew the means how it might be present, nor would believe that he was able to give his flesh to be eaten in many places. And even such is the unbelief of the Heretics about this matter at this day. \fq ¹ \ft Heretics believe not the real presence, because they see bread and wine: as the Jews believed not his God-head because of the shape of a poor man. \fq ² \ft The disciples revolting at Christ's words, prove that he spake not metaphorically, as at other times.\ef*
\v 67 \sc Jesus\sc* therefore said to the Twelve: What, will you also depart?
\v 68 Simon Peter therefore answered him: Lord, to whom shall we go? thou hast the words of eternal life. \ef - \fr 6.68 \fq Peter answered. \ft ¹ Peter answereth for the Twelve, not knowing that Judas in heart was already naught, and believed not Christ's former words touching the B. Sacrament, but was to revolt afterward as well as the other. ² Wherein Peter beareth the person of the Church and all Catholic men, that for no difficulty of his word, nor for any revolt (be it never so general) of Schismatics, Heretics, or Apostatæs, either for this Sacrament or any other Article, will ever forsake Christ. And when company draweth us to revolt, let us say thus: Lord, whither or to whom shall we go, when we have forsaken thee? to Calvin, Luther, or such: and forsake thee and thy Church with the unfaithful multitude? No, thou hast the words of life, and we believe thee, and thy Church will not nor cannot beguile us. \+it Thou hast\+it* (saith S. Augustine) ³ \+it life everlasting in the ministration of thy body and blood,\+it* and a little after, \+it Thou art life everlasting itself, and thou givest not in thy flesh and blood but that which thyself art.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft As S. Peter beareth the person of all believing Catholics: so Judas of all unbelieving Heretics. He being the first Arch-heretic; and this, against the B. Sacrament, the first heresy. \fq ² \ft \+it Cypr. ep. 55. nu. 3.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it Tract. 27. in Evang. Io.\+it*\ef*
\v 69 And we believe and have known that thou art Christ the Son of God.
\v 70 \sc Jesus\sc* answered them: Have not I chosen you the Twelve; and of you one is a devil?
\v 71 And he meant Judas Iscariot, Simon's son: for this same was to betray him, whereas he was one of the Twelve.
\c 7
\cd The Jews (of Hierusalem) seeking his death, he walketh in Galilee: Where he signifieth to his brethren, that not in this feast Scenopegia, but in another (to Wit, Pasch following) the Jews should kill him: that is, not when they would, but when he will, 10. in so much that at this feast he teacheth openly in the Temple, and converteth many, 14. both in the middle day 37. and the last day thereof, without any hurt, though also the Rulers send to apprehend him.
\p
\v 1 \sc After\sc* \f 1 \fr 7.1 \ft The Gospel upon Tuesday in Passion Week.\f* these things \sc Jesus\sc* walked \f 2 \fr 7.1 \fq into Galilee \ft \+it in Galilee,\+it*\f* into Galilee, for he would not walk \f 3 \fr 7.1 \fq into Jewry \ft \+it in Jury:\+it*\f* into Jewry: because the Jews sought to kill him.
\v 2 And the festival day of the Jews, \x - \xt Lev. 23, 34.\x* \f 2 \fr 7.2 \ft \+it Scenopegia (Lev. 23. σκηνῶν ἑορτὴ)\+it* is the \+it feast of Tabernacles,\+it* which the Jews kept from the 7. Octob. for eight days together, by God's commandment, for a memory that their Fathers dwelt by God's protection forty years in tabernacles or tents, and not in houses, coming out of ÆGYPT. See Levit. 23, 34.\f* Scenopegia, was at hand.
\v 3 And his brethren said to him: Pass from hence, and go into Jewry; that thy Disciples also may see thy works which thou doest.
\v 4 For no man doeth any thing in secret, and seeketh himself to be in public. If thou do these things, manifest thyself to the world.
\v 5 For neither did his brethren believe in him.
\v 6 \sc Jesus\sc* therefore saith to them: My time is not yet come; but your time is always ready.
\v 7 The world cannot hate you, but me it hateth: because I give testimony of it, that the works thereof are evil.
\v 8 Go you up to this festival day: \f 1 \fr 7.8 \fq I go not up \ft \+it I will not yet go up\+it*\f* I go not up to this festival day; because my time is not yet accomplished.
\v 9 When he had said these things, himself tarried in Galilee.
\v 10 But after his brethren were gone up, then he also went up to the festival day, not openly, but as it were in secret.
\v 11 The Jews therefore sought him in the festival day, and said: Where is he?
\v 12 And there was much murmuring in the multitude of him. For certain said: That he is good. And others said: No, but he seduceth the multitudes.
\v 13 Yet no man spake openly of him for fear of the Jews.
\v 14 \f 1 \fr 7.14 \ft The Gospel upon Tuesday in the 4. week of Lent.\f* And when the festivity was now half done, \sc Jesus\sc* went up into the temple, and taught.
\v 15 And the Jews marveled, saying: How doth this man know letters, whereas he hath not learned?
\v 16 \sc Jesus\sc* answered them, and said: My doctrine is not mine, but his that sent me.
\v 17 If any man \f 1 \fr 7.17 \ft The way to come to know the truth, is to live well.\f* will do the will of him, he shall understand of the doctrine whether it be of God, or I speak of myself.
\v 18 \f 1 \fr 7.18 \ft It is spoken of Antichrist specially, and it is true in all Heretics. \+it August. tract. 29. in Evang. Io.\+it*\f* He that speaketh of himself, seeketh his own glory. But he that seeketh the glory of him that sent him, he is true, and injustice in him there is not.
\v 19 Did not Moyses give you the law, and none of you doeth the law?
\v 20 \x - \xt Io. 5, 18.\x* Why seek you to kill me? The multitude answered, and said, \f 2 \fr 7.20 \ft No marvel, when these speak thus to Christ himself, if Heretics call his vicar Antichrist.\f* Thou hast a devil, who seeketh to kill thee?
\v 21 \sc Jesus\sc* answered and said to them: One work I have done; and you do all marvel.
\v 22 Therefore \x - \xt Lev. 12, 3.\x* Moyses gave you circumcision: not that it is of Moyses, but \x - \xt Gen. 17, 10.\x* of the fathers, and in the Sabboth you circumcise a man.
\v 23 If a man receive circumcision in the Sabboth, that the law of Moyses be not broken; are you angry at me because I have healed a man wholly in the Sabboth?
\v 24 Judge not according to the face, but judge just judgement.
\v 25 Certain therefore of Jerusalem said: Is not this he whom they seek to kill?
\v 26 And behold, he speaketh openly, and they say nothing to him. Have the Princes known indeed that this is \sc Christ\sc*?
\v 27 But this man we know whence he is. But when \sc Christ\sc* cometh, no man knoweth whence he is.
\v 28 \sc Jesus\sc* therefore cried in the temple teaching and saying: Both me you do know, and whence I am you know. And of myself I am not come, but he is true that sent me, whom you know not.
\v 29 I know him, because I am of him, and he sent me.
\v 30 They sought therefore to apprehend him: and no man laid hands upon him, because his hour was not yet come.
\v 31 But of the multitude many believed in him, and said: \sc Christ\sc*, when he cometh shall he do more signs than these which this man doeth?
\v 32 The Pharisees heard the multitude murmuring these things touching him: and the \f 1 \fr 7.32 \fq Princes \ft \+it chief Priests\+it*\f* Princes and Pharisees sent Ministers to apprehend him.
\v 33 \f 1 \fr 7.33 \ft The Gospel upon Monday in Passion Week.\f* \sc Jesus\sc* therefore said to them: Yet a little time I am with you: and I go to him that sent me.
\v 34 \x - \xt Io. 13, 33.\x* You seek me, and shall not find: and where I am, you cannot come.
\v 35 The Jews therefore said among themselves, whither will this man go, that we shall not find him? will he go into the dispersion of the Gentiles, and teach the Gentiles?
\v 36 What is this saying that he hath said: You shall seek me, and shall not find; and where I am, you cannot come.
\v 37 And in the last, the \x - \xt Lev. 23, 36.\x* great day of the festivity \sc Jesus\sc* stood and cried, saying: If any man thirst, let him come to me, and drink.
\v 38 He that believeth in me, as the scripture saith, \it Out of his belly shall flow rivers of living water.\it* \x - \xt Ioel. 2, 28.\x*
\v 39 (And this he said \x - \xt * Act. 2, 1.\x* of the Spirit that they should receive which believed in him. \f 2 \fr 7.39 \ft This was fulfilled on Whitsunday Act. 2, & afterward always by imposition of hands in the Sacrament of Confirmation: visibly in the primitive Church, and invisibly to the end of the world.\f* For as yet the Spirit was not given: because \sc Jesus\sc* was not yet glorified.)
\v 40 Of that multitude therefore, when they had heard these words of his, some said: This is the Prophet indeed.
\v 41 Others said: This is \sc Christ\sc*. But certain said: Why, doth \sc Christ\sc* come from Galilee?
\v 42 Doth not the \x - \xt Ps. 131, 11. Mich. 5, 2.\x* scripture say: That of the seed of David, and from Bethlehem the town where David was, \sc Christ\sc* doth come?
\v 43 Therefore there arose dissension in the multitude for him.
\v 44 And certain of them would have apprehended him: but no man laid hands upon him.
\v 45 The ministers therefore came to the chief Priests and the Pharisees. And they said to them: Why have you not brought him?
\v 46 The Ministers answered: Never did there man so speak, as this man.
\v 47 The Pharisees therefore answered them: Why, are you also seduced?
\v 48 Hath any of the Princes believed in him, or of the Pharisees?
\v 49 but this multitude that knoweth not the law, are accursed.
\v 50 Nicodemus said to them, \x - \xt Io. 3, 2.\x* he \f 2 \fr 7.50 \ft Christ hath some good always even among the wicked, which secretly serve him and by wise delays avert the execution of unjust laws against him and his people, as Nicodemus & Gamaliel.\f* that came to him by night, who was one of them:
\v 51 Doth our law judge a man, unless it first hear him, and know what he doeth?
\v 52 They answered & said to him: Why, art thou also a Galilæan? Search, \f 1 \fr 7.52 \ft \+it Search the scriptures\+it*\f* & see that from Galilee a Prophet riseth not.
\v 53 And every man returned to his house.
\c 8
\cd Again in the Temple (absolving an adultress after his merciful manner, and yet withal declaring against his enemies that he is not a favourer of sin, no more than Moyses) 12. he teacheth openly, and is not for all that apprehended, telling them both of his Godhead, 21. and of their reprobation, 28. of his exaltation also by their Crucifying of him: 31. exhorting the believers to persevere: 33. and showing them that seek his death, that they are neither free, 39. nor of Abraham, 41. nor of God, 44. but of the Devil, 45. but that himself is of God, 52. and greater and ancienter than Abraham. 59. For the which they go about to stone him, but in vain.
\p
\v 1 \sc And \f 1 \fr 8.1 \ft The Gospel upon Saturday the 3. week of Lent.\f* Jesus\sc* went into the Mount-olivet:
\v 2 and early in the morning again he came into the temple, and the people came to him, and sitting he taught them.
\v 3 And the Scribes and Pharisees bring a woman taken in adultery; and they did set her in the midst,
\v 4 And said to him: Master, this woman was even now taken in adultery.
\v 5 And \x - \xt Lev. 20, 10.\x* in the law Moyses commanded us to stone such. What sayest thou therefore?
\v 6 And this they said tempting him, that they might accuse him. But \sc Jesus\sc* bowing himself down, with his finger wrote in the earth.
\v 7 When they therefore continued asking him, he lifted up himself, and said to them: \f 1 \fr 8.7 \ft We cannot conveniently reprehend or condemn other men's faults, if ourselves be guilty of the same or other greater. \+it Cyril in Io. See Annot. Mt. 7, 1.\+it*\f* He that is without sin of you, let him first throw the stone at her.
\v 8 And again bowing himself, he wrote in the earth.
\v 9 And they hearing, went out one by one, beginning at the seniors: and \sc Jesus\sc* alone remained, and the woman standing in the midst.
\v 10 And \sc Jesus\sc* lifting up himself, said to her: Woman, where are they that accused thee? Hath no man condemned thee?
\v 11 Who said: No man, Lord. And \sc Jesus\sc* said: \f 1 \fr 8.11 \ft S. Augustine by this example of our Master proveth that Clergy men specially should be given much to mercy: and that they ought often, as the cause and time require, to get pardon of the secular Magistrates for offenders that be penitent. \+it Ep. 54.\+it*\f* Neither will I condemn thee. Go, and now sin no more.
\v 12 \f 1 \fr 8.12 \ft b The Gospel upon Saturday the 4. Week in Lent.\f* Again therefore \sc Jesus\sc* spake to them, saying: I am the light of the world: he that followeth me, walketh not in darkness, but shall have the light of life.
\v 13 The Pharisees therefore said to him: Thou givest testimony of thyself; thy testimony is not true.
\v 14 \sc Jesus\sc* answered, and said to them: Although I do give testimony of myself, my testimony is true: because I know whence I came, & whither I go: but you know not whence I come, or whither I go.
\v 15 You judge according to the flesh: I do not judge any man.
\v 16 And if I do judge, my judgement is true: because I am not alone, but I and he that sent me, the Father.
\v 17 And in your law it is written, that \x - \xt Deu. 17, 6. 19, 15.\x* the testimony of two men is true.
\v 18 I am he that give testimony of myself: and he that sent me, the Father, giveth testimony of me.
\v 19 They said therefore to him: Where is thy Father? \sc Jesus\sc* answered, Neither me do you know, nor my Father. If you did know me, perhaps you might know my Father also.
\v 20 These words \sc Jesus\sc* spake in the Treasury, teaching in the temple, and no man apprehended him, because his hour was not yet come.
\v 21 \f 1 \fr 8.21 \ft The Gospel upon Monday the 2. week of Lent.\f* Again therefore \sc Jesus\sc* said to them: I go, and you shall seek me, and shall die in your sin. Whither I go, you cannot come.
\v 22 The Jews therefore said: Why, will he kill himself, because he saith: Whither I go, you cannot come?
\v 23 And he said to them: You are from beneath, I am from above. You are of this world, I am not of this world.
\v 24 Therefore I said to you that you shall die in your sins. For if you believe not that I am he, you shall die in your sin.
\v 25 They said therefore to him: Who art thou? \sc Jesus\sc* said to them: \f 1 \fr 8.25 \ft So read S. Cyril, S. Ambrose, & S. Augustine: expounding it of Christ's person, that he is the beginning or cause of all creatures.\f* The Beginning who also speak to you.
\v 26 Many things I have to speak and judge of you. But he that sent me, is true: and what I have heard of him, these things I speak in the world.
\v 27 And they knew not that he said to them that his Father was God.
\v 28 \sc Jesus\sc* therefore said to them: When you shall have exalted the Son of man, then you shall know that I am he, and of myself I do nothing, but as the Father hath taught me, these things I speak:
\v 29 and he that sent me, is with me: and he hath not left me alone, because the things that please him I do always.
\v 30 When he spake these things, many believed in him.
\v 31 \sc Jesus\sc* therefore said to them that believed him, the Jews: If you \f 1 \fr 8.31 \ft Only faith is not sufficient without perseverance or abiding in the keeping of his commandments.\f* abide in my word, you shall be my Disciples indeed.
\v 32 And you shall know the truth, and the truth shall make you free.
\v 33 They answered him: We are the seed of Abraham, and we never served any man: how sayest thou, You shall be free?
\v 34 \sc Jesus\sc* answered them: Amen, amen I say to you, that \x - \xt Ro. 6, 16. 2. Pet. 2, 19.\x* every one which committeth sin, is the servant of sin. \ef - \fr 8.34 \fq Amen, amen. \ft ¹ \+it What is it (saith S. Augustine upon this place) when our Lord saith,\+it* Amen, amen? \+it He doth much commend and urge the thing that he so uttereth, doubling it. It is a certain oath of his, if a man may so say. for Amen in Hebrew signifieth\+it* verum, a truth. \+it Yet is it not translated, Whereas it might have been said,\+it* verum, verum \+it dico vobis, but neither the\+it* ² \+it Greek interpreter durst do it, nor the Latin, the Hebrew word hath remained still, that so it might be the more esteemed. Tract. 41 in Joan. By which words and the like\+it* ³ \+it recorded in other places of this new Testament, the Reader may see great reason, why we also say,\+it* Amen, amen, \+it and durst not translate it and such like words into our English tongue\+it*. \fq ¹ \ft Why \+it Amen, amen\+it*, is not translated. \fq ² \ft See the preface, & Annot. in \+it Apoc. c. 19. 4.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it * Ἀμὴν, ἀμὴν\+it*.\ef*
\v 35 And the servant abideth not in the house for ever: the son abideth for ever.
\v 36 If therefore the Son make you free, you shall be \f 1 \fr 8.36 \ft Man was never without free will: but having the grace of Christ, his will is truly made free (as S. Augustine saith) from servitude of sin also. \+it tract. 41. in Evang. Io.\+it*\f* free indeed.
\v 37 I know that you are the children of Abraham: but you seek to kill me, because my word taketh not in you.
\v 38 I speak that which I have seen with my Father: and you do the things that you have seen with your father.
\v 39 They answered, and said to him: Our father is Abraham. \sc Jesus\sc* saith to them: If you be the children of Abraham, \f 1 \fr 8.39 \ft Not only faith but good works also make men the children of Abraham, according as S. James also speaketh of Abraham's works c. 2.\f* do the works of Abraham.
\v 40 But now, you seek to kill me, a man that have spoken the truth to you, which I have heard of God. This did not Abraham.
\v 41 You do the works of your father. They said therefore to him: We were not born of fornication. We have one Father, God.
\v 42 \sc Jesus\sc* therefore said to them: If God were your Father, verily you would love me. For from God I proceeded, and came: for I came not of myself, but he sent me:
\v 43 Why do you not know my speech? Because you cannot hear my word.
\v 44 You are of your father the devil, & the desires of your father you will do. He was a \f 1 \fr 8.44 \ft S. Augustine compareth Heretics in their spiritual murder by driving Christian men out of the Church, to the Devil that drove our parents out of Paradise. \+it Cont. lit. Petil. li. 2, c. 13.\+it*\f* man-killer from the beginning, & he stood not in the verity; because verity is not in him. When he speaketh a lie, he speaketh of his own, because he is a liar, & the father thereof.
\v 45 But because I say the verity, you believe me not.
\v 46 Which of you shall argue me of sin? If I say the verity, why do you not believe me?
\v 47 \f 1 \fr 8.47 \ft The Gospel upon PASSION Sunday.\f* He that is of God, heareth the words of God. Therefore you hear not, because you are not of God.
\v 48 The Jews therefore answered, and said to him: Do not we say well that thou art a Samaritane, and hast a devil?
\v 49 \sc Jesus\sc* answered: \f 1 \fr 8.49 \ft He denieth not that he is a Samaritane, because he is our keeper or protector, as the word signifieth, and because he is indeed that merciful Samaritane in the parable of the wounded man Luc. 10, 22. \+it Aug. tract. 43. in Ioan.\+it*\f* I have no devil: but I do honour my Father, and you have dishonoured me.
\v 50 but I seek not mine own glory. There is that seeketh and judgeth.
\v 51 Amen, amen I say to you: If any man keep my word, he shall not see death for ever.
\v 52 The Jews therefore said: Now we have known that thou hast a devil. Abraham is dead, and the Prophets; and thou sayest: If any man keep my word, he shall not taste death for ever.
\v 53 Why, art thou greater than our father Abraham, who is dead? and the Prophets are dead. Whom doest thou make thyself?
\v 54 \sc Jesus\sc* answered: If I do glorify myself, my glory is nothing. It is my Father that glorifieth me, whom you say that he is your God.
\v 55 And you have not known him, but I know him. And if I shall say that I know him not, I shall be like to you, a liar. But I do know him, and do keep his word.
\v 56 Abraham your father rejoiced that he might see my day: and he saw, and was glad.
\v 57 The Jews therefore said to him: Thou hast not yet fifty years and hast thou seen Abraham?
\v 58 \sc Jesus\sc* said to them: Amen, amen I say to you, before that Abraham was made, I am.
\v 59 They took stones therefore to cast at him. But \sc Jesus\sc* hid himself, and went out of the temple.
\c 9
\cd To shew that by his Baptism (being the Sacrament of illumination or faith) he will take away the blindness of the world, he giveth with strange ceremonies sight to one born blind. 8. By which wonderful miracle (the attestation of the party himself and of his parents concurring) first the neighbours, then also the Pharisees themselves are plainly confounded. Yet so obstinate they are, that because it was the Sabboth when he wrought it, they infer that he is not of God: yea and throw out of their Synagogue the party for confessing him. 15. But our Lord receiveth him: 39. and foretelleth by this occasion, \it the excecation of the Jews (because of their willful obstinacy) and illumination of the Gentiles who confess their own blindness.\it*
\p
\v 1 \sc And \f 1 \fr 9.1 \ft The Gospel upon Wednesday in the 4. week of Lent.\f* Jesus\sc* passing by, saw a man blind: from his nativity;
\v 2 and his Disciples asked him: Rabbi, who hath sinned, this man, or his parents, that he should be born blind? \sc Jesus\sc* answered:
\v 3 \f 1 \fr 9.3 \ft Though many infirmities fall for sin, yet not all: some coming for probation, and some sent that God by the cure thereof may be glorified.\f* Neither hath this man sinned, nor his parents; but that the works of God may be manifested in him.
\v 4 I must work the works of him that sent me, whiles it is day. The night cometh, \f 1 \fr 9.4 \ft The time of working, and meriting, is in this life: after death we can deserve no more by our deeds, but must only receive good or ill, according to the difference of works here.\f* when no man can work.
\v 5 As long as I am in the world, I am the light of the world.
\v 6 When he had said these things, he spit on the ground, and made clay of the spittle, and spread the clay upon his eyes, \ef - \fr 9.6 \fq Made clay. \ft Christ that could have cured this man by his only will or word, yet used certain creatures as his instruments in working, ¹ and diverse circumstances and ceremonies, clay, water, anointing, washing, &c. No marvel then that he and his Church use such diversities of Sacraments and ceremonies external in curing our souls. \fq ¹ \ft External ceremonies.\ef*
\v 7 and said to him: Go, \f 1 \fr 9.7 \ft c This was a figure of Baptism, to which all men born in sin and blindness are sent for health & sight. \+it Ambr. li. 3. c. 2. de Sacramentis.\+it*\f* wash in the pool of Siloe, which is interpreted, Sent. He went therefore, and washed; and he came seeing.
\v 8 Therefore the neighbours, and they which had seen him before, that he was a beggar, said: Is not this he that sat, and begged? Others said: That this is he.
\v 9 But others: No, not so, but he is like him. But he said: That I am he.
\v 10 They said therefore to him: How were thine eyes opened?
\v 11 He answered: That man that is called \sc Jesus\sc*, made clay, and anointed mine eyes, and said to me: Go to the pool of Siloe, and wash. And I went, and washed, and saw.
\v 12 And they said to him: Where is he? He saith: I know not.
\v 13 They bring him that had been blind, to the Pharisees.
\v 14 And it was the Sabboth when \sc Jesus\sc* made the clay, and opened his eyes.
\v 15 Again therefore the Pharisees asked him, how he saw. But he said to them: He put clay upon mine eyes, & I washed; and I see.
\v 16 Certain therefore of the Pharisees said: This man is not of God, that keepeth not the Sabboth. But others said: How can a man that is a sinner do these signs? And there was a schism among them.
\v 17 They say therefore to the blind again: Thou, what sayest thou of him that opened thine eyes? And he said: That he is a Prophet.
\v 18 The Jews therefore did not believe of him, that he had been blind and saw, until they called the parents of him that saw,
\v 19 and asked them, saying: Is this your son, whom you say that he was born blind? how then doth he now see?
\v 20 His parents answered them, and said: We know that this is our son, and that he was born blind;
\v 21 but how he now seeth, we know not, or who hath opened his eyes, we know not, ask himself; he is of age, let himself speak of himself.
\v 22 These things his parents said, because they feared the Jews. For the Jews had now conspired, that if any man should confess him to be \sc Christ\sc*, he should be put out of the Synagogue. \ef - \fr 9.22 \fq Put out of the Synagogue. \ft ¹ The Heretics untruly translate here (& v. 35.) \+it Excommunicate\+it*: to make the simple conceive the Church's Excommunication to be no other, or no better, or no more rightly used against them, than this casting out of the Synagogue of such as confessed our Saviour. They might as well have translated for Synagogue, ² Church: for the Old Testament, the new: for Law, grace: for flesh, spirit: for Moyses, Christ. For no less difference is there between, casting out of the Synagogue, and, Excommunication. Besides that, not every one which was not of the Jews' Synagogue, was therefore out of the communion of the Faithful, many true believers being in other parts of the world not subject to the Jews' Synagogue, Law, or Sacraments. ³ And therefore it was not all one to be out of the Synagogue, and to be excommunicated. as now, whosoever is out of the Church's communion, either by his own will, or for his just deserts thrust out of it by the spiritual Magistrate, he is quite abandoned out of all the society of Saints in heaven and earth, so long as he so continueth. \fq ¹ \ft Heretical translation. \fq ² \ft Casting out of the Synagogue. \fq ³ \ft The Church's Excommunication. See in the \+it Annot. Mat. 18, 17.\+it*\ef* \ef - \fr 9.22 \ft As for the cause of thrusting this poor man and such other out of the Synagogue, and excommunicating Heretics, there is as great odds as betwixt heaven and hell: he being used so for following Christ and his Church, these for forsaking Christ and his Church. Some more agreement there is between that corrupt sentence of the Jews against the followers of Christ, and the pretended excommunication executed against Catholic men by our Heretics, although in truth there is no great resemblance. For, the Jews though they abused their power sometimes, yet had they authority indeed by God's law so to punish contemners of their Law, & therefore it was feared and respected even of good men. But the excommunication used by heretics against Catholics or any offenders, is not to be respected at all, ¹ being no more but a ridiculous usurpation of the Church's right and fashion of the same. For, out of their Synagogues all faithful men ought to flee, and not tarry to be thrust out: according to the warning given against Core and Dathan. \+it Be ye separated from their tabernacles, lest you be wrapped in their sins\+it*. ² \fq ¹ \ft The Heretics' ridiculous Excommunication. \fq ² \ft \+it Num. 16.\+it*\ef*
\v 23 Therefore did his parents say: That he is of age, ask himself.
\v 24 They therefore again called the man that had been blind, and said to him: \f 1 \fr 9.24 \ft So say the Heretics when they derogate from miracles done by Saints or their Relics, pharisaically pretending the glory of God. As though it were not God's glory, when his Saints do it by his power and virtue: yea his greater glory, that doeth such things by his servants, & by the meanest things belonging to them, as Peter's shadow \+it Act. 5.\+it* & Paul's napkin \+it Act. 19.\+it*\f* Give glory to God. We know that this man is a sinner.
\v 25 He therefore said to them: Whether he be a sinner, I know not: one thing I know, that whereas I was blind, now I see.
\v 26 They said therefore to him: What did he to thee? how did he open thine eyes?
\v 27 He answered them: I have now told you, and you have heard; why will you hear it again? will you also become his Disciples?
\v 28 They reviled him therefore, & said: Be thou his Disciple: but we are the Disciples of Moyses.
\v 29 We know that to Moyses God did speak; but this man we know not whence he is.
\v 30 The man answered and said to them: For in this it is marvelous that you know not whence he is, and he hath opened mine eyes.
\v 31 And we know that sinners God doth not hear. But if a man be a server of God, and do the will of him, him he heareth.
\v 32 From the beginning of the world it hath not been heard that any man hath opened the eyes of one born blind.
\v 33 unless this man were of God, he could not do any thing.
\v 34 They answered and said to him: Thou wast wholly born in sins, and doest thou teach us? And they did cast him forth.
\v 35 \sc Jesus\sc* heard that they cast him forth; & when he had found him, he said to him: Doest thou believe in the Son of God?
\v 36 He answered, and said: Who is he Lord, that I may believe in him?
\v 37 And \sc Jesus\sc* said to him: Both thou hast seen him; and he that talketh with thee, he it is.
\v 38 But he said, I believe Lord. And falling down he adored him.
\v 39 And \sc Jesus\sc* said to him: For judgement came I into this world; \f 1 \fr 9.39 \ft By this we see that this miracle was not only marvelous and beneficial to the blind, but also significative of taking away spiritual blindness.\f* that they that see not, may see: and they that see, may become blind.
\v 40 And certain of the Pharisees that were with him, heard; and they said to him: Why, are we also blind?
\v 41 \sc Jesus\sc* said to them: If you were blind, you should not have sin, but now you say, That we see. Your sin remaineth.
\c 10
\cd He continueth his talk to the Pharisees, shewing that they and all other that will not enter in by him, are wolves: and that they which hear them, are not the true sheep. 11. But that himself is the good Pastor, and therefore to save the sheep from these wolves, he will yield his life, which otherwise no might of theirs could take from him: fortelling also his Resurrection, and vocation of the Gentiles. 22. Again another time, he telleth these Jews openly, that they are not of his sheep, and that no might of theirs shall take from him his true sheep, because he is God, even as his Father is God. 31. Which by his miracles and by Scripture he sheweth to be no blasphemy: and they in vain seeking to stone and to apprehend him, 40. he goeth out to the place where John Baptist had given open witness of him.
\p
\v 1 \sc Amen\sc*, \f 1 \fr 10.1 \ft The Gospel upon Tuesday in Whitsunweek.\f* amen I say to you, he that entereth not by the door into the fold of the sheep, but climbeth up another way; he is \f 2 \fr 10.1 \ft The thief, is the Heretic specially, & any other that unlawfully break in upon the sheep to kill & destroy them by false doctrine and otherwise.\f* a thief and a robber. \ef - \fr 10.1 \fq Climbeth another way. \ft Whosoever taketh upon him to preach without lawful sending, to minister Sacraments, and is not Canonically ordered of a true Catholic Bishop, to be a Curate of souls, Person, Bishop, or what other spiritual Pastor so ever, and cometh not in by lawful election and holy Church's ordinance to that dignity, but breaketh in against order by force or favour of men, and by human laws, he is a thief and a murderer. ¹ So came in Arius, Calvin, Luther, and all Heretics: and all that succeed them in room and doctrine. And generally every one that descendeth not by Lawful succession in the known ordinary line of Catholic Bishops and Pastors that have been in all Countries since their conversion. And according to this rule \+it S. Irenæus li. 3 c. 3\+it* trieth the true shepherds from the thieves and Heretics. So do \+it Tertul. de Præscr. nu. 11. S. Cypr. de unit. Ec. nu. 7. S. August. ep. 165. & cont. ep. Manich. c. 4.\+it* and \+it Lirinensis\+it*. \fq ¹ \ft Arch-heretics specially, are the thieves that climb in another way, not by the door.\ef*
\v 2 But he that entereth by the door, is the Pastor of the sheep.
\v 3 To this man the porter openeth; and the sheep hear his voice: and he calleth his own sheep by name, and leadeth them forth.
\v 4 And when he hath let forth his own sheep, he \f 1 \fr 10.4 \ft That is the fashion of Jewry & other countries, signifying that the shepherd or Pastor must teach the sheep, and not they him.\f* goeth before them: and the sheep follow him, because they know his voice.
\v 5 But a stranger they follow not, but fly from him because they know not the voice of strangers.
\v 6 This proverb \sc Jesus\sc* said to them. But they knew not what he spake to them.
\v 7 \sc Jesus\sc* therefore said to them again: Amen, amen I say to you, that I am the door of the sheep.
\v 8 And how many soever have come, are thieves and robbers: but the sheep heard them not.
\v 9 I am the door. By me if any enter, he shall be saved: & he shall go in and shall go out, & shall find pastures.
\v 10 The thief cometh not but to steal and kill & destroy. I came that they may have life, & may have more aboundantly.
\v 11 \f 1 \fr 10.11 \ft The Gospel upon the 2. Sunday after Easter, & for S. Thomas of Canterbury. \+it Decemb. 29.\+it* And in his Translation. \+it Iulij. 7.\+it*\f* I am the good Pastor. \ef - \fr 10.11 \fq Good Pastor. \ft The good Pastor, is he whose special care is not of his own advantage, but of the safety of the flock. The hireling, is he that respecteth not the profit and good of the flock, but his own lucre. The wolf, is the Heretic, or any persecutor of the Church, which is Christ's flock.\ef*
\v 12 \x - \xt Esa. 40, 11. Ezec. 34, 23.\x* The good Pastor giveth his life for his sheep. But the hireling & he that is not the Pastor, whose own the sheep are not, seeth the wolf coming, and leaveth the sheep, and flyeth: and the wolf raveneth, and disperseth the sheep.
\v 13 And the hireling flyeth because he is a hireling; and he hath no care of the sheep. \ef - \fr 10.13 \fq Fleeth. \ft Every Bishop and Pastor is bound to abide with his flock in times of danger and persecution even to death, ¹ except himself be personally sought for, rather than the flock, or the flock itself forsake him. For in such cases the Pastor may flee, as the Apostles did, and S. Athanasius, and others. \+it S. Athan. Apol. de sua fuga. August. ep. 180.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft When the Pastor must tarry, or may flee.\ef*
\v 14 I am the good Pastor; and I know mine, and mine know me.
\v 15 As the Father knoweth me, and I know the Father: and \f 1 \fr 10.15 \ft Christ's death was so necessary for the flock, that when he might have escaped, he voluntarily offered himself to death for his flock.\f* I yield my life for my sheep.
\v 16 And other sheep I have that are not of this fold: them also I must bring, and they shall hear my voice, and there shall be made \x - \xt Ezec. 37, 24.\x* one fold and one Pastor.
\v 17 \f 1 \fr 10.17 \ft c He meaneth the Church of the Gentiles.\f* Therefore the Father loveth me: because I yield my life, that I may take it again.
\v 18 No man taketh it away from me: but \x - \xt Esa. 53, 7.\x* I yield it of myself. And I have power to yield it: and I have power to take it again. This commandment I received of my Father.
\v 19 A dissension rose again among the Jews for these words.
\v 20 And many of them said: He hath a devil and is mad; why hear you him?
\v 21 Others said: These are not the words of one that hath a devil. Can a devil open the eyes of blind men?
\v 22 \x - \xt 1. Mach. 4, 56. 59.\x* And the Dedication was in Jerusalem: and it was winter. \ef - \fr 10.22 \fq The dedication. \ft ¹ This is the feast of Dedication instituted by Judas Machabæus \+it li. 1 Mach. c. 4.\+it* Christ vouchsafed to honour and keep that feast instituted by him: & our Heretics vouchsafe not to pray and sacrifice for the dead. ² used and approved by him. The Dedication also of Christian Churches is warranted thereby, with the annual memories thereof. And it proveth that such things may be instituted without any express commandment in Scripture. \fq ¹ \ft Judas Machabæus. Dedication of Churches. \fq ² \ft \+it * 2 Macha. 12.\+it*\ef*
\v 23 \f 1 \fr 10.23 \ft The Gospel upon Wednesday in Passion Week.\f* And \sc Jesus\sc* walked in the temple, in Salomon's porch.
\v 24 The Jews therefore compassed him round about, and said to him: How long doest thou hold our soul in suspense? if thou be \sc Christ\sc*, tell us openly.
\v 25 \sc Jesus\sc* answered them: I speak to you; and you believe not; the works that I do in the name of my Father, they give testimony of me.
\v 26 But you do not believe, because you are not of my sheep.
\v 27 My sheep hear my voice; and I know them, and they follow me.
\v 28 And I give them life everlasting; and they shall not perish for ever, and no man shall pluck them out of my hand.
\v 29 \f 1 \fr 10.29 \ft Another reading is, \+it My father that hath given me, &c.\+it*\f* My Father, that which he hath given me, is greater than all: and no man can pluck them out of the hand of my Father. \ef - \fr 10.29 \fq That which he gave me. \ft Thus read also diverse of the Fathers, namely \+it S. Hilar. Trin. li. 1 post medium. S. Ambr. de Sp. S. li. 3. c. 18. S. August. in Io. tract. 41. S. Cyril. li. 7. Io. c. 10.\+it* and use it to prove that Christ had his essence and nature of the Father. ¹ And therefore some Heretics of our time wickedly accuse the Councel of Lateran for falsifying this place and applying it to the same purpose. which they less can abide, for that it is against Calvin's Autotheism, ² holding that Christ took his person of the Father, but not his substance. \+it See the 2 Annot in 1 Io. v. 1.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Christ's essence & divine nature of the Father. \fq ² \ft Calvin's auto-theism.\ef*
\v 30 I and the Father are \f 1 \fr 10.30 \ft c unum.\f* one.
\v 31 The Jews took up stones, to stone him.
\v 32 \sc Jesus\sc* answered them: Many good works I have shewed you from my Father, for which of those works do you stone me?
\v 33 The Jews answered him: For a good work we stone thee not, but for blasphemy, and because thou being a man, makest thyself God.
\v 34 \sc Jesus\sc* answered them: Is it not written in your law, that \it I said, you are gods?\it* \x - \xt Ps. 81, 6.\x*
\v 35 If he called them gods, to whom the word of God was made, and the scripture cannot be broken;
\v 36 whom the Father hath sanctified and sent into the world, say you, That thou blasphemest, because I said I am the Son of God?
\v 37 If I do not the works of my Father, believe me not.
\v 38 But if I do, and if you will not believe me, believe the works: that you may know and believe that the Father is in me, and I in the Father.
\v 39 They sought therefore to apprehend him; and he went forth out of their hands.
\v 40 And he went again beyond Jordan into that place where John was baptizing first; and he tarried there.
\v 41 and many came to him, and they said: That John indeed did no sign. But all things whatsoever John said of this man, were true.
\v 42 And many believed in him.
\c 11
\cd He cometh once again into Jewry boldly (the time that he would be killed of them, being not yet come) and raiseth Lazarus four days buried. 47. At which miracle the blind malice of the rulers so increaseth, that in Councel they conclude to make him away, howbeit the high Priest prophesieth unawares, of the salvation of the world by his death. 54. He thereupon goeth again out of the way.
\p
\v 1 \sc And\sc* \f 1 \fr 11.1 \ft The Gospel upon Friday in the 4. week of Lent.\f* there was a certain sick man, Lazarus of Bethania, of the town of Mary & Martha her sister.
\v 2 (And Mary was she \x - \xt Lu. 7, 37. Mat, 26, 7. Mar. 14, 3. Io. 12, 3.\x* that anointed our Lord with ointment, & wiped his feet with her hair: whose brother Lazarus was sick.)
\v 3 His sisters therefore sent to him saying: Lord, behold, he whom thou lovest, is sick.
\v 4 And \sc Jesus\sc* hearing, said to them: This sickness is not to death, but for the glory of God: that the Son of God may be glorified by it.
\v 5 And \sc Jesus\sc* loved Martha, and her sister Mary, and Lazarus.
\v 6 As he heard therefore that he was sick, then he tarried in the same place two days:
\v 7 then after this he saith to his Disciples: Let us go into Jewry again.
\v 8 The Disciples say to him: Rabbi, now the Jews sought to stone thee; and goest thou thither again?
\v 9 \sc Jesus\sc* answered: Are there not twelve hours of the day? If a man walk in the day, he stumbleth not: because he seeth the light of this world:
\v 10 but if he walk in the night, he stumbleth, because the light is not in him.
\v 11 These things he said; and after this he saith to them: Lazarus our friend sleepeth; but I go that I may raise him from sleep.
\v 12 His Disciples therefore said: Lord, if he sleep, he shall be safe.
\v 13 But \sc Jesus\sc* spake of his death; & they thought that he spake \f 1 \fr 11.13 \ft \+it c de dormitione somni.\+it*\f* of the sleeping of sleep.
\v 14 Then therefore \sc Jesus\sc* said to them plainly: Lazarus is dead;
\v 15 and I am glad for your sake, that you may believe, because I was not there: but let us go to him.
\v 16 Thomas therefore, who is called Didymus, said to his condisciples: Let us also go, to die with him.
\v 17 \sc Jesus\sc* therefore came, and found him now having been four days in the grave.
\v 18 (And Bethania was nigh to Jerusalem about fifteen furlongs.)
\v 19 And many of the Jews were come to Martha and Mary, to comfort them concerning their brother.
\v 20 Martha therefore when she heard that \sc Jesus\sc* was come, went to meet him; but Mary sat at home.
\v 21 \f 1 \fr 11.21 \ft The Gospel in a Mass for the dead upon the day of the burial or deposition, also the 3. 7. and 30. day.\f* Martha therefore said to \sc Jesus\sc*: Lord if thou hadst been here, my brother had not died.
\v 22 But now also I know that what things soever thou shalt ask of God, God will give thee.
\v 23 \sc Jesus\sc* saith to her: Thy brother shall rise again.
\v 24 Martha saith to him: I know that he shall rise again in the resurrection, in the last day.
\v 25 \sc Jesus\sc* saith to her: I am the resurrection and the life; he that believeth in me, although he be dead, shall live.
\v 26 And every one that liveth, and believeth in me, shall not die for ever. Believest thou this?
\v 27 She saith to him: Yea Lord, I have believed that thou art \sc Christ\sc* the Son of God that art come into this world.
\v 28 And when she had said these things, she went, and called Mary her sister secretly, saying: The Master is come, & calleth thee.
\v 29 She, when she heard, riseth quickly & cometh to him.
\v 30 For \sc Jesus\sc* was not yet come into the town: but he was yet in that place where Martha had met him.
\v 31 The Jews therefore that were with her in the house and did comfort her, when they saw Mary that she rose quickly and went forth, followed her, saying: That she goeth to the grave, to weep there.
\v 32 Mary therefore when she was come where \sc Jesus\sc* was, seeing him, fell at his feet, and saith to him: Lord, if thou hadst been here, my brother had not died.
\v 33 \sc Jesus\sc* therefore when he saw her, weeping, and the Jews that were come with her, weeping, he groaned in spirit, and troubled himself,
\v 34 and said: Where have you laid him? They say to him: Lord, come and see.
\v 35 And \sc Jesus\sc* wept.
\v 36 The Jews therefore said: Behold how he loved him.
\v 37 But certain of them said: Could not he that \x - \xt Io. 9, 6.\x* opened the eyes of the blind man, make that this man should not die?
\v 38 \sc Jesus\sc* therefore again groaning in himself, cometh to the grave: and it was a cave; and a stone was laid over it.
\v 39 \sc Jesus\sc* saith: Take away the stone. Martha the sister of him that was dead, said to him: Lord, now he stinketh, for he is now of four days.
\v 40 \sc Jesus\sc* saith to her: Did not I say to thee, that if thou believe, thou shalt see the glory of God?
\v 41 They took therefore the stone away. And \sc Jesus\sc* lifting his eyes upward, said: Father, I give thee thanks that thou hast heard me.
\v 42 And I did know that thou doest always hear me, but for the people that standeth about, have I said it, that they may believe that thou hast sent me.
\v 43 When he had said these things, he cried with a loud voice: Lazarus, come forth.
\v 44 And forthwith he came forth that had been dead, bound feet and hands with winding bands, and his face was tied with a napkin. \sc Jesus\sc* said to them: \f 1 \fr 11.44 \ft S. Cyril \+it li. 7. c. ult. in Io.\+it* and S. August. \+it Tract. 49. in Io.\+it* apply this to the Apostles and Priest's authority of absolving sinners: affirming Christ to revive none from sin, but in the Church and by the Priest's ministry.\f* Loose him, and let him go.
\v 45 Many therefore of the Jews that were come to Mary & Martha, & had seen the things that \sc Jesus\sc* did, believed in him.
\v 46 And certain of them went to the Pharisees, & told them the things that \sc Jesus\sc* did.
\v 47 \f 1 \fr 11.47 \ft The Gospel upon Friday in Passion Week.\f* The chief Priests therefore & the Pharisees gathered a Council, & said: What do we, for this man doeth many signs.
\v 48 If we let him alone so, all will believe in him: & the Romans will come, & \f 1 \fr 11.48 \ft All men, but specially Nations must take heed, that whiles to save their temporal state, they forsake God, they lose not both as the Jews did. \+it Aug. tract. 49. in Io.\+it*\f* take away our place & nation.
\v 49 But one of them named Caiphas, being the high Priest of that year, said to them: You know nothing,
\v 50 neither do you consider: that it is expedient for us that one man die for the people, & the whole nation perish not.
\v 51 And this he said not of himself: but being the high Priest of that year, he prophesied that \sc Jesus\sc* should die for the nation. \ef - \fr 11.51 \fq Being the high Priest. \ft Marvel not that Christ preserveth his truth in the Church as well by the unworthy as the worthy Prelates thereof: ¹ the gifts of the Holy Ghost following their Order and office, as we see here in Caiphas, and not their merits or person. And if this man being many ways wicked, and in part an usurper, and the Law and Priesthood being to decline and to give place to Christ's new ordinance, had yet some assistance of God for utterance of truth which himself meant not, nor knew not: how much more may we be assured, that Christ will not leave Peter's Seat, ² whose faith he promised should never fail, though the persons which occupy the same, were as ill as the blasphemous and malicious mouths of Heretics do affirm? \fq ¹ \ft The privilege of the office & order, though in a wicked person. \fq ² \ft \+it Lu. 22, 32.\+it*\ef*
\v 52 And not only for the nation, but to gather into one the children of God that were dispersed.
\v 53 From that day therefore they devised to kill him.
\v 54 \sc Jesus\sc* therefore walked no more openly among the Jews, but he went into the country beside the desert unto a city that is called Ephrem, and there he abode with his Disciples.
\v 55 And the Pasch of the Jews was at hand: & many of the country went up to Jerusalem before the Pasch to sanctify themselves.
\v 56 They sought \sc Jesus\sc* therefore; and they communed one with another, standing in the temple: What think you, in that he is not come to the festival day? And the chief Priests & Pharisees had given commandment, that if any man should know where he was, he should tell, that they might apprehend him.
\c 12
\cd The Rulers dealing as if he hid himself, 1. he cometh to Bethania. 3. Where by occasion of Judas the thief murmuring at Mary Magdalen's costly devotion, he foretelleth his death. 12. From thence, though they did now intend to kill Lazarus also, he rideth openly into Hierusalem, the people (because he had raised Lazarus) confessing with their acclamations that he is Christ. 20. Where certain Gentiles desiring to see him, 23. he foretelleth the conversion of the whole world from the Devil to him, to be now instant, as the effect of his death upon the Cross. 28. The Father also answering from heaven of his prayer made to that purpose, 37. yet after all this, the Jews continue incredulous as Esay prophesied of them: 42. though many believed, but were ashamed to confess him. 44. Whereupon he sheweth that it is glorious before God, and salvation to themselves, to believe in him, and confess him: and damnable, to despise him. \f - \ft The 4. part. THE 4 Pasch, & Holy Week of his Passion in Hierusalem.\f*
\p
\v 1 \sc Jesus\sc* \x - \xt Mt. 26, 6. Mar. 14, 3.\x*\f 2 \fr 12.1 \ft The Gospel upon Monday in Holy Week.\f* therefore six days before the Pasch came to Bethania, where Lazarus was, that had been dead, whom \sc Jesus\sc* raised.
\v 2 \f 1 \fr 12.2 \ft PALM SUNDAY eve.\f* And they made him a supper there: and Martha ministered, but Lazarus was one of them that sat at the table with him.
\v 3 \f 1 \fr 12.3 \ft Of this woman's extraordinary offices of devotion, & how acceptable they were to Christ, see the Annot. Mt. 26.\f* Mary therefore took a pound of ointment of right spikenard, precious, and anointed the feet of \sc Jesus\sc*, and wiped his feet with her hair: and the house was filled of the odour of the ointment.
\v 4 One therefore of his Disciples, Judas Iscariote, he that was to betray him, said:
\v 5 Why was not this ointment sold for three-hundred pence, and given to the poor? \ef - \fr 12.5 \fq Why was. \ft So wicked, covetous, and sacrilegious persons reprehend good men for bestowing their goods upon Church ornaments &c. ¹ under pretence of better bestowing them on the poor. Such provide for the poor as Judas did. \fq ¹ \ft Church ornaments.\ef*
\v 6 And he said this, not because he cared for the poor; but because he was a thief, and having the purse, carried the things that were put in. \ef - \fr 12.6 \fq A thief. \ft Judas did not then first perish when he sold our Lord, for he was a thief before: and being lost he yet followed Christ, not in heart, but in body only. Which our Master tolerated, ¹ to give us a lesson to tolerate the ill, rather than divide the body. \+it Aug. tract. 50 in Io.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Toleration of evil.\ef*
\v 7 \sc Jesus\sc* therefore said: Let her alone that she may keep it for the day of my \f 1 \fr 12.7 \ft The devout offices of balming and anointing the dead\f* burial.
\v 8 For the poor you have always with you; but \f 1 \fr 12.8 \ft Not in visible and mortal condition, to receive alms of you or such like offices for supply of my necessities.\f* me you shall not have always.
\v 9 A great multitude therefore of the Jews knew that he was there; and they came, not for \sc Jesus\sc* only, but that they might see Lazarus, whom he raised from the dead.
\v 10 But the chief Priests devised for to kill Lazarus also:
\v 11 \f 1 \fr 12.11 \ft The Gospel upon Saturday in Passion Week.\f* because many for him of the Jews went away, and believed in \sc Jesus\sc*.
\v 12 And on the morrow a great multitude that was come to the festival day, \f 1 \fr 12.12 \ft PALM SUNDAY.\f* when they had heard that \sc Jesus\sc* cometh to Jerusalem,
\v 13 they took the \x - \xt * Mt. 21, 7. Mr. 11, 7. Lu. 19, 35.\x* boughs of palms, and went forth to meet him, & cried: \it Hosanna, Blessed is he that cometh in the name of our Lord, the King of Israel.\it* \x - \xt Ps. 117, 26.\x*
\v 14 And \sc Jesus\sc* found a young ass, and sat upon it, as it is written:
\v 15 \it Fear not daughter of Sion: behold, thy King cometh sitting upon an ass's colt.\it* \x - \xt Zach. 9, 9.\x*
\v 16 These things his Disciples did not know at the first: but when \sc Jesus\sc* was glorified, then they remembered that these things had been written of him, and these things they did to him.
\v 17 The multitude therefore gave testimony, which was with him when he called Lazarus out of the grave, and raised him from the dead.
\v 18 For therefore also the multitude came to meet him, because they heard that he had done this sign.
\v 19 The Pharisees therefore said among themselves: Do you see that we prevail nothing? Behold the whole world is gone after him.
\v 20 And there were certain Gentiles of them that \f 1 \fr 12.20 \ft We may see there is a great difference where a man pray or adore, at home or in the Church & holy places: when the Gentiles also came of devotion a pilgrimage to the Temple in Hierusalem.\f* came up to adore in the festival day.
\v 21 These therefore came to Philippe who was of Bethsaida of Galilee, and desired him, saying: Sir, we are desirous to see \sc Jesus\sc*.
\v 22 Philippe cometh and telleth Andrew. Again Andrew & Philippe told \sc Jesus\sc*.
\v 23 But \sc Jesus\sc* answered them, saying: The hour is come, that the Son of man shall be glorified.
\v 24 \f 1 \fr 12.24 \ft b The Gospel for S. Ignatius \+it Febr. 1.\+it* And S. Laurence \+it Aug. 10.\+it* And for some other martyrs not Bishops.\f* Amen, amen I say to you, unless the grain of wheat falling into the ground, die: itself remaineth alone. but if it die, it bringeth much fruit.
\v 25 He that loveth his life shall lose it: and he that hateth his life in this world, doth keep it to life everlasting.
\v 26 If any man minister to me, let him follow me: and where I am, there also shall my minister be. If any man minister to me, my Father will honour him.
\v 27 Now my soul is troubled. And what shall I say? Father, save me from this hour. But therefore came I into this hour.
\v 28 Father, glorify thy name. A voice therefore came from Heaven: Both I have glorified it, and again I will glorify it.
\v 29 The multitude therefore that stood and had heard, said that it thundered. Others said, An Angel spake to him.
\v 30 \sc Jesus\sc* answered, and said: This voice came not for me, but for your sake.
\v 31 \f 1 \fr 12.31 \ft The Gospel upon Holy Rood day \+it Septemb. 14.\+it* in Latin, \+it Exaltatio S. Crucis.\+it*\f* Now is the judgement of the world: now the Prince of this world shall be cast forth.
\v 32 And I, \x - \xt Io. 3, 14.\x* if I be exalted from the earth, will draw all things to myself.
\v 33 (And this he said, signifying what death he should die.)
\v 34 The multitude answered him: We have heard out of the law, that \sc Christ\sc* abideth for ever; and how sayest thou: The Son of man must be exalted? Who is this Son of man?
\v 35 \sc Jesus\sc* therefore said to them: Yet a little while, the light is among you. Walk whiles you have the light, that the darkness overtake you not. And he that walketh in darkness, knoweth not whither he goeth.
\v 36 Whiles you have the light, believe in the light, that you may be the children of light. These things \sc Jesus\sc* spake and he went away, and hid himself from them.
\v 37 And whereas he had done so many signs before them, they believed not in him:
\v 38 that the saying of Esay the Prophet might be fulfilled, which he said: \it Lord, who hath believed the hearing of us? and the arm of our Lord to whom hath it been revealed?\it* \x - \xt Esa. 53, 1.\x*
\v 39 Therefore they \f 1 \fr 12.39 \ft If any man ask (saith S. Augustine) why they could not believe: I answer roundly, because they would not. \+it Tract. 33. in Io.\+it* See the meaning of this speech Annot. \+it Mat. 13. 15. Mar. 4, 12.\+it*\f* could not believe, because Esay said again:
\v 40 \it He hath blinded their eyes, and indurated their heart: that they may not see with their eyes, nor understand with their heart, and be converted, and I heal them.\it* \x - \xt Esa. 6, 9. Mt. 13, 14. Mr. 4, 12. Lu. 8, 10. Act. 28, 27.\x*
\v 41 These things said Esaie, when he saw his glory, & spake of him.
\v 42 But yet of the Princes also many believed in him: but \f 1 \fr 12.42 \ft This is the case of many principal men in such countries where heresy hath the upper-hand. Who know and believe the Catholic faith: but making choice rather to keep man's favour than God's, they dare not confess the same. Such may pray that God and the world agree together: for else it is seen whose part they will take.\f* for the Pharisees they did not confess, that they might not be cast out of the Synagogue.
\v 43 For they loved the glory of men more, than the glory of God.
\v 44 But \sc Jesus\sc* cried, and said: He that believeth in me, doth not believe in me, but in him that sent me.
\v 45 And he that seeth me, seeth him that sent me.
\v 46 I a light am come into this world: that every one which believeth in me, may not remain in the darkness.
\v 47 And if any man hear my words, and keep them not: I do not judge him. For I came not to judge the world, but to save the world.
\v 48 He that despiseth me, & receiveth not my words, hath that judgeth him. the word that I have spoken, that shall judge him in the last day.
\v 49 Because of myself I have not spoken, but the Father that sent me, he gave me commandment what I should say, and what I should speak.
\v 50 And I know that his commandment is life everlasting. The things therefore that I speak: as the Father said to me, so do I speak.
\c 13
\cd At his last supper, to give his farewell, and that in most wonderful loving manner, 4. he washeth his Disciples' feet, 6. beginning with Peter, 8. (shewing how necessary it is for us to be washed by him in Baptism, and needful also after Baptism) 12. and by this example teaching them all humility one toward another. 21. Then he foretelleth, that (notwithstanding his exceeding love toward them) one even of them will betray him, meaning Judas, 22. as to John he secretly sheweth. After whose going out, he rejoiceth and saith that even now the hour is come, 34. commendeth unto them to love together, as a new commandment, 36. and foretelleth Peter who presumed too much of his own strength, that \it even this night he will deny him thrice.\it*
\p
\v 1 \sc And\sc* \x - \xt Mt. 26, 1. Mr. 14, 1. Luc. 22, 1.\x*\f 2 \fr 13.1 \ft The Gospel upon Maundy Thursday at Mass, & at the washing of feet.\f* before the festival day of Pasch, \sc Jesus\sc* knowing that his hour was come that he should pass out of this world to his Father: whereas he had loved his that were in the world, unto the end he loved them. \f 3 \fr 13.1 \ft MAUNDY THURSDAY.\f*
\v 2 And when \f 1 \fr 13.2 \ft By supper, he meaneth the eating of the Paschal lamb. For, the institution of the B. Sacrament was after this.\f* supper was done, whereas the devil now had put into the heart of Judas Iscariote the son of Simon, to betray him:
\v 3 knowing that the Father gave him all things into his hands, & that he came from God, and goeth to God:
\v 4 he riseth from supper, and layeth aside his garments, and having taken a towel, girded himself.
\v 5 After that, he put water into a basin, and began to wash the feet of the Disciples, and to wipe them with the towel where with he was girded. \ef - \fr 13.5 \fq Began to wash. \ft This lotion was not only so courtesy, such as the Jews used toward their guests, nor only for example of humility: but for mystery and signification of the great purity that is required before we come to receive the holy Sacrament, ¹ which straight after this washing was to be instituted and given to the Apostles. \+it Ambr. li. 3 de Sacra. c. 1 Bernard. de coena Domini Serm. 1.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Purity required to the receiving of the B. Sacrament.\ef*
\v 6 He cometh therefore to Simon Peter. And Peter saith to him; Lord: doest thou wash my feet?
\v 7 \sc Jesus\sc* answered and said to him: That which I do, thou knowest not now, hereafter thou shalt know.
\v 8 Peter saith to him: Thou shalt not wash my feet for ever. \sc Jesus\sc* answered him: If I wash thee not, thou shalt not have part with me.
\v 9 Simon Peter saith to him: Lord, not only my feet, but also hands, and head.
\v 10 \sc Jesus\sc* saith to him: He that is washed, needeth not but to wash his feet, but is clean wholly. And you are clean, but not all. \ef - \fr 13.10 \fq To wash his feet. \ft The foulness of the feet, when all the rest is clean, signifieth the earthly affections and relics of former sins remitted: ¹ which are to be cleansed by devout acts of charity & humility, as \+it S. Ambrose li. 2 de Sacr. c. 1.\+it* and \+it S. Augustine ep. 108. & tract. 56 in Io.\+it* do note. And because this was only a ceremony, & yet had such force, both now and afterward used of the Apostles, that it purged smaller offences and filthiness of the soul, as \+it S. Ambrose\+it* and \+it S. Bernard\+it* gather, ² it may not seem strange that holy water and such ceremonies may remit venial sins. \fq ¹ \ft Venial sins taken away by sacred ceremonies. \fq ² \ft \+it Ambr. & Bern. locis citatis.\+it*\ef*
\v 11 For he knew who he was that would betray him. therefore he said: You are not clean all.
\v 12 Therefore, after he had washed their feet, and taken his garments, being set down, again he said to them: Know you what I have done to you?
\v 13 You call me, Master, and Lord: and you say well, for I am so.
\v 14 If then I have washed your feet, Lord and Master; you also ought to wash one another's feet. \ef - \fr 13.14 \fq You also ought. \ft Our Master never spake plainer, nor seemed to command more precisely, either of Baptism or the Eucharist or any other Sacrament: and yet by the Church's judgement directed by the Holy Ghost, we know this to be no Sacrament nor necessary ceremony, and the other to be. And why do they believe the Church in this, ¹ and do not credit her affirming the chalice not to be necessary for the communicants? \fq ¹ \ft The Church defineth which are Sacraments and which not &c.\ef*
\v 15 For I have given you an example, that as I have done to you, so you do also.
\v 16 Amen, amen I say to you, a servant is not greater than his Lord, neither is an Apostle greater than he that sent him.
\v 17 If you know these things, you shall be blessed if you do them.
\v 18 I speak not of you all: I know whom I have chosen. But that the scripture may be fulfilled, \it He that eateth bread with me shall lift up his heel against me.\it* \x - \xt Ps. 40, 10.\x*
\v 19 From this time I tell you, before it come to pass: that when it shall come to pass, you may believe, that I am he.
\v 20 Amen, amen, I say to you, he that receiveth any that I send, receiveth me: & he that receiveth me, receiveth him that sent me.
\v 21 When \sc Jesus\sc* had said these things, he was troubled in spirit: and he protested, and said: \x - \xt Mat. 26, 18. Mar. 14, 16. Luc. 22, 21.\x* Amen, amen I say to you that one of you shall betray me.
\v 22 The Disciples therefore looked one upon another, doubting of whom he spake.
\v 23 There was therefore one of his Disciples leaning in the bosom of \sc Jesus\sc*, he whom \sc Jesus\sc* loved.
\v 24 Therefore Simon Peter beckoneth to him, and said to him: Who is it of whom he speaketh?
\v 25 He therefore leaning upon the breast of \sc Jesus\sc* saith to him: Lord, who is he?
\v 26 \sc Jesus\sc* answered: He it is to whom I shall reach the dipped bread. And when he had dipped the bread, he gave it to Judas Iscariote Simon's son.
\v 27 And after the morsel, then Satan entered into him. And \sc Jesus\sc* saith to him: That which thou doest, do it quickly.
\v 28 But no man knew of those that sat at table to what purpose he said this unto him.
\v 29 For certain thought, because Judas had the \f 1 \fr 13.29 \ft Christ had some provision before hand given him by the Collections of the faithful, which was used both in his own necessities, & bestowed upon the poor.\f* purse, that \sc Jesus\sc* had said to him: Buy those things which are needful for us to the festival day: or that he should give something to the poor.
\v 30 He therefore having received the morsel, incontinent went forth. And it was night.
\v 31 When he therefore was gone forth, \sc Jesus\sc* said: Now the Son of man is glorified, and God is glorified in him.
\v 32 If God be glorified in him, God also will glorify him in himself, and incontinent will he glorify him.
\v 33 Little children, yet a little while I am with you. You shall seek me, & \x - \xt Io. 7, 34.\x* as I said to the Jews, whither I go, you cannot come: to you also I say now.
\v 34 \x - \xt 1. Io. 3, 23.\x* A new commandment I give to you, That you love one another; as I have loved you, that you also love one another. \ef - \fr 13.34 \fq A new commandment. \ft The commandment of mutual love was given before, but manifoldly misconstrued, and abridged by the Jews to friends only, to this life only, for earthly respects only: but Christ reneweth it and enlargeth it after the form of his own love toward us, and giveth grace to fulfil it.\ef*
\v 35 In this all men shall know that you are my Disciples, if you have love one to another.
\v 36 Simon Peter saith to him: Lord, whither goest thou? \sc Jesus\sc* answered, Whither I go, thou canst not now follow me, but hereafter thou shalt follow.
\v 37 Peter saith to him: Why cannot I follow thee now? \x - \xt Mt. 26, 35. Mr. 14, 29. Lu. 22, 33.\x* I will yield my life for thee.
\v 38 \sc Jesus\sc* answered him: Thy life wilt thou yield for me? Amen, amen I say to thee, the cock shall not crow, until thou deny me thrice.
\c 14
\cd They being sad, because he said that he must go from them, he comforteth them many ways, as, putting them in hope to follow him unto the same place, so that they keep his commandments. Where he telleth them, that himself is the way thither according to his Humanity, and also the end according to his Divinity, no less than his Father, because he is consubstantial. 15. promising also to send unto them (that is, to his Church) the Holy Ghost to be after his departure with them for ever. 28. And saying that it is his promotion (according to his Humanity) to go to the Father, for, whose obedience this his death shall be, & not for any gilt of his own.
\p
\v 1 \sc Let\sc* \f 1 \fr 14.1 \ft The Gospel upon SS. Philip and Jacob's day \+it Maii. 1.\+it*\f* not your heart be troubled. You believe in God, believe in me also.
\v 2 In my Father's house there be \f 1 \fr 14.2 \ft These mansions signify differences of glory in heaven. \+it Hiero. li. 2. adv. Iovin.\+it*\f* many mansions. If not, I would have told you, Because I go to prepare you a place.
\v 3 And if I go, and prepare you a place: I come again and will take you to myself, that where I am, you also may be.
\v 4 And whither I go you know, and the way you know.
\v 5 Thomas saith to him: Lord, we know not whither thou goest; and how can we know the way?
\v 6 \sc Jesus\sc* saith to him: I am the way, and the verity, and the life. No man cometh to the Father; but by me.
\v 7 If you had known me, my Father also certes you had known: and from hence forth you \f 1 \fr 14.7 \fq shall know \ft \+it do know\+it*\f* shall know him, and you have seen him.
\v 8 Philippe saith to him: Lord shew us the Father, and it sufficeth us.
\v 9 \sc Jesus\sc* saith to him: So long time I am with you; & have you not known me? Philippe, he that seeth me, seeth the Father also. How sayest thou, Shew us the Father?
\v 10 \f 1 \fr 14.10 \ft \+it do ye not believe.\+it*\f* Doest thou not believe that I am in the Father, & the Father in me? The words that I speak to you, of myself I speak not. But my Father that abideth in me, he doeth the works.
\v 11 Believe you not, that I am in the Father and the Father in me? Otherwise for the works themselves believe.
\v 12 Amen, amen I say to you, he that believeth in me, the works that I do, he also shall do, and greater than these shall he do, \ef - \fr 14.12 \fq Greater than these. \ft ¹ S. Chrysostom ² in a whole book against the Pagans proveth that this was fulfilled not only in Peter's shadow, and Paul's garments, which as we read in the ³ Acts, healed infirmities: but also by the Relics and monuments of Saints, namely of S. Babylas, of whom he there treateth: thereby inferring that Christ is God, who could and did perform these wonderful words, by the very ashes of his servants. The Protestants clean contrary, as patrons of the Pagan's infidelity, as though our Saviour had promised these & the like miraculous works in vain, either not meaning or not able to fulfil them, so do they discredit all the approved histories of the Church concerning miracles wrought by Saints, namely that \+it S. Gregory Thaumaturgus\+it* ⁴ removed a mountain, the miracle of \+it S. Paul\+it* the eremite and \+it S. Hilarion\+it* written by \+it S. Hierom\+it*, the miracles of \+it S. Martin\+it* written by \+it Severus Sulpitius\+it*, the miracles testified by \+it S. Augustine de Civit. Dei\+it*, the miracles approved by \+it S. Gregory\+it* in his Dialogs, the miracles reported by \+it S. Bede\+it* in his Ecclesiastical story and lives of Saints: and all other miracles never so faithfully recorded in Ecclesiastical writers. ⁵ In all which things above their reach of reason and nature, they are as little persuaded and have no more faith than had the Pagans, against whom \+it S. Chrysostom\+it* in the foresaid book, and \+it S. Augustine de Civ. Dei li. 22 c. 1.\+it* and other Fathers heretofore have written. No man therefore needeth to marvel that the very Image of our Lady, & the like, do miracles, even as Peter's shadow did: nor wonder, if such things seem stranger and greater than those which Christ himself did: whereas our Saviour to put us out of doubt, saith expressly, that his Saints shall do greater things than himself did. \fq ¹ \ft We may and must easily believe the miracles of Saints and of their relics, when Christ himself foretelleth they shall do such wonderful things. \fq ² \ft \+it li. de Babyla Mart. to. 5.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it * Act. 5, 15. 19, 12.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft \+it * See Anno. Mat. 17, 20.\+it* \fq ⁵ \ft The Heretics as faithless in this point, as the old Pagans.\ef*
\v 13 because I go to the Father, & whatsoever you shall ask in my name, that will I do: that the Father may be glorified in the Son.
\v 14 \f 1 \fr 14.14 \ft The Gospel in a votive Mass for the election of the Pope, & upon Whitsuneve.\f* If you ask me any thing in my name, that will I do.
\v 15 If you \f 1 \fr 14.15 \ft It is then possible both to love Christ, and to keep his commandments.\f* love me, keep my commandments.
\v 16 And I will ask the Father, and he will give you another \f 1 \fr 14.16 \ft c \+it Paraclete\+it* by interpretation is either a comforter, or an advocate: and therefore to translate it by any one of them only, is perhaps to abridge the sense of this place.\f* Paraclete, that he may abide with you for ever, \ef - \fr 14.16 \fq For ever. \ft ¹ If the Holy Ghost had been promised only to the Apostles, their successors and the Church after them could not have challenged it, but it was promised them fore ever. Whereby we may learn, both that the privileges and promises made to the Apostles were not personal, but pertaining to their offices perpetually: and also that the Church and Pastors in all ages had and have the same Holy Ghost to govern them, that the Apostles and primitive Church had. \fq ¹ \ft The HOLY GHOST is promised to the CHURCH for ever.\ef*
\v 17 the Spirit of truth, whom the world cannot receive, because it seeth him not, neither knoweth him, but you \f 1 \fr 14.17 \fq know \ft \+it shall know\+it*\f* know him: because he shall abide with you, and shall be in you. \ef - \fr 14.17 \fq The spirit of truth. \ft They had many particular gifts and graces of the Holy Ghost before, and many virtues by the same, as all holy men have at all times: but the Holy Ghost here promised to the Apostles and their successors for ever, is to this use specially promised, ¹ to direct them in all truth and verity: and is contrary to the spirit of error, heresy, and falsehood. And therefore the Church cannot fall to Apostasy or Heresy, or to nothing, as the Adversaries say. \fq ¹ \ft The spirit of truth shall assist the CHURCH always.\ef*
\v 18 I will not leave you orphans: I will come to you.
\v 19 Yet a little while: and the world seeth me no more. But you see me: because I live, and you shall live.
\v 20 In that day you shall know that I am in my Father, and you in me; and I in you.
\v 21 He that hath my commandments, & keepeth them: he it is that loveth me. And he that loveth me, shall be loved of my Father: and I will love him, and will manifest myself to him.
\v 22 Judas saith to him, not that Iscariote: Lord, what is done, that thou wilt manifest thyself to us, and not to the world?
\v 23 \f 1 \fr 14.23 \ft The Gospel upon Whitsunday. And in a votive Mass of the Holy Ghost.\f* \sc Jesus\sc* answered, and said to him: If any love me, he will keep my word, and my Father will love him, and we will come to him, and will make abode with him.
\v 24 He that loveth me not, keepeth not my words. And the word which you have heard, is not mine; but his that sent me, the Father's.
\v 25 These things have I spoken to you abiding with you.
\v 26 But the Paraclete the Holy Ghost, whom the Father will send in my name, he shall \f 1 \fr 14.26 \ft See the Annot. upon the 16. Chap. vers. 12. & 13.\f* teach you all things, & suggest unto you all things whatsoever I shall say to you.
\v 27 Peace I leave to you, my peace I give to you; not as the world giveth, do I give to you. Let not your heart be troubled, nor fear.
\v 28 You have heard that I said to you, I go, and I come to you. If you loved me, you would be glad verily, that I go to the Father: because the Father is greater than I. \ef - \fr 14.28 \fq Father greater than I. \ft There is no place of Scripture that seemeth any thing so much to make for the Sacramentaries, as this and other in outward shew of words seemed to make for the Arians, ¹ who denied the equality of the Son with the Father. Which words yet indeed rightly understood after the Church's sense, make nothing for their false sect, but only signify that Christ according to his Manhood was inferior indeed, and that according to his Divinity he came of the Father. And if the Heresy or disease or this time were Arianism, we should stand upon these places and the like against the Arians, as we now do upon others against the Protestants, whose sect is the disease and bane of this time. \fq ¹ \ft The Arians allege as plain Scriptures as the Protestants.\ef*
\v 29 And now I have told you before it come to pass: that when it shall come to pass, you may believe.
\v 30 Now I will not speak many things with you. For the Prince of this world cometh, and in me he hath not any thing.
\v 31 But that the world may know that I love the Father: and as the Father hath given me commandment, so do I: Arise, let us go hence.
\c 15
\cd He exhorteth them to abide in him (that is, his Church, being the true vine, and not the Synagogue of the Jews any more) 9. and in his love, loving one another, and keeping his commandments: 13. shewing how much he accounteth of them, by this that he dieth for them, 15. and revealeth unto them the secrets of heaven, 17. and appointeth their fruit to be perpetual: 18. confirming them also against the persecutions and hatred of the obstinate Jews.
\p
\v 1 I \f 1 \fr 15.1 \ft The Gospel for some Martyrs, between Easter & Whitsunday. And on SS. Simon and Jude's eve. Oct. 27.\f*\f 2 \fr 15.1 \ft The Gospel upon S. George's day: April. 23. And for some other Martyrs between Easter & Whitsunday.\f* AM the true vine; and my Father is the husband-man.
\v 2 Every \f 1 \fr 15.2 \ft Christ hath some branches in his body mystical that be fruitless. Therefore ill livers also may be members of Christ & the Church.\f* branch in me, not bearing fruit, he will take it away: & every one that beareth fruit, he will purge it, \f 2 \fr 15.2 \ft Man may continually increase in justice and sanctification, so long as he liveth.\f* that it may bring more fruit. \ef - \fr 15.2 \fq Every branch in me. \ft By this it appeareth that Christ hath some branches in his body mystical that be fruitless: therefore ill livers also may be members of Christ and the Church, though none can be saved but fruitful branches.\ef* \ef - \fr 15.2 \fq More fruit. \ft This proveth that a just man may continually increase in justice & sanctification so long as he liveth.\ef*
\v 3 Now you are clean for the \f 1 \fr 15.3 \ft S. Augustine expoundeth it of the Sacramental word of Baptism, and not as Heretics do, of preaching only. \+it Tract. 80. in Io.\+it*\f* word which I have spoken to you.
\v 4 Abide in me: and I in you. As the branch cannot bear fruit of itself, unless it abide in the vine, so you neither unless you abide in me. \ef - \fr 15.4 \fq Unless it abide. \ft ¹ Whosoever by Heresy or Schism or for any other cause is cut off or separated from the Church, he can do no meritorious work to Salvation. \fq ¹ \ft No salvation out of the Church.\ef* \ef - \fr 15.4 \fq Unless you abide. \ft ¹ These conditional speeches, \+it if you remain in the vine, if you keep my commandments\+it*, and such like, give us to wit that we be not sure to persist or persevere, nor to be saved, but under conditions to be fulfilled by us. \+it Aug. de corrept. & gra. c. 13.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft No man sure of perseverance.\ef*
\v 5 I am the vine: you the branches. He that abideth in me, and I in him, the same beareth much fruit: for without me you can do nothing.
\v 6 If any abide not in me: he shall be cast forth as the branch, and shall wither, and they shall gather him up, and cast him into the fire, and he burneth.
\v 7 If you \f 1 \fr 15.7 \ft If a Schismatic pray never so much, he is not heard, because he remaineth not in the body of Christ.\f* abide in me, & my words abide in you: you shall ask what thing soever you will, and it shall be done to you.
\v 8 In this my Father is glorified; that you bring very much fruit, and become my Disciples.
\v 9 As my Father hath loved me, I also have loved you. Abide in my love.
\v 10 If you keep my precepts, you shall abide in my love: as I also have kept my Father's precepts, and do abide in his love. \ef - \fr 15.10 \fq Keep my precepts. \ft ¹ This careful and often admonition of keeping his commandments, proveth that a Christian man's life is not only or principally in faith, but in good works. \fq ¹ \ft Only faith sufficeth not to salvation.\ef*
\v 11 These things I have spoken to you, that my joy may be in you, and your joy may be filled.
\v 12 \x - \xt Io. 13, 34.\x* This is my precept, that you love one another, as I have loved you.
\v 13 \f 1 \fr 15.13 \ft The Gospel upon S. Barnabee's day, and on the eves of some Apostles.\f* Greater love than this no man hath, that a man yield his life for his friends.
\v 14 You are my friends, if you do the things that I command you.
\v 15 Now I call you not servants: for the servant knoweth not what his Lord doeth. But you I have called friends; because all things whatsoever I heard of my Father, I have notified unto you.
\v 16 You chose not me, but I chose you; and have appointed you: that you go, & bring fruit; and your fruit abide: that whatsoever you ask the Father in my name, he may give it you.
\v 17 These things I command you, that you love one another.
\v 18 \f 1 \fr 15.18 \ft The Gospel upon SS. Simon and Jude's day. Octob. 28.\f* If the world hate you, know ye that it hath hated me before you.
\v 19 If you had been of the world, the world would love his own. But because you are not of the world, but I have chosen you out of the world, therefore the world hateth you.
\v 20 Remember my word that I said to you: \x - \xt Io. 13, 16. Mt. 10, 24. Lu. 6, 40.\x* The servant is not greater than his Master. If they have persecuted me, you also will they persecute. If they have kept my word, \f 2 \fr 15.20 \ft He foresheweth that many will not obey the Church's words; & no marvel, because they contemned Christ's own precepts.\f* yours also will they keep.
\v 21 But all these things they will do to you for my name sake; because they know not him that sent me.
\v 22 If I had not come, and spoken to them, they should not have sin: but now they have no excuse of their sin.
\v 23 He that hateth me, hateth my Father also.
\v 24 If I had not done among them works that no other man hath done, they should not have sin: but now both they have seen, and they do hate both me and my Father. \ef - \fr 15.24 \fq If I had. \ft If the Jews had not sinned by refusing Christ, in case he had not done greater miracles than any other: then were it a great folly of Catholics to believe Luther's or Calvin's new opinions without any miracles at all.\ef*
\v 25 But that the word may be fulfilled, which is written in their law: \it That they hated me gratis.\it* \x - \xt Ps. 24, 19.\x*
\v 26 \x - \xt Act. 2, 1.\x* But when the Paraclete cometh whom I \f 2 \fr 15.26 \ft The Gospel upon Sunday within the Octave, of the Ascension. And in a Votive of the B. Trinity.\f* will send you from the Father, the Spirit of truth, which proceedeth from the Father, he shall give testimony of me: \ef - \fr 15.26 \fq Whom I will send. \ft The Holy Ghost is sent by the Son, therefore he proceedeth from him also, as from the Father: though the late Schismatical Greeks think otherwise.\ef*
\v 27 and you shall give testimony, because you are with me from the beginning. \ef - \fr 15.27 \fq You shall give. \ft He vouchsafeth to join together the testimony of the Holy Ghost, and of the Apostles: that we may see the testimony of truth jointly to consist in the Holy Ghost and in the Prelates of the Church.\ef*
\c 16
\cd The cause why he foretelleth them their persecution by the Jews, is, that they be not afterward scandalized thereat. 6. Though they think this heavy news, it is for their vantage that he departeth, because of the great benefits that they shall receive by the coming then of the Holy Ghost, who shall also be his witness against his enemies. 16. Although in this world they shall so be persecuted, yet to his heavenly Father they and their prayers made in his name, shall be most acceptable, and at length the child (that is, Christ in all his members) being born, their joy shall be such as no persecutor can take from them. 31. Howbeit at this instant of his apprehension, they will all forsake him.
\p
\v 1 \sc These\sc* things have I spoken to you, that you be not scandalized.
\v 2 Out of the Synagogues they will \f 1 \fr 16.2 \ft The Heretics translate, \+it Excommunicate you.\+it* See what corruption this is, and the reason thereof, \+it Annot. c. 9, 22.\+it*\f* cast you: but the hour cometh, that every one which killeth you, shall think that he doeth service to God.
\v 3 And these things they will do to you, because they have not known the Father, nor me.
\v 4 But these things I have spoken to you: that when the hour shall come, you may remember them, that I told you.
\v 5 But I told you not these things from the beginning, because I was with you. And now I go to him that sent me, and none of you asketh me: Whither goest thou?
\v 6 \f 1 \fr 16.6 \ft The Gospel upon the 4. Sunday after Easter.\f* But because I have spoken these things to you, sorrow hath filled your heart.
\v 7 But I tell you the truth, it is expedient for you that I go. For if I go not, the Paraclete shall not come to you: but if I go, I will send him to you.
\v 8 And when he is come, he shall argue the world of sin, and of justice, and of judgement.
\v 9 Of sin: because they believed not in me.
\v 10 But of justice: because I go to the Father; and now you shall not see me.
\v 11 And of judgement: because the Prince of this world is now judged.
\v 12 Yet many things I have to say to you: but you cannot bear them now. \ef - \fr 16.12 \fq Yet many things. \ft This place convinceth that the Apostles and the faithful be taught many things, which Christ omitted to teach them for their weakness: and that it was the providence of God that Christ in presence should not teach and order all things, ¹ that we might be no less assured of the things that the Church teacheth by the Holy Ghost, than of the things that himself delivered. \fq ¹ \ft Christ left many things to be taught by the Church.\ef*
\v 13 But when he, the Spirit of truth, cometh, \f 1 \fr 16.13 \ft If he shall teach all truth, & that for ever (as before c. 14, 16:) how is it possible, that the Church can err, or hath erred at any time or in any point?\f* he shall teach you all truth. For he shall not speak of himself; but what things soever he shall hear, he shall speak: and the things that are to come he shall shew you. \ef - \fr 16.13 \fq The Spirit of truth. \ft Ever note that the Holy Ghost in that he is promised to the Church, is called the Spirit of truth. which Holy Spirit for many other causes is given to diverse private men and to all good men, to sanctification: ¹ but to teach all truth and preserve in truth and from error, he is promised and performed only to the Church and the chief Governor and general Councels thereof. \fq ¹ \ft The Spirit of truth.\ef*
\v 14 He shall glorify me; because he shall receive of mine, and shall shew to you.
\v 15 All things whatsoever the Father hath, be mine. Therefore I said, that he shall receive of mine, and shall shew to you.
\v 16 \f 1 \fr 16.16 \ft The Gospel upon the 3. Sunday after Easter.\f* A little while, and now you shall not see me; & again a little while, & you shall see me: because I go to the Father.
\v 17 Some therefore of his Disciples said one to another: What is this that he saith to us: A little while, and you shall not see me; and again a little while and you shall see me, and, because I go to the Father?
\v 18 They said therefore: What is this that he saith, A little while? we know not what he speaketh.
\v 19 And \sc Jesus\sc* knew, that they would ask him; & he said to them: Of this you do question among yourselves, because I said to you: A little while, and you shall not see me; and again a little while, and you shall see me.
\v 20 \f 1 \fr 16.20 \ft The Gospel for some Martyrs between Easter. & Whitsunday.\f* Amen, amen I say to you, that you shall weep and lament, but the world shall rejoice: and you shall be made sorrowful, but your sorrow shall be turned into joy.
\v 21 A woman when she travaileth, hath sorrow, because her hour is come: but when she hath brought forth the child, now she remembereth not the anguish for joy, that a man is born into the world.
\v 22 And you therefore, now indeed you have sorrow, but I will see you again, and your heart shall rejoice; and your joy no man shall take from you.
\v 23 \f 1 \fr 16.23 \ft The Gospel upon the 5. Sunday after Easter.\f* And in that day me you shall not ask any thing. Amen, amen I say to you, if you ask the Father any thing \f 2 \fr 16.23 \ft Upon this the Church concludeth all her prayers, \+it Per Christum Dominum nostrum,\+it* even those also that be made to Saints.\f* in my name, he will give it you.
\v 24 Until now you have not asked any thing in my name. Ask and you shall receive; that your joy may be full.
\v 25 These things in proverbs I have spoken to you. The hour cometh when in proverbs I will no more speak to you, but plainly of the Father I will shew you.
\v 26 In that day you shall ask in my name: and I say not to you, that I will ask the Father for you.
\v 27 For the Father himself loveth you, because you have loved me, and have believed that I came forth from God.
\v 28 I came forth from the Father, and came into the world: again I leave the world, and I go to the Father.
\v 29 His Disciples say to him: Behold now thou speakest plainly, and sayest no proverb.
\v 30 Now we know that thou knowest all things, and thou needest not that any man ask thee. In this we believe that thou camest forth from God.
\v 31 \sc Jesus\sc* answered them: Now do you believe?
\v 32 \x - \xt Mt. 27, 31. Mr. 14, 27.\x* Behold the hour cometh, and it is now come, that you shall be scattered every man into his own, and me you shall leave alone: and I am not alone because the Father is with me.
\v 33 These things I have spoken to you, that in me you may have peace. In the world you shall have distress, but have confidence, I have overcome the world.
\c 17
\cd After his Sermon of farewell, he prayeth to his Father, that seeing he hath now finished his work, he will give him his appointed glory, for the conversion of all nations, 6. and preserve his Apostles, and his Church after them in unity and verity (that is, from Schism and Heresy:) 24. finally also glorify them with him in heaven.
\p
\v 1 \sc These\sc* \f 1 \fr 17.1 \ft The Gospel upon Ascension eve.\f* things spake \sc Jesus\sc*: and lifting up his eyes into Heaven, he said: Father, the hour is come, \f 2 \fr 17.1 \ft The Father glorifieth the Son by raising him from death, exalting him up to his right hand, making all creatures to bow down at his Name, and giving him all powers and judgement. The Son again glorifieth the Father, by making his honour, which only in a manner was in Jewry before, now known to all Nations.\f* glorify thy Son, that thy Son may glorify thee.
\v 2 As thou hast given him power over all flesh, that all which thou hast given him, to them he may give life everlasting.
\v 3 And this is life everlasting that they know thee, the only true God, and whom thou hast sent \sc Jesus Christ\sc*. \ef - \fr 17.3 \fq Life everlasting. \ft Both the life of glory in heaven, and of grace here in the Church, consisteth in the knowledge of God: ¹ that, in perfect vision: this, in faith working by charity. For, knowledge of God without keeping his commandments, is not true knowledge, that is to say, it is an unprofitable knowledge. \+it 1 Io. 2.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft True knowledge of God.\ef*
\v 4 I have glorified thee upon the earth: I have consummated the work which thou gavest me to do:
\v 5 and now glorify thou me O Father with thyself, with the glory which I had before the world was, with thee.
\v 6 I have manifested thy name to the men whom thou gavest me out of the world. Thine they were, and to me thou gavest them: and they have kept thy word.
\v 7 Now they have known that all things which thou gavest me are from thee:
\v 8 because the words which thou gavest me, I have given them: and they have received, and known in very deed that I came forth from thee, and have believed that thou didst send me.
\v 9 For them do I pray: Not for the world do I pray, but for them whom thou hast given me;
\v 10 because they be thine: and all things be thine, and thine be mine: and I am glorified in them. And now I am not in the world, and these are in the world, & I come to thee.
\v 11 \f 1 \fr 17.11 \ft The Gospel in a votive Mass against Schism.\f* Holy father, \f 2 \fr 17.11 \ft His petition is specially to keep the Apostles and his Church in unity and from Schisms.\f* keep them in thy name, whom thou hast given me; that they may be one, as also we.
\v 12 When I was with them, I kept them in thy name. Those \x - \xt Io. 18. 9.\x* whom thou gavest me, have I kept: and none of them perished, but the son of perdition, that the \x - \xt Ps. 40, 10. 108, 8.\x* scripture may be fulfilled.
\v 13 And now I come to thee: and these things I speak in the world, that they may have my joy filled in themselves.
\v 14 I have given them thy word, and the world hath hated them, because they are not of the world: as I also am not of the world.
\v 15 I pray not that thou take them away out of the world, but that thou preserve them from evil.
\v 16 Of the world they are not: as I also am not of the world.
\v 17 Sanctify them in truth. Thy word is truth. \ef - \fr 17.17 \fq Sanctify them. \ft ¹ Christ prayeth that the Apostles, their successors, & all that shall be of their belief, may be sanctified in truth. which is as much to say, as to desire that the Church may ever have the Spirit of truth, and be free from error. which prayer of Christ had not been heard, if the Church might err. \fq ¹ \ft The Church cannot err.\ef*
\v 18 As thou didst send me into the world, I also have sent them into the world.
\v 19 And for them I do \f 1 \fr 17.19 \ft To sanctify himself, is to sacrifice himself, by dedicating his holy body and blood to his Father, both upon the Cross, and in the holy Sacrament.\f* sanctify myself: that they also may be sanctified in truth.
\v 20 And not for them only do I pray, but for them also that by their word shall believe in me: \ef - \fr 17.20 \fq But for them. \ft He expresseth (and it is a great comfort) that he prayeth not only for the Apostles, but for the whole Church after them, ¹ that is, for all believers. And all this profound and divine prayer is resembled in the holy Canon of the Mass before the consecration, as here it was made before his visible Sacrifice on the cross. \fq ¹ \ft The Canon of the Mass.\ef*
\v 21 that they all may be one, as thou (Father) in me & I in thee; that they also in us may be one: that the world may believe that thou hast sent me.
\v 22 And the glory that thou hast given me, have I given to them; that they may be one as we also are one.
\v 23 I in them, and thou in me: that they may be consummate in one: and the world may know that thou hast sent me, and hast loved them, as me also thou hast loved.
\v 24 Father, whom thou hast given me, I will, that where I am, they also may be with me: that they may see my glory which thou hast given me, because thou hast loved me before the creation of the world.
\v 25 Just Father, the world hath not known thee. But I have known thee: and these have known, that thou didst send me.
\v 26 And I have notified thy name to them, and will notify it: that the love wherewith thou hast loved me may be in them, and I in them.
\c 18
\cd Being gone to the place that Judas the Traitor did know, 4. he offereth himself to the band of his enemies, sheweth his Divine might in overthrowing them all with a word, and in saving his Apostles from them also with a word: 10. rebuketh Peter that would defend him from them: 12. and so being apprehended, is brought bound to Annas and Caiphas, where he is stricken by a servant, and thrice denied of Peter. 28. Again in the morning he is by them brought to Pilate. 29. Who demanding their accusation, whereas they would oppress him with their authority, 33. and examining the point of his kingdom, pronounceth him innocent: yet they cry rather to have a thief's life saved.
\p
\v 1 \sc When \x - \xt Mt. 26, 36. Mr. 14, 32. Lu. 22, 39.\x*\f 2 \fr 18.1 \ft THURSDAY night. The PASSION according to S. John in these two Chapters, is the Gospel at Mass upon Good Friday. So the PASSION is read in Holy Week four times, according to the four Evangelists, as S. Augustine appointed also in his Church at \+it Hippo. Ser. 144. de tempore.\+it*\f* Jesus\sc* had said these things, he went forth with his Disciples beyond the Torrent-Cedron, where was a garden, into the which he entered and his Disciples.
\v 2 And Judas also, that betrayed him, knew the place: because \sc Jesus\sc* had often resorted thither together with his Disciples.
\v 3 \x - \xt Mt. 26, 47. Mr. 14, 43. Luc. 22, 47.\x* Judas therefore having received the band of men, and of the chief Priests and Pharisees, ministers, cometh thither with lanterns and torches and weapons.
\v 4 \sc Jesus\sc* therefore knowing all things that should come upon him, went forth, and said to them: Whom seek ye?
\v 5 They answered him, \sc Jesus\sc* of Nazareth. \sc Jesus\sc* saith to them: I am he. And Judas also that betrayed him, stood with them.
\v 6 As soon therefore as he said to them, I am he; they went backward, and fell to the ground.
\v 7 Again therefore he asked them: Whom seek ye? And they said: \sc Jesus\sc* of Nazareth.
\v 8 \sc Jesus\sc* answered, I have told you, that I am he. If therefore you seek me, let these go their ways.
\v 9 That the word might be fulfilled which he said, \x - \xt Io. 17, 12.\x* That of them whom thou hast given me, I have not lost any.
\v 10 Simon Peter therefore having a sword, drew it out; and smote the servant of the high Priest, & cut off his right ear. And the name of the servant was Malchus.
\v 11 \sc Jesus\sc* therefore said to Peter: Put up thy sword into the scabbard. The chalice which my Father hath given me, shall not I drink it?
\v 12 The band therefore and the Tribune & the ministers of the Jews apprehended \sc Jesus\sc*, and bound him:
\v 13 and they brought him to Annas first, for he was father in law to Caiphas, who was the high Priest of that year.
\v 14 And \x - \xt Io. 11, 49.\x* Caiphas was he that had given the counsel to the Jews, That it is expedient that one man die for the people.
\v 15 \x - \xt Mt. 26, 58. Mr. 14, 54. Lu. 22, 54.\x* And Simon Peter followed \sc Jesus\sc*, and another Disciple. And that Disciple was known to the high Priest, and went in with \sc Jesus\sc* into the court of the high Priest.
\v 16 But Peter stood at the door without. The other Disciple therefore that was known to the high Priest, went forth, and spake to the portress, and brought in Peter.
\v 17 The wench therefore that was portress, saith to Peter: Art not thou also of this man's Disciples? He saith to her, \f 1 \fr 18.17 \ft It is all one for a man to deny Christ, and that he is a disciple of Christ, or a Catholic, or a Christian man, when he is demanded. \+it Aug. tract. 113 in Io.\+it* for so Peter here denieth Christ, in denying himself to be his Disciple.\f* I am not.
\v 18 And the servants & ministers stood at a fire of coals, because it was cold, and warmed themselves. And with them was Peter also standing, and warming himself.
\v 19 The high Priest therefore asked \sc Jesus\sc* of his Disciples, and of his doctrine.
\v 20 \sc Jesus\sc* answered him: I have openly spoken to the world: I have always taught in the Synagogue, and in the temple whither all the Jews resort together: and in secret I have spoken nothing.
\v 21 Why askest thou me? ask them that have heard what I have spoken unto them: behold they know what things I have said.
\v 22 When he had said these things, one of the ministers standing by, gave \sc Jesus\sc* a blow, saying: Answerest thou the high Priest so?
\v 23 \sc Jesus\sc* answered him: If I have spoken ill, give testimony of evil: but if well, why strikest thou me?
\v 24 And Annas sent him bound to Caiphas the high priest.
\v 25 And Simon Peter was standing, and warming himself. They said therefore to him: Art not thou also of his Disciples: He denied and said: I am not.
\v 26 One of the servants of the high Priest saith to him, his cousin whose ear Peter did cut off: Did not I see thee in the garden with him?
\v 27 Again therefore Peter denied: and forthwith the cock crew.
\v 28 \x - \xt Mt. 27, 1. Mr. 15, 1. Lu. 23, 1.\x*\f 2 \fr 18.28 \ft GOOD FRIDAY.\f* They therefore bring \sc Jesus\sc* from Caiphas into the Palace. And it was morning: and they went not in into the Palace, that they might not be contaminated, but that they might eat the Pasch.
\v 29 Pilate therefore went forth to them without, and said: What accusation bring you against this man?
\v 30 They answered and said to him: If he were not a malefactor, we would not have delivered him up to thee.
\v 31 Pilate therefore said to them: Take him you, and according to your law judge him. The Jews therefore said to him: It is not lawful for us to kill any man.
\v 32 \x - \xt Io. 12, 33. Mt. 20, 19.\x* That the word of \sc Jesus\sc* might be fulfilled which he said, signifying what death he should die.
\v 33 \x - \xt Mt. 27, 11. Mr. 15, 2. Lu. 23, 4.\x* Pilate therefore went into the Palace again, and called \sc Jesus\sc*, and said to him: Art thou the King of the Jews?
\v 34 \sc Jesus\sc* answered: Sayest thou this of thyself, or have others told it thee of me?
\v 35 Pilate answered: Why, am I a Jew? \f 1 \fr 18.35 \ft It pleased God, that Christ who was to die both for the Jews & the Gentiles, should be betrayed of the one, and put to death by the other.\f* Thy Nation, and the chief Priests have delivered thee up to me: what hast thou done?
\v 36 \sc Jesus\sc* answered: My Kingdom is not of this world. If my Kingdom were of this world, my ministers verily would strive that I should not be delivered to the Jews. But now my Kingdom is not from hence.
\v 37 Pilate therefore said to him: Art thou a King then? \sc Jesus\sc* answered: Thou sayest, that I am a King. For this was I born, and for this came I into the world: that I should give testimony to the truth. Every one that is of the truth, heareth my voice.
\v 38 Pilate saith to him: What is truth? And when he had said this, he went forth again to the Jews, and saith to them: I find no cause in him.
\v 39 \x - \xt Mt. 27, 15. Mr. 15, 6. Lu. 23, 17.\x* But you have a custom that I should release one to you in the Pasch: will you therefore that I release unto you the King of the Jews?
\v 40 They all therefore cried again, saying: Not him but Barabbas. And Barabbas was a thief.
\c 19
\cd The Jews are not satisfied with his scourging and irrision. 8. Pilate hearing them say that he made himself the Son of God, is more afraid. 12. Yet, they urging him with his loyalty toward Cæsar, and professing that themselves will no king but Cæsar, he yieldeth unto them. 17. And so Christ carrying his own Cross, is crucified between two thieves, 19. Pilate writing notoriously the only cause of his death to be, for that he is their king or Christ. 23. His garments be so used, even as the Scriptures foretold. 25. He hath special care of his mother to the end. 28. He signifieth all that was written of his Passion, to be fulfilled, and so yieldeth up his ghost. 31. Then by the Jews' means also other Scriptures about his legs and side, are fulfilled. 38. And finally, he is honourably buried.
\p
\v 1 \sc Then\sc* therefore Pilate took \sc Jesus\sc*, and scourged him. \x - \xt Mt. 27, 27. Mr. 15, 16.\x*
\v 2 And the soldiers platting a crown of thorns, put it upon his head: and they put about him a purple garment.
\v 3 And they came to him, and said: Hail King of the Jews; and they gave him blows.
\v 4 Pilate went forth again, and saith to them: Behold I bring him forth unto you, that you may know that I find no cause in him.
\v 5 \sc Jesus\sc* therefore went forth carrying the crown of thorns, and the purple vestiment. And he saith to them: Lo the man.
\v 6 When the chief Priests therefore and the ministers had seen him, they cried, saying: Crucify, crucify him. Pilate saith to them: Take him you, and crucify him. For I find no cause in him.
\v 7 The Jews answered him: We have a Law; and according to the Law he ought to die; because he hath made himself the Son of God.
\v 8 When Pilate therefore had heard this saying, he feared more.
\v 9 And he entered into the Palace again; and he saith to \sc Jesus\sc*: Whence art thou? But \sc Jesus\sc* gave him no answer.
\v 10 Pilate therefore saith to him: Speakest thou not to me? knowest thou not that I have power to crucify thee, and I have power to release thee?
\v 11 \sc Jesus\sc* answered: Thou shouldest not have any power against me, unless it were given thee from above. Therefore he that hath betrayed me to thee, hath the greater sin.
\v 12 From thence forth Pilate sought to release him. But the Jews cried, saying: If thou release this man, thou art not Cæsar's friend. Every one that maketh himself a king, speaketh against Cæsar.
\v 13 But Pilate when he had heard these words, brought forth \sc Jesus\sc*: and he sat in the judgement seat, in the place that is called Lithostrotos, and in Hebrew Gabbatha.
\v 14 And it was the Parasceve of Pasch, about the \f 1 \fr 19.14 \ft He meaneth midday, counting from sun rising. For so doth the Scripture count the hours of the day. \+it Mat. 20. Mar. 15. Luc. 23. Io. 4. Act. 3. & 10.\+it*\f* sixth hour, and he saith to the Jews: Lo your King.
\v 15 But they cried: Away, away with him, crucify him. Pilate saith to them: Shall I crucify your King? The chief Priests answered: We have no King, but Cæsar.
\v 16 Then therefore he delivered him unto them for to be crucified. And they took \sc Jesus\sc*, and led him forth.
\v 17 \x - \xt Mt. 27, 33. Mr. 15, 20. Lu. 23, 33.\x* And bearing his own cross he went forth into that which is called the place of Calvary, in Hebrew Golgotha. \ef - \fr 19.17 \fq His own cross. \ft This cross, for that it was the instrument of our redemption, and as it were the altar of the supreme sacrifice, highly sanctified by the touching, bearing, and oblation of the sacred body and blood of our Lord, is truly called the \+sc Holy Cross\+sc*: ¹ and hath been endued with virtue of miracles, both the whole and every little piece thereof. For the which causes, and specially for the most need memorial of Christ's Passion, it hath been visited in Pilgrimages, honoured by festival days and otherwise, reserved & reverenced of the ancient fathers, with all devotion: as contrarywise it hath been abused of Pagans, Apostatæs, and Heretics, seeking in vain to deface and destroy it. See S. Cyril \+it li. 6 cont. Iulian.\+it* S. Hierom \+it ep. 17. S. Paulinus ep. 11. Ruffinus li. 1. c. 7. & 8. Evagr. histo. li. 4 c. 25.\+it* S. Leo \+it ep. 72. and Ser. 8 de Passione. Paulus Diac. li. 18.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft The HOLY CROSS.\ef*
\v 18 Where they crucified him, and with him two others, on the one side & on the other, and in the midst \sc Jesus\sc*.
\v 19 And Pilate wrote a title also: and he put it upon the cross. And it was written, \sc Jesus of Nazareth The King of The Jews\sc*.
\v 20 This title therefore many of the Jews did read: because the place where \sc Jesus\sc* was crucified, was nigh to the city: and it was written in \f 1 \fr 19.20 \ft These three tongues being for other causes most famous before in all the world, are now also dedicated to God in the triumphant title of the Cross of Christ, and in them the Holy Scriptures are more conveniently written, taught, & preserved.\f* Hebrew, in Greek, and in Latin.
\v 21 The chief Priests therefore of the Jews said to Pilate: Write not, The King of the Jews; but that he said, I am King of the Jews.
\v 22 Pilate answered: That which I have written, I have written.
\v 23 The \x - \xt * Mt. 27, 35. Mr. 15, 24. Luc. 23, 34.\x* soldiers therefore when they had crucified him, took his garments (and they made four parts, to every soldier a part) & his coat. And his \f 2 \fr 19.23 \ft This coat without seam is a figure of the unity of the church. \+it Cypr. de unit. Ec.\+it* And Euthymius and other write that our Lady made it.\f* coat was without seam, wrought from the top throughout.
\v 24 They said therefore one to another: Let us not cut it, but let us cast lots for it whose it shall be. That the scripture might be fulfilled saying: \it They have parted my garments among them: and upon my vesture they have cast lots.\it* \x - \xt Ps. 21, 19.\x* And the soldiers did these things.
\v 25 \f 1 \fr 19.25 \ft The Gospel in a votive Mass of our B. Lady between Easter & Whitsunday.\f* And there stood beside the cross of \sc Jesus\sc*, \f 2 \fr 19.25 \ft The great love, faith, courage, compassion, and sorrows, that our Lady had: who forsook not the Cross and her Son, when so many were fled from him, and his chief Apostles denied him.\f* his mother, and his mother's sister, Mary of Cleophas, and Mary Magdalene.
\v 26 When \sc Jesus\sc* therefore had seen his mother and the Disciple standing whom he loved, he saith to his mother: Woman, behold thy son. \ef - \fr 19.26 \fq To his mother. \ft ¹ The marvelous respect that Christ had to his mother, vouchsaving to speak to her, and to take order for her even from the cross in the midst of his infinite anguishes and mysteries a working for mankind. \fq ¹ \ft By this you see why in Catholic Churches MARY and John stand by the Rood.\ef*
\v 27 After that, he saith to the Disciple: Behold thy mother. And from that hour the Disciple took her to his own. \ef - \fr 19.27 \fq To the Disciple. \ft A great honour to John and charge, to have that blessed jewel in keeping: and an unspeakable comfort, that from that day forward the one was to the other mother and son. \+it virginem virgini commendavit\+it*, saith S. Hierom. \+it He commended the virgin to a virgin\+it*. ¹ \fq ¹ \ft \+it li. 2. adv. Iovinian.\+it*\ef*
\v 28 \x - \xt Psa. 68, 22.\x* Afterward \sc Jesus\sc* knowing that all things were now consummate, that the \f 2 \fr 19.28 \ft b The Gospel in a votive Mass of the Passion.\f* scripture might be fulfilled, he saith, I thirst.
\v 29 A vessel therefore stood there full of vinegar. And they putting a sponge full of vinegar about hyssop, offered it to his mouth.
\v 30 \sc Jesus\sc* therefore when he had taken the vinegar, said: It is consummate. And bowing his head, he gave up the ghost.
\v 31 The Jews therefore (because it was the Parasceve) that the bodies might not remain upon the cross on the Sabboth (for that was a great Sabboth day) they desired Pilate that their legs might be broken, and they might be taken away.
\v 32 The soldiers therefore came: and of the first indeed they brake the legs, and of the other that was crucified with him.
\v 33 But after they were come to \sc Jesus\sc*, when they saw that he was dead, they did not break his legs,
\v 34 but one of the soldiers with a spear opened his side, & incontinent there came forth blood and water. \ef - \fr 19.34 \fq Blood and water. \ft This piercing of Christ's side, though on the soldier's part it was done blindly and insolently, ¹ yet by God's ordinance it contained great mysteries, and was prefigured by Moyses striking the rock with his rod: ² as this stream of blood and water drawn miraculously out of his dead body, running in the Sacraments of the Church after the people of God, was signified by the water of the same rock following the Israelites in the desert, \+it Out of this side\+it* (saith S. Augustine) ³ \+it issued the Sacraments. Hence\+it* (saith S. Chrysostom) ⁴ \+it the great mysteries have their beginning\+it*. Who warneth us, that when we come to drink of the holy chalice, we should so approach, as though we drew the blood out of Christ's side. And both blood and water apart did flow forth, to shew us the fountain of the two principal Sacraments, and their several matters, Baptism and the Eucharist, springing to life everlasting in the Church. ⁵ The fathers also say that the Church who is Christ's spouse and his coadjutrice in applying the blood and water to the benefit of the Faithful, was here formed, builded, and taken out of this holy side of Christ sleeping on the Cross, as ⁶ Eve was of Adam's side, when he was cast a sleep in Paradise. \fq ¹ \ft The Sacraments issued out of Christ's side, and thence have their virtue. \fq ² \ft \+it Exo. 18. Num. 30.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it Tract. 9. in Ioan.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft \+it Hom. 84. in Ioan.\+it* \fq ⁵ \ft The CHURCH builded of Christ's side, as Eve of Adam's. \fq ⁶ \ft \+it Gen. 2, 21.\+it*\ef*
\v 35 And he that saw it, hath given testimony: and his testimony is true. And he knoweth that he saith true, that you also may believe.
\v 36 For these things were done that the scripture might be fulfilled: \it You shall not break a bone of him.\it* \x - \xt Exo. 12, 46.\x*
\v 37 And again another scripture saith: \it They shall look on him whom they pierced.\it* \x - \xt Zac. 12, 10.\x*
\v 38 And after these things \x - \xt * Mt. 27, 57. Mr. 15, 42. Lu. 23, 50.\x* Joseph of Arimathæa (because he was a Disciple of \sc Jesus\sc*, but secret for fear of the Jews) desired Pilate that he might take away the body of \sc Jesus\sc*. And Pilate permitted. He came therefore, & took away the body of \sc Jesus\sc*.
\v 39 \x - \xt Io. 3, 1. 7, 50.\x* Nicodemus also came, he that at the first came to \sc Jesus\sc* by night, bringing a mixture of myrrh and aloes, about an hundred pounds.
\v 40 They took therefore the body of \sc Jesus\sc*, and bound it in linen cloths with the spices, as the manner is with the Jews to bury.
\v 41 And there was in the place where he was crucified, a garden: and in the garden a new monument, wherein no man yet had been laid.
\v 42 There therefore because of the Parasceve of the Jews, they laid \sc Jesus\sc*, because the monument was hard by.
\c 20
\cd Upon Easter day his body is missed in the Sepulcher, first by M. Magdalene, 3. secondly by Peter also and John, the winding clothes yet remaining. 11. Then to M. Magdalene, after she had seen two Angels, JESUS also himself appeareth. 18. She having told to the Disciples, he appeareth to them also the same day, and sendeth them as himself was sent, giving them the Holy Ghost to remit and to retain sins. 26. Again upon low Sunday he appeareth to them, letting Thomas see, that he might believe, and commending such as not seeing yet do believe. 30. The effect of this book.
\p
\v 1 \sc And\sc* \x - \xt EASTER DAY. Mt. 28, 1. Mr. 16, 1. Lu. 24, 1.\x*\f 2 \fr 20.1 \ft The Gospel upon Saturday in Easter week.\f* the \f 3 \fr 20.1 \ft That is, the first day of the week, as some interpret it, taking Sabboth (as sometime it is) for a week. This is our Sunday, called \+it Dies Dominica,\+it* because of our Lord's resurrection. See the \+it marg. annot. Luc. 24, 1.\+it*\f* first of the Sabboth, Mary Magdalene cometh early, when it was yet dark, unto the monument: and she saw the stone taken away from the monument.
\v 2 She ran therefore and cometh to Simon Peter, and to the other Disciple whom \sc Jesus\sc* loved, and saith to them: They have taken our Lord out of the monument, and we know not where they have laid him.
\v 3 Peter therefore went forth and that other Disciple, and they came to the monument.
\v 4 And both ran together, and that other Disciple did out-run Peter, and came first to the monument. \x - \xt Luc. 24, 12.\x*
\v 5 And when he had stooped down, he saw the linen cloths lying: but yet he went not in.
\v 6 Simon Peter therefore cometh, following him, and went into the monument and saw the linen cloths lying,
\v 7 and the napkin that had been upon his head, not lying with the linen cloths, but apart, wrapped up into one place.
\v 8 Then therefore went in that other Disciple also which came first to the monument: and he saw, and believed.
\v 9 For as yet they knew not the scripture, that he should rise again from the dead.
\v 10 The Disciples therefore departed again to themselves.
\v 11 \x - \xt Mt. 28, 1. Mr. 16, 5. Luc. 24, 4.\x* But \f 2 \fr 20.11 \ft b The Gospel upon Thursday in Easter week.\f* Mary stood at the \f 3 \fr 20.11 \ft The Sepulchres of Martyrs (saith \+it S. Hierom ep. 17.\+it* ) we do honour every-where, & putting their holy ashes to our eyes, if we may, we touch it also with our mouth: and be there some that think the monument wherein our Lord was laid, is to be neglected: where the Devil and his Angels, as often as they are cast out of the possessed before the said monument, tremble and roar as if they stood before the judgement seat of Christ?\f* monument without, weeping. Therefore as she was weeping, she stooped down, & looked into the monument:
\v 12 and she saw two Angels in white, sitting, one at the head, and one at the feet, where the body of \sc Jesus\sc* had been laid.
\v 13 They say to her: Woman, why weepest thou? She saith to them: Because they have taken away my Lord, and I know not where they have put him.
\v 14 When she had said thus, she turned backward, and saw \sc Jesus\sc* standing; and she knew not that it is \sc Jesus\sc*.
\v 15 \sc Jesus\sc* saith to her: Woman, why weepest thou? whom seekest thou? She thinking that it was the gardener, saith to him: Sir, if thou hast carried him away, tell me where thou hast laid him; and I will take him away.
\v 16 \sc Jesus\sc* saith to her: Mary. She turning saith to him: Rabboni (which is to say, Master.)
\v 17 \sc Jesus\sc* said to her: Do not touch me, for I am not yet ascended to my Father: but go to my Brethren, and say to them, I ascend to my Father and your Father, my God and your God.
\v 18 Mary Magdalene cometh and telleth the Disciples, That I have seen our Lord, and thus he said unto me.
\v 19 \x - \xt Mr. 16, 14. Lu. 24, 36. 1. Cor. 15, 5.\x* Therefore when it was \f 2 \fr 20.19 \ft The Gospel upon Dominica in albis or Low Sunday, And for peace.\f* late that day, the first of the Sabboths, and the doors were shut, where the Disciples were gathered together for fear of the Jews, \sc Jesus\sc* came and stood in the midst, and saith to them: Peace be to you. \ef - \fr 20.19 \fq The doors were shut. \ft Such Heretics as deny Christ's body to be or that it can be in the B. Sacrament, for that it is in heaven, and cannot be in two places at once, nor without the natural manner of the quantity, ¹ space or place agreeable to the condition of his humanity, be invincibly refuted by Christ's entering into the Disciples, the doors shut: and by that that his true natural body, whole and perfect in all his limbs, length, breadth, and thickness, distinct and diverse from the substance and corpulence of the wood, was in the same proper place that the wood was in, and passed through the same: as he also came out of his mother's womb, the clausure not sturred: and passed through the stone, out of his Sepulcher. By all which the Heretics being plainly reproved, and convinced of infidelity, they boldly deny the plain Scriptures, or so fondly shift themselves from the evidence thereof, that their impudency is specially to be marked in this point. ² Some say, that he came in at the window: some, that the door opened of itself to let him in: some, that to come in, the doors being shut, signifieth no more, but that he came in late in the evening, at what time men use to shut their doors: and such other slights to defend falsehood, against express Scriptures, and against the Apostle's testimony, who therefore took him to be a Spirit, because they saw him stand suddenly in the midst of them, all the house being close shut. And the Fathers all confess that he went in, the doors being shut. See S. Ambrose \+it li. 10 in Lucam c. 24.\+it* S. Augustine \+it ep. 3 ad Volusian. & li. 22 de civit. c. 8,\+it* & S. Cyril, \+it in Io li. 12, c. 53.\+it* & S. Hiero. \+it li. 1 cont. Iovinianum c. 21.\+it* We know it is the natural course of God's ordinance, that every body should have but one and his own proper place fitted to the lineaments, quantity, terms and limits of the same: without which naturally the bodies were no where, and consequently not at all, as S. Augustine saith \+it ad Dardanum.\+it* ³ but that God supernaturally and miraculously cannot by his omnipotency dispose otherwise of his own body, ⁴ than the natural form or quantity or quality thereof require, that is great incredulity, seeing we must believe that he can do so with any other body of mere men or other creatures, the Scripture being plain that he can make a camel pass through a needle's eye, continuing in his natural figure and quantity still: and S. Augustine ⁵ telleth of a woman whose ring fell from her girdle, both being fast and whole: and Rupertus of a Religious man, whose girdle fast buckled fell down before him from his body. \+it De off. Eccl.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft The being of Christ's body in the B. Sacrament without space or quantity correspondent there unto, is proved by other examples in Scriptures. \fq ² \ft Heretical shifts to avoid plain Scripture. \fq ³ \ft \+it August. ep. 57.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft Christ can dispose of his own body & others above nature. \fq ⁵ \ft \+it Mat. 19. Aug. li. 22. c. 8. de civ. Dei.\+it*\ef* \ef - \fr 20.19 \ft ¹ Therefore it is to much unfaithfulness, by rules of place to embar Christ of his will or wisdom to be in the Sacrament how himself list, and on as many altars or places as he liketh. We detest for all that, the wicked heresy of certain Protestants, holding quite contrary to the Zuinglians, that Christ according to his Humanity is in every place where the Divinity is: which is both against faith, and the common rules of nature and divinity. \fq ¹ \ft Christ sheweth his commission & so giveth the Apostles power to remit sins.\ef*
\v 20 And when he had said this, he shewed them his hands and side. The Disciples therefore were glad when they saw our Lord.
\v 21 He said therefore to them again: \f 1 \fr 20.21 \ft Though he gave them his peace hard before, yet now entering to a new divine action, to prepare their hearts to grace and attention, he blesseth them again.\f* Peace be to you. As my Father hath sent me, I also do send you. \ef - \fr 20.21 \fq As my Father. \ft As when he gave them commission to preach and baptise through the world, he made mention of his own power therein: so here before he institute the Sacrament of Penance, and give them authority to remit sins, ¹ lest the wicked should ask afterward, by what right they do such great functions, he sheweth his Father's commission given to himself, and then in plain terms most amply imparteth the same to his Apostles: that whosoever deny the Apostle's & their successors, the Priests of God's Church, to have right to remit sins, should deny consequently Christ as man to have authority to do the same. \fq ¹ \ft Ubiquetaries or Brentiani.\ef*
\v 22 When he had said this: he breathed upon them; and he said to them: Receive ye the Holy Ghost: \ef - \fr 20.22 \fq He breathed. \ft He giveth the Holy Ghost in and by an external sign, to his Apostles, not visibly and to all such purposes as afterward at Whitsuntide, ¹ but for the grace of the Sacrament of Orders, as S. Augustine saith, ² and that none make doubt of the Priest's right in remission of sins, seeing the Holy Ghost is purposely given them to do this same. In which case if any be yet contentious, he must deny the Holy Ghost to be God, and not to have power to remit sins. \+it It is not absurd\+it* (saith S. Cyril) ³ \+it that they forgive sins, which have the Holy Ghost. For when they remit or retain, the Holy Ghost remitteth or retaineth in them, and that they do two ways, first in Baptism, and then in Penance.\+it* As S. Ambrose also \+it (li. 1 c. 7 de poenitentia)\+it* refelling the Novatians (a Sect of old Heretics which pretending God's glory as our new Sectaries do, denied that Priests could remit sins in the Sacrament of Penance) asketh, why it should be more dishonour to God, or more impossible or inconvenient for men, to forgive sins by Penance than by Baptism, seeing it is the Holy Ghost that doeth it, by the Priest's office and ministry in both. \fq ¹ \ft The Holy Ghost is here purposely given to the Apostles, to remit sins. \fq ² \ft \+it Augu. q. no. Test. q. 93. Cont. ep. Parmen. li. 2. c. 11.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it Cyril. li. 12. c. 56. in Io.\+it*\ef*
\v 23 \sc Whose Sins You Shall Forgive, They Are Forgiven Them: And Whose You Shall Retain, They Are Retained\sc*. \ef - \fr 20.23 \fq Whose sins. \ft Power to offer Sacrifice, which is the principal function and act of Priesthood, was given them at the institution of the B. Sacrament: the second and next special faculty of Priesthood, consisting in remitting sins, is here bestowed on them. ¹ And withal the holy Sacrament of Penance implying Contrition, Confession, and Satisfaction in the Penitent, and absolution on the Priest's part, is instituted. For in that, that express power and commission is given to Priests to remit or retain all sins: and in that, that Christ promiseth, whose sins soever they forgive, they be of God forgiven also: and whose sins they retain, they be retained before God: it followeth necessarily, that we be bound to submit ourselves to their judgement for release of our sins. ² For, this wonderful power were given them in vain, if none were bound to seek for absolution at their hands. Neither can any rightly seek for absolution of them, unless they confess particularly at least all their mortal offences, whether they be committed in mind, heart, will and ³ cogitation only, or in word, and work. For, God's priests being in this Sacrament of Penance constituted in Christ's stead as judges in causes of our conscience, cannot rightly rule our cases without full and exact cognition and knowledge of all our sins, and the necessary circumstances and ⁴ differences of the same. Which cannot otherwise be had of them being mortal men, than by our simple, sincere, and distinct utterance to them of our sins, with humble contrite heart, ready to take and to do penance according to their injunction. For that authority to retain sins, ⁵ consisteth specially in enjoining satisfaction and penitential works of praying, fasting, alms, and such like. All which God's ordinance whosoever condemneth or contemneth, as Heretics do, or neglecteth, as some careless Catholics may perhaps do: let them be assured they cannot be saved. Neither must any such Christian man pretend or look to have his sins after Baptism, remitted by God only, without this Sacrament: (which was the old Heresy of Novatians \+it Ambr. li. 1 de poenit. c. 2. Socrat. li. 7 Ec. hist. c. 25\+it* ) more than any may hope to be saved or have his original or other sins before Baptism, ⁶ forgiven by God without the same Sacrament. Let no man deceive himself, this is the \+it second table or board after shipwreck\+it*, as S. Hierom ⁷ calleth it. Whosoever take not hold of it, shall perish without all doubt, because they contemn God's counsel and order for their salvation. And therefore S. Augustine (ep. 180) joining both together, saith it is a pitiful case, when by the absence of God's Priests, men depart this life, \+it aut non regenerati, aut legati.\+it* that is, \+it either not regenerated\+it* by Baptism, \+it or fast bound\+it*, and not absolved by the Sacrament of penance and reconciliation: because they shall be excluded from eternal life, and \+it destruction followeth them.\+it* And S. Victor \+it (li. 2 de persecut. Vandalica)\+it* telleth the miserable lamentation of the people, when their Priests were banished by the Arian Heretics. \+it Who\+it* (say they) \+it shall baptize these infants? Who shall minister penance unto us, & loose us from the bands of sins &c?\+it* And therefore S. Cyprian very often (namely ep. 54) calleth it great cruelty, & such as Priests shall answer for at the later day, to suffer any man that is poenitent of his sins, to depart this life without this reconciliation and absolution: \+it because\+it* (saith he) \+it the Lawmaker himself\+it* (Christ) ⁸ \+it hath granted, that things bound in earth, should also be bound in heaven: and that those things might there be loosed, which were loosed before here in the Church.\+it* ⁹ And it is a world to see, how the Heretics wrastle with this so plain a commission of remitting sins, referring it to preaching, to denouncing God's threats upon sinners, and to we cannot tell what else. ¹⁰ though to our English Protestants this authority seemeth so clear, that in their order of visiting the sick, their Ministers acknowledge & challenge the same, using a formal absolution according to the Church's order, after the special confession of the party. ¹¹ But to conclude the matter, let every one that list to see the true meaning of Christ's words, and the Priest's great power and dignity given them by the same words and other, mark well these words of S. Chrysostome. ¹² \+it For\+it*, saith he, \+it they that dwell on the earth, and converse in it, to them is commission given to dispense those things that are in heaven, to them is it given to have the power which God would not to be given neither to Angels nor Archangels.\+it* ¹³ \+it For, neither to them was it said, Whatsoever you shall bind in earth, shall be bound in heaven: and whatsoever you shall loose in earth, shall be loosed in heaven. The earthly Princes indeed have also power to bind, but the bodies only: but that bond of Priests which I speak of, toucheth the very soul itself and reacheth even to the heavens: in so much that whatsoever the Priests shall do beneath, the self same God doth ratify above, and the sentence of the servants the Lord doth confirm. For indeed what else is this, than that the power of all heavenly things is granted them of God? Whose sins so ever, saith he, you shall retain, they are retained. What power (I beseech you) can be greater than this one? The Father gave all power to the Son: but I see the same power altogether delivered by the Son unto them.\+it* And as this concerneth the Priest's high authority to absolve, so thereupon concerning confession also to be made unto them, the ancient Fathers speak in this sort. S. Cyprian \+it de Lapsis nu. 11. They\+it* (saith he) \+it that have greater faith and fear of God, though they did not fall in persecution, yet because they did only think it in their mind, this very cogitation they confess to God's Priests sorrowfully and plainly, opening their conscience, uttering and discharging the burden of their mind, and seeking wholesome medicine for their wounds though but small and little.\+it* ¹⁴ And a little after. \+it Let every one (my brethren) I beseech you, confess his sin, whiles he is yet alive, whiles his confession may be admitted, whiles satisfaction and remission made by the Priests is acceptable before God.\+it* S. Cyril (or as some think, Origen) \+it li. 2 in Levit.\+it* calleth it a great part of penance, when a man is ashamed, and yet openeth his sins to our Lord's Priest. See also \+it Tertul. li. de Poenit. S. Hiero. in c. 10 Ecclesiastæ. S. Basil. in Regulis breu. quæst, 229.\+it* Who compare sinners that refuse to confess, to them that have some disease in their secret parts, and are ashamed to shew it to the Physician or Surgeon, that might cure it. Where they must needs mean secret confession to be made to them that may absolve. And S. Leo ep. 80 most plainly (as before S. Cyril) expressly nameth Priests. \+it That confession is sufficient which is made first to God, then to the Priest also.\+it* And again. ¹⁵ \+it It is sufficient that the guiltiness of men's consciences be uttered to the Priest only by the secrecy of confession.\+it* S. Hierome in 16 Mat. saith, that Priests loose or bind, \+it audita peccatorum varietate, having heard the variety and differences of sins.\+it* S. Paulinus writeth of S. Ambrose, \+it that as often as any confessed his sins unto him for to receive penance, he so wept for compassion, that thereby he caused the penitent to weep also.\+it* ¹⁶ He addeth moreover, that this holy Doctor was so secret in this case, that no man knew the sins confessed, but God and himself. And S. Augustine ho. 49 de 50 homiliis to. 10. saith thus, \+it Do penance such as is done in the Church, let no man say, I do it secretly, I do it to God. in vain then was it said,\+it* ¹⁷ Whatsoever you shall loose in earth, shall be loosed in heaven. See S. Ambrose \+it de poenitentia\+it* throughout. S. Cyprian \+it de Lapsis\+it*, the book \+it de vera & falsa poenit.\+it* in S. Augustine. beside all antiquity which is full of these speeches concerning absolution, and confession. \fq ¹ \ft The Sacrament of PENANCE instituted. \fq ² \ft Men are bound to confess all their mortal sins, and that in particular. \fq ³ \ft \+it * Cypr. de laps. nu. 11.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft \+it * Hiero. in 16. Mat.\+it* \fq ⁵ \ft To retain sins. \fq ⁶ \ft The necessity of this Sacrament. \fq ⁷ \ft \+it Hiero. ad Demetriadem. c. 6, to. 1.\+it* \fq ⁸ \ft \+it Mat. 18.\+it* \fq ⁹ \ft The Heretics wrestling against plain Scripture. \fq ¹⁰ \ft The English Ministers hear confessions, and absolve. \fq ¹¹ \ft \+it See the Communion book.\+it* \fq ¹² \ft \+it Li. 3. de Sacerd.\+it* \fq ¹³ \ft Priests' power to forgive sins, is above the power of Angels or worldly Princes. \fq ¹⁴ \ft Confession to Priests. \fq ¹⁵ \ft Secret or auricular Confession. \fq ¹⁶ \ft \+it In vita D. Ambr. prope finem.\+it* \fq ¹⁷ \ft \+it Mt. 18.\+it*\ef*
\v 24 \f 1 \fr 20.24 \ft The Gospel upon S. Thomas the Apostle's day, Decemb. 21.\f* But Thomas one of the Twelve, who is called Didymus, was not with them when \sc Jesus\sc* came.
\v 25 The other Disciples therefore said to him: We have seen our Lord. But he said to them: unless I see in his hands the print of the nails, and put my finger into the place of the nails, and put my hand into his side: I will not believe.
\v 26 \f 1 \fr 20.26 \ft LOW SUNDAY.\f* And after eight days, again his Disciples were within; and Thomas with them. \sc Jesus\sc* cometh \f 2 \fr 20.26 \ft See the annotation on the 19. verse of this Chapter.\f* the doors being shut, and stood in the midst, and said: Peace be to you.
\v 27 Then he saith to Thomas: Put in thy finger hither, and see my hands, and bring hither thy hand, and put it into my side; & be not incredulous but faithful.
\v 28 Thomas answered, & said to him: My Lord, & my God.
\v 29 \sc Jesus\sc* saith to him: Because thou hast seen me, Thomas, thou hast believed: \f 1 \fr 20.29 \ft They are more happy that believe without sensible argument or sight, than such as be induced by sense or reason to believe.\f* Blessed are they that have not seen & have believed.
\v 30 \x - \xt Io. 21, 25.\x* Many other signs also did \sc Jesus\sc* in the sight of his Disciples, which are not written in this Book.
\v 31 And these are written, that you may believe that \sc Jesus\sc* is \sc Christ\sc* the Son of God: and that believing, you may have life in his name.
\c 21
\cd Appearing again in Galilee, where Peter was fishing with his fellows: and causing them after they had all night taken none, to catch a great multitude, which Peter draweth to land, where he also dineth them: 15. He (expressing what this fishing signified) maketh Peter his Vicar, committing unto him the feeding of his lambs and sheep: 18. and revealeth unto him, that he also shall be crucified, to the glory of God, 20. admonishing him to mind that, rather than to be curious about John's death.
\p
\v 1 \sc After \f 1 \fr 21.1 \ft The Gospel on Wednesday in Easter week.\f* Jesus\sc* manifested himself again to the Disciples at the sea of Tiberias. And he manifested thus.
\v 2 There were together Simon Peter, and Thomas who is called Didymus, and Nathanael which was of Cana in Galilee, & the sons of Zebedee, and two others of his Disciples.
\v 3 Simon Peter saith to them: I go to fish. They say to him: We also come with thee. And they went forth and got up into the boat: and that night they took nothing.
\v 4 But when morning was now come, \sc Jesus\sc* stood on the shore: yet the Disciples knew not that it was \sc Jesus\sc*.
\v 5 \sc Jesus\sc* therefore saith to them: Children, have you any meat? They answered him, No.
\v 6 He saith to them: Cast the net on the right side of the boat; and you shall find. They therefore did cast it: and now they were not able to draw it for the multitude of fishes.
\v 7 That Disciples therefore whom \sc Jesus\sc* loved, saith to Peter: It is our Lord. \f 1 \fr 21.7 \ft See in S. Augustine \+it tract. 122. in Io.\+it* the great mystery hereof concerning the CHURCH, and in S. Gregory \+it hom. 24. in Evang.\+it* and S. Bernard \+it li. 2. c. 8 de consid.\+it* Peter's PRIMACY here mystically signified.\f* Simon Peter when he had heard that it is our Lord, girded his coat unto him (for he was naked) & cast himself into the sea.
\v 8 But the other Disciples came in the boat (for they were not far from the land, but as it were two hundred cubits) drawing the net of fishes.
\v 9 Therefore after they came down to land, they saw hot coals lying, and fish laid thereon, and bread.
\v 10 \sc Jesus\sc* saith to them: Bring hither of the fishes that you took now.
\v 11 Simon Peter went up, and drew the net to the land, full of great fishes, an hundred fifty three. And although they were so many the net was not broken.
\v 12 \sc Jesus\sc* saith to them: Come, dine. And none of them that sat at meat, \f 1 \fr 21.12 \ft \+it the disciples,\+it*\f* durst ask him: Who art thou? knowing that it is our Lord.
\v 13 And \sc Jesus\sc* cometh & taketh the bread and giveth them, & the fish in like manner.
\v 14 This now the \f 1 \fr 21.14 \ft Not the third apparition, but the third day of his apparitions: for he appeared in the very day of his Resurrection often, again upon Low Sunday, then this third time, And S. Mark saying, \+it last he appeared, c. 16, 14:\+it* meaneth his last apparition the first day.\f* third time \sc Jesus\sc* was manifested to his Disciples, after he was risen from the dead.
\v 15 \f 1 \fr 21.15 \ft The Gospel on the eve of SS. Peter and Paul.\f* Therefore when they had dined, \sc Jesus\sc* saith to Simon Peter: Simon of John, lovest thou me more than these? He saith to him: Yea Lord; thou knowest that I love thee. He saith to him: \sc Feed my Lambs\sc*.
\v 16 He saith to him again: Simon of John, lovest thou me? He saith to him: Yea Lord, thou knowest that I love thee. He saith to him: \f 1 \fr 21.16 \ft \+it c ποίμαινε feed & rule.\+it*\f* \sc Feed my Lambs\sc*.
\v 17 He saith to him the third time: Simon of John, lovest thou me? Peter was stroken sad because he said unto him the third time, Lovest thou me? And he said to him: Lord, thou knowest all things: thou knowest that I love thee. He said to him: \sc Feed my Sheep\sc*. \ef - \fr 21.17 \fq Feed my sheep. \ft As it was promised him \+it Mat. 16.\+it* that the Church should be builded upon him, and that the keys of heaven should be given to him: so here it is performed, & he is actually made the general Pastor and Governor of all Christ's sheep. ¹ For though the other ten (as Matthias and Paul also afterward) were Apostles, Bishops, Priests, and had authority to bind and loose, to remit and retain, to preach, baptise, and such like, as well as he: Yet in these things and all other government, Christ would have him to be their head, and they to depend of him as head of their College, and consequently of the whole flock of Christ: no Apostle nor no Prince in earth (if he acknowledge himself to be a sheep of Christ) exempted from his charge. And that Christ maketh a difference between Peter and the rest, and giveth him some greater preeminence and regiment than the rest, ² it is plain by that he is asked whether he love our Lord more than the other Apostles also do. Where his special charge giveth no difference of love had been sufficient. To Peter (saith Cyprian) ³ our Lord after his Resurrection said, Feed my sheep, and builded his Church upon him alone, and to him he giveth the charge of feeding his sheep. For although after his Resurrection he gave his power alike to all, saying, As my Father sent me, so I send you, take the Holy Ghost, if you remit to any their sins, they shall be remitted &c. Yet to manifest unity, he constituted one Chair, & so disposed by his authority that unity should have origin of one. The rest of the Apostles were that Peter was, in equal fellowship of honour and power, but the beginning cometh of unity: the Primacy is given to Peter, that the Church of Christ may be shewed to be one Chair. S. Chrysostome also saith thus: ⁴ Why did our Lord shed his blood? truly to redeem those sheep, the care of which he committed both to Peter and also to his Successors. ⁵ And a little after, Christ would have Peter endued with such authority, and to be far above all his other Apostles: for he saith: Peter, dost thou love me more than all these do? Whereupon our Master might have inferred, If thou love me Peter, use much fasting, sleep on the hard flour, watch much, be patron to the oppressed, father to the orphans, and husband to the widows: but omitting all these things, he saith, Feed my sheep. For, all the foresaid virtues certainly be done easily of many subjects; but to bear the burden of the Principality, to undertake the government of the Church and committing the charge of so many souls, all woman kind must needs wholly give place to the burden and greatness thereof, and a great number of men also. So writeth he. \fq ¹ \ft Peter is here made the general Pastor, and the Church is builded upon him. \fq ² \ft The Protestants otherwise denying this preeminence of Peter, yet to uphold their Archbishops, do avouch & prove it against the Puritanes. \fq ³ \ft \+it Cypr. de unit. Ec. & li. 2. de Sacerd.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft First proof. \fq ⁵ \ft Peter's successors succeed him in universal authority.\ef*
\v 18 Amen, amen I say to thee, when thou wast younger, thou didst gird thyself, and didst walk where thou wouldest. But when thou shalt be old thou shalt stretch forth thy hands, and another shall gird thee, and lead thee whither thou wilt not. \ef - \fr 21.18 \fq Another shall gird thee. \ft He Prophesieth of Peter's Martyrdom, and of the kind of death which he should suffer, that was, crucifying.¹ Which ² the Heretics fearing that it were a step to prove he was martyred in Rome, deny: whereas the Fathers and ancient writers are as plain in this, as that he was at Rome. \+it Origen apud Euseb. li. 3. c. 1. Euseb. li. 2 c. 24. Hist. Ec. Tertul. de præscript. nu. 14. Aug. tract. 123 in Ioan. Chrysost. Bedæ in hunc locum.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft \+it Beza in hunc locum\+it* \fq ² \ft Peter crucified at Rome.\ef*
\v 19 And this he said, signifying by what death he should glorify God. \f 1 \fr 21.19 \ft b The Gospel upon S. John the Evangelist's day in Christmas\f* And when he had said this, he saith to him: Follow me.
\v 20 Peter turning, saw that Disciple whom \sc Jesus\sc* loved, following, \x - \xt Io. 13, 23.\x* who also leaned at the supper upon his breast, and said, Lord who is he that shall betray thee?
\v 21 Him therefore when Peter had seen, he saith to \sc Jesus\sc*: Lord, and this man what?
\v 22 \sc Jesus\sc* saith to him: \f 1 \fr 21.22 \ft c So readeth S. Ambrose \+it in Ps. 45. & ser. 20 in Ps. 118.\+it* S. Aug. \+it tract. 124 in Io.\+it* & most ancient copies and service books extant in Latin. Others read, \+it If I will:\+it* others, \+it If so I will,\+it* &c.\f* So I will have him to remain till I come, what to thee? follow thou me.
\v 23 This saying therefore went abroad among the brethren, that that Disciple dieth not. And \sc Jesus\sc* did not say to him, he dieth not; but, So I will have him to remain till I come, what to thee?
\v 24 This is that Disciple which giveth testimony of these things, and hath written these things: and we know that his testimony is true.
\v 25 But there are \x - \xt Io. 20, 30.\x* many \x - \xt * See li. 1. ep. 72. 75. li. 2. ep. 37. 45. li. 4. ep. 95. li. 7. ep. 63.\x* other things also which \sc Jesus\sc* did: which if they were written in particular, neither the world it-self I think were able to contain those books that should be written.
